window.CALTPA.rewrittenCases = {"ms-historical": {"id": "ms-historical", "path": "ms-math", "title": "Grade 3 opinion writing", "file": "01_MS_Cycle_1_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_CalTPAMSCycle1ExampleRevised.docx", "parts": [{"id": "A", "title": "Part A Contextual Information", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-1\"><p>Step 1: Plan Getting to Know Your Students Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-historical-2\"><p>Directions: Provide the information listed below about your whole class, your classroom context, and each of the three focus students by responding to the following prompts (up to 5 pages). To protect the privacy of the focus students, refer to these students as Focus Student 1 (FS1), Focus Student 2 (FS2), and Focus Student 3 (FS3). Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt. Do not delete or alter the prompts. Whole Class 1. Description of Students\u2019 Assets and Needs a. prior academic knowledge related to the specific content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-3\"><p>My third graders have just completed five-paragraph animal reports. They researched a topic and organized their findings with an opening sentence, supporting information, transitions, and a conclusion. That work gives them a foundation for our next writing unit. We will move from explaining a topic to taking a position and supporting it with reasons, as called for in CCSS.ELA-Literacy.W.3.1. I will continue to reinforce the paragraph skills they have already practiced.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-4\"><p>b. English language proficiency levels (Standard English learners and English learners)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-5\"><p>Two eight-year-olds are English learners: a Spanish-speaking boy and an Assyrian-speaking girl. Both received an overall ELPAC level of 3. Their oral-language results are stronger than their reading and writing results: speaking and listening were well developed, while literacy skills were somewhat or moderately developed. They participate across subjects with limited differentiation. In planning this lesson, I will provide the light language support described for their bridging level.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-6\"><p>c. cultural and linguistic resources and funds of knowledge (i.e., knowledge and skills derived from cultural experience)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-7\"><p>The class includes 23 students: five Asian, three Indian, six Hispanic, one African American, and eight Caucasian. I learned about them through conversations, classroom observations, cumulative records, and discussions with my Master Teacher, case managers, and resource teachers. The background information lists 19 students born in the United States and three born elsewhere\u2014Hong Kong, Assyria, and Mexico. Most have grown up in California near XX.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-8\"><p>Students bring experiences with sports, drama, robotics, and other school activities. They generally enjoy reading, technology, and collaborative work, although a few prefer quieter participation. Their individual Chromebooks are a strong source of interest in math, literacy, typing, and coding activities. The school serves families from both lower-income housing and a wealthy neighborhood in a high-cost area associated with technology. Nine students qualify for free or reduced-price meals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-9\"><p>Living near the suburban edge of XX gives students access to beaches, mountains, snow, and other nearby destinations. Families observe Christmas, Easter, Diwali, Rama Navami, Chinese New Year, and Baoutha d\u2019Ninawaye. Our classroom work recognizes these experiences and encourages students to respect differences. That sense of acceptance supports participation and helps children feel that they belong.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-10\"><p>d. prior experiences and interests related to the content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-11\"><p>Our recent science unit covered clouds, storms, weather, and natural disasters. Students were especially interested in observing weather outside. Reading Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs will connect that interest with our first work on opinion writing. They enjoy listening to stories and can draw on the organization they used in their animal reports when they explain how they feel about the weather in this book.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-12\"><p>2. Contextual Information for the Class a. grade level(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-13\"><p>Third grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-14\"><p>b. content area</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-15\"><p>English language arts, with a focus on opinion writing within text types and purposes.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-16\"><p>c. classroom setting (face-to-face, online, hybrid)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-17\"><p>In-person instruction.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-18\"><p>d. resources and materials you have access to in your classroom</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-19\"><p>Each student has a Chromebook. The classroom also has two Apple TVs, a document camera, headphones, a library, science and math manipulatives, and games and activities collected by the teacher.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-20\"><p>e. number of students: i. total number of students (whole class)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-21\"><p>23</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-22\"><p>ii. number of identified English learners</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-23\"><p>2</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-24\"><p>iii. number of Standard English learners</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-25\"><p>0</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-26\"><p>iv. number of students with an IEP</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-27\"><p>2</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-28\"><p>v. number of students with a 504 plan</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-29\"><p>1</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-30\"><p>vi. number of students identified for GATE</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-31\"><p>0</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-32\"><p>f. dual-language setting, if applicable; indicate whether setting is: bilingual</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-33\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-34\"><p>one-way immersion</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-35\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-36\"><p>two-way immersion</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-37\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-38\"><p>Focus Students 3. Description of 3 Focus Students\u2019 Assets and Needs IMPORTANT NOTE: To protect the privacy of the focus students, refer to these students throughout your submitted evidence as Focus Student 1 (FS1), Focus Student 2 (FS2), and Focus Student 3 (FS3). Focus Student 1 a. current proficiency in reading, writing, and speaking/listening in English and mastery of the primary language</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-39\"><p>FS1 came to the United States from an Assyrian background at age two. She has taken the ELPAC during each of her three years at this school. Last year&#x27;s overall score was 1481, placing her at level 3. Listening and speaking were at level 4, while reading and writing were at level 3. These results point to stronger oral English and continued growth in literacy.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-40\"><p>b. prior academic knowledge related to the specific content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-41\"><p>FS1&#x27;s weather-unit work shows that she understands the clouds, storms, and weather that appear in the read-aloud. Her animal report also shows experience organizing a paragraph and supporting its topic. I will help her apply those skills to an opinion. I do not yet know whether she recognizes the term opinion, so I will explain it in familiar language before asking her to use it.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-42\"><p>c. social identity (student self-concept derived from a perceived membership in a social group that is evident in classroom interactions)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-43\"><p>FS1 has two close classmates whom she sees on the playground and at playdates. She is conscientious about her work and consistently follows classroom routines. Her relationships and dependable participation give her a comfortable place in the class.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-44\"><p>d. cultural and linguistic resources and funds of knowledge</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-45\"><p>FS1 uses English and Assyrian at home and attends an Assyrian-language school. She spends time with friends from that school on weekends. She is an only child. Through the Meet the Student activity and an individual conversation, I learned that she enjoys clay, drawing, the library, music, and learning new things. Read-aloud time is a particular favorite.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-46\"><p>e. prior experiences and interests related to the content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-47\"><p>FS1 often chooses to draw or write in her journal during free time. Together with her interest in read-alouds, these habits give her several reasons to engage with an opinion paragraph and its illustration.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-48\"><p>f. developmental considerations (e.g., social-emotional, typical and atypical child/adolescent development)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-49\"><p>My observations suggest age-appropriate social, emotional, and developmental functioning for FS1.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-50\"><p>Focus Student 2 a. learning challenge (identified disability and IEP goals, focus of 504 plan or MTSS support, or need for greater instructional challenge through GATE)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-51\"><p>FS2 has had hearing loss in his left ear since birth and uses a hearing aid in his right ear. He needs a front seat that places his right ear toward the teacher. My Master Teacher and I use microphones so he can hear instruction clearly.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-52\"><p>b. prior academic knowledge related to the specific content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-53\"><p>FS2&#x27;s animal report included an organized paragraph structure, researched details, transitions, and a closing sentence. His weather work also shows familiarity with the language this story uses. Those experiences should help him enter the lesson. Because I am unsure whether he knows the term opinion, I will give him time during the read-aloud to form a view and think of reasons before writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-54\"><p>c. social identity (student self-concept derived from a perceived membership in a social group that is evident in classroom interactions)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-55\"><p>FS2 is close to three classmates and usually chooses one particular friend as a partner. He enjoys soccer with these friends and appears proud of his participation. These relationships seem to support a positive view of himself.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-56\"><p>d. cultural and linguistic resources and funds of knowledge</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-57\"><p>FS2 was born in Hong Kong and moved to XX before his first birthday. He speaks Chinese and English, has an older brother, and enjoys sports. Technology is another strong interest, especially building robots in the STEAM lab.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-58\"><p>e. prior experiences and interests related to the content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-59\"><p>Storms were one of FS2&#x27;s favorite parts of the weather unit, which should help draw him into the story. His completed animal report also gives him a strong starting point for organizing and supporting a written response.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-60\"><p>f. developmental considerations (e.g., social-emotional, typical and atypical child/adolescent development)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-61\"><p>FS2 appears to be developing in ways typical of children his age, including socially and emotionally.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-62\"><p>g. assistive technologies as appropriate</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-63\"><p>I will use the classroom microphone during instruction to complement FS2&#x27;s right-ear hearing aid and support his access to the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-64\"><p>Focus Student 3 a. life experience(s) either inside or outside of school that may result in a need for additional academic and/or emotional support</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-65\"><p>FS3 has missed school frequently, and my Master Teacher told me that his father died last year. He often has difficulty sustaining attention, remaining still, and managing impulses. His mother has raised the possibility of ADHD but has not pursued a diagnosis; he has neither an IEP nor a 504 plan.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-66\"><p>He needs frequent check-ins and redirection to finish activities without interrupting classmates. Since beginning tutoring, he has started completing and submitting homework. His records show relative strength in mathematics and continuing needs in reading and language arts. I will use that information to plan support without assuming a diagnosis.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-67\"><p>b. prior academic knowledge related to the specific content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-68\"><p>FS3 participated in the weather unit and completed an animal report. His science work suggests that he can follow the weather content in the story. His report needed stronger transitions and a conclusion, so I will pay particular attention to those elements as we develop opinion writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-69\"><p>c. social identity (student self-concept derived from a perceived membership in a social group that is evident in classroom interactions)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-70\"><p>FS3 is cheerful and often has a joke, story, or action to share. His impulsivity sometimes strains friendships. He especially enjoys one classmate&#x27;s company, although that peer can become frustrated when FS3 has trouble settling or focusing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-71\"><p>d. cultural and linguistic resources and funds of knowledge</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-72\"><p>FS3 is an only child, is Caucasian, and speaks English as his first language. He was born in Hawaii and moved to XX two years ago. Conversation, drawing, comics, and finding books in the school library are important interests and ways he expresses himself.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-73\"><p>e. prior experiences and interests related to the content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-74\"><p>The opportunity to talk about an opinion and then write and draw fits FS3&#x27;s interest in conversation and journal work. I expect those choices to help him engage with the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-75\"><p>f. developmental considerations (e.g., social-emotional, typical and atypical child/adolescent development)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-76\"><p>FS3 appears to be developing appropriately in some emotional areas, but he needs more support than many classmates with attention, movement, and work completion. Calling out or making inappropriate comments can interfere with peer relationships. I will keep those observed needs in mind when organizing participation and independent work.</p></div>"}, {"id": "B", "title": "Part B Lesson Planning", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-77\"><p>.1 Step 1: Plan Optional Lesson Plan Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-historical-78\"><p>Directions: Use the optional lesson plan template to establish content-specific learning goal(s) and develop one lesson plan. Lesson Overview Title of Lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-79\"><p>Opinion Writing with Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-80\"><p>Subject</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-81\"><p>English language arts.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-82\"><p>Grade Level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-83\"><p>Third grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-84\"><p>Time Frame</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-85\"><p>60 minutes.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-86\"><p>Content Standard(s) CA ELA Standards - Text Types and Purposes 1. Write opinion pieces on topics or texts, supporting a point of view with reasons. CA ELA Standards \u2013 Comprehension and Collaboration 1.b Follow agreed-upon rules for discussions (e.g., gaining the floor in respectful ways, listening to others with care, speaking one at a time about the topics and texts under discussion). CA ELA Standards \u2013 Comprehension and Collaboration 1.d \u2013 Explain their own ideas and understanding in light of the discussion. ELD Standard(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-87\"><p>ELD Standards for Grade 3: A. Collaborative 1. Exchanging information and ideas with others through oral collaborative discussions on a range of social and academic topics. B. Interpretive 5. Listening actively to spoken English in a range of social and academic contexts C. Productive 11. Supporting own opinions and evaluating other\u2019s opinions in speaking and writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-88\"><p>Learning Goal(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-89\"><p>Students will listen to Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs and follow our discussion routines. At three pauses, they will consider life in Chewandswallow, decide how they would feel about its weather, and explain their view to a partner. They will then write three to five sentences giving their opinion and at least one supporting reason. Their illustration will show a day when a food of their choice falls from the sky.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-90\"><p>Content of Lesson What do you expect students to deeply understand about the lesson? What do you expect students to retain after the lesson and use in future learning?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-91\"><p>I want students to understand what an opinion is and recognize that classmates can reasonably think differently. They should be able to carry that understanding into discussions and writing about other topics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-92\"><p>What misunderstandings or misconceptions do you expect students might have from the lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-93\"><p>Some students may already understand that people have different ideas without knowing the word opinion. I will connect that new term to familiar examples so the label does not prevent them from understanding the concept.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-94\"><p>What knowledge and skills do you expect students to have after engaging in the lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-95\"><p>By the end of the lesson, students should be able to state a view and give a reason for it in a short piece of writing. With support, they should begin using the same approach when responding to another topic.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-96\"><p>Assessment/Checking for Understanding What essential questions will you ask to determine if students are not meeting, meeting, or exceeding the learning goal(s) of the lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-97\"><p>I will first find out what students know about opinion. Then I will ask whether people can have different opinions about the same thing. Their explanations will show me where I need to clarify the concept before they begin writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-98\"><p>What will students do to demonstrate achievement of content during the lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-99\"><p>Students will show their understanding in partner conversations, whole-class sharing, and an individual paragraph. I will help them move from stating a preference to explaining why they hold it, then look for both elements in the writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-100\"><p>How will you know students understand the content?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-101\"><p>I will listen during the read-aloud and partner talk, ask follow-up questions, and circulate as students write. Those checks will help me respond while learning is taking place. Afterward, I will review the completed paragraphs for the stated opinion, supporting reason, and other lesson expectations, sorting the work into below, meeting, or exceeding expectations.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-102\"><p>Structured Student Learning Activities What activities will the students be involved in during the lesson to support their achievement of the learning goal(s)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-103\"><p>Students will gather on the carpet for the story. At three planned points, they will think about living in Chewandswallow, find a partner, and discuss their opinions. We will hear several ideas as a class before returning to the reading. This oral rehearsal will prepare students to write independently on the handout. Once the writing is complete, we will return to the carpet to share and consider how opinions apply to other subjects.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-104\"><p>How will you group students and manage group work to support student learning?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-105\"><p>I will use my Master Teacher&#x27;s carpet arrangement, which reflects how students work together and puts the focus students near instruction. During partner talk, I will check that everyone has someone to talk with and a chance to contribute. For writing, students may choose flexible seating. I will monitor those choices and separate combinations that make concentration difficult.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-106\"><p>Instruction to Support Learning What instructional strategies will support student learning through multiple modalities?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-107\"><p>The read-aloud and its illustrations will let students hear and see the story. I will also display a model paragraph and sentence openings on the classroom TVs. Discussion will give students a chance to rehearse language, while changing partners will build movement into the lesson. Drawing will provide another way to represent an idea. Flexible seating will help students find workable writing spaces. Across these activities, I will move from explicit instruction and shared practice toward independent writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-108\"><p>What resources, materials, and/or educational technology will you or your students use during the lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-109\"><p>I will use Judi Barrett&#x27;s Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs, my opinion-writing handout, a model paragraph, and displayed sentence starters. The handout begins with a prompt about living in Chewandswallow and includes a space to illustrate a chosen food falling from the sky. The Apple TVs will display the writing supports, and my microphone necklace will make the spoken instruction easier to hear.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-110\"><p>What adaptations and accommodations, including, as appropriate, assistive technologies, will support individual student learning needs beyond the UDL supports built into the lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-111\"><p>I will check seating choices, confer with the three focus students, and provide feedback tied to the writing criteria. The model and sentence starters will be available to everyone, with particular support for students who need help beginning. If time and FS3&#x27;s progress require an adjustment, he may explain his opinion and reasons orally. A fidget or privacy screen will also be available to support his concentration.</p></div>"}, {"id": "C", "title": "Part C Planning Narrative", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-112\"><p>Step 1: Plan Lesson Plan Rationale Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-historical-113\"><p>Directions: Thinking about the class context information and students\u2019 assets and needs for the whole class and focus students, briefly respond to the following prompts (up to 5 pages). Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt. Do not delete or alter the prompts. 1. Prior Academic Knowledge: Explain how the lesson plan builds on students\u2019 prior academic knowledge related to the content-specific learning goal(s) selected for the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-114\"><p>The month-long informational-writing unit required students to introduce an animal, group related information, provide facts, connect ideas, and conclude. Their reports and my Master Teacher&#x27;s grading show that 86% met the standard. The remaining students are continuing to revise with small-group support. Opinion writing will draw on the same organizational skills while changing the purpose: students will now state a position and support it with reasons.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-115\"><p>2. Student Assets and Needs: Explain how the lesson plan incorporates or builds on students\u2019 cultural and linguistic resources, socioeconomic backgrounds, funds of knowledge, prior experiences, and interests related to the content of the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-116\"><p>Students&#x27; own experiences with weather and their enthusiasm during science give them a familiar entry into the book&#x27;s unusual storms. Its accessible language will support the class during a read-aloud. I also considered the appeal of imagining freely available food in a class that includes students receiving meal assistance. That is an instructional possibility I anticipated, rather than something I have established about individual children&#x27;s experiences.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-117\"><p>3. Student Learning Activities: Explain why you selected the learning activities and how you will engage all students in higher-order thinking (analysis, synthesis, evaluation, interpretation, transfer) and applications of concepts or skills to purposefully advance their understanding of the specific content (e.g., use of manipulatives, think-pair-share, models, drawings or maps, graphic organizers, performances, demonstrations, labs).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-118\"><p>Partner talk will give students repeated chances to form, explain, and reconsider an opinion. As they decide whether they would want to live in Chewandswallow, they must interpret events in the story and use those events to support a view. Hearing classmates&#x27; ideas will let us compare perspectives and clarify misunderstandings. In the closing discussion, I will ask students to apply the idea of opinion to a new topic, extending the learning beyond the book.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-119\"><p>4. Instructional Strategies: Explain why you will use specific instructional strategies and what adaptations you might make to improve student access to learning. Describe how you will support student engagement with the content you are teaching in this lesson (e.g., modeling, scaffolding, asking questions, providing instructions to guide an activity).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-120\"><p>I will introduce the task to the whole class and model an opinion before students develop their own. Partner and class discussion will provide oral practice, especially useful for English learners before writing. Questions and thumbs-up or thumbs-down checks will help me decide when to clarify or add a scaffold. While students write, I will circulate, confer with the focus students, and adjust support to what their work shows.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-121\"><p>5. Student Grouping: Explain your rationale for grouping students in this lesson\u2014whole group, small group, pairs, individual\u2014and why you think this will support student learning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-122\"><p>The grouping follows the move toward independence. Whole-class instruction will establish a shared understanding of the new task. Pair discussion and class reflection will let students try out their thinking with support. Individual writing will then show what each student can communicate after that preparation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-123\"><p>6. Academic Language Development: How will you address the academic language development needs of the students you are teaching, including English learners and Standard English learners? What vocabulary or terminology is necessary to access the content?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-124\"><p>I will introduce opinion by first asking what students already know, hearing their explanations, and offering a clear definition. They will practice the term through choral responses and use it while sharing their own ideas. This gives English learners repeated opportunities to connect the word to a meaningful action. At the close, we will revisit the definition and consider opinions about other topics, such as sports, so students can use the term beyond this story.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-125\"><p>7. Resources and Materials to Support Learning: Explain why you chose particular resources and materials to support student learning and language demands in this lesson. What is the difficulty level of text, materials, or resources needed for the lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-126\"><p>Cloudy with a Chance of Meatballs builds on a topic students enjoyed and is appropriate for a third-grade read-aloud. The handout&#x27;s opening phrase gives them a clear starting point for writing about Chewandswallow. Lined space on half the page keeps the task manageable, and the illustration space gives them another way to represent the idea. The displayed example and sentence starters will make the writing expectations visible.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-127\"><p>8. Assessments: Explain how the assessments will check on students\u2019 understanding of the content taught during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-128\"><p>The initial thumbs check will tell me how much explanation the word opinion needs. Listening to partner conversations and class sharing will show whether students can state a view and support it. The completed paragraphs will provide individual evidence. Sorting them by the lesson criteria will help me distinguish students ready to move on from those who need another explanation or targeted practice.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-129\"><p>9. Developmental considerations (e.g., social-emotional, typical and atypical child/adolescent development): Explain how the lesson plan addresses the developmental considerations of your students.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-130\"><p>The lesson gives students practice recognizing another person&#x27;s viewpoint while using their interest in peers. I will help quieter children find partners and make sure they have a chance to speak. Moving to a partner provides breaks from sitting. Flexible seating during writing offers further support, especially for FS3, who likes the library floor or a beanbag with a writing tray.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-131\"><p>10. Focus Students: Explain how the lesson plan addresses individual needs of the 3 focus students, including as appropriate, assistive technologies, and provides inclusive learning opportunities (if relevant, may include an explanation of additional support that occurs outside the classroom) to engage fully with the content of the lesson. a. Focus Student 1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-132\"><p>FS1&#x27;s stronger oral-language results suggest that discussion can prepare her for the harder writing task. I will provide the handout&#x27;s opening phrase, a paragraph model, and sentence frames that include transitions. I will read the supports aloud before displaying them. Individual check-ins and feedback tied to the criteria will help her see what is working and what still needs attention. I have shared the plan and materials with her resource teacher and will report how she performs after the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-133\"><p>b. Focus Student 2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-134\"><p>FS2 will sit near me with a clear view of the illustrations and my face. I will wear the microphone necklace to support his hearing. Listening during partner talk and checking his writing will help me assess his understanding directly. Any feedback or additional guidance will address the opinion-writing goals while maintaining access to the spoken lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-135\"><p>c. Focus Student 3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-136\"><p>FS3 will sit close enough for quiet redirection. Because sitting for more than about ten minutes is difficult for him, the three partner activities will include movement. He may also choose a suitable writing space, including the beanbag or library area. I will check his progress, support transitions and a closing sentence, and give specific feedback. If distractions interfere, I will offer a privacy folder and adjust his workspace.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-137\"><p>Retained Source Materials</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-138\"><p>The following source pages retain the instructional materials, assessment tools, and student work as supplied.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-historical-image1.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 3 opinion writing: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-historical-image1.png\" alt=\"Grade 3 opinion writing: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-historical-image2.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 3 opinion writing: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-historical-image2.png\" alt=\"Grade 3 opinion writing: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-139\"><p>Video Annotations</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-140\"><p>Clip 1-Opening the Lesson   0:00:12\u20130:00:22</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-141\"><p>Annotation Title: Setting Expectations for Learning</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-142\"><p>Annotation Text: I remind a student to check his seating position. This establishes the carpet routine: crossed legs, an appropriate sitting position, and attention on me.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-143\"><p>Clip 1-Opening the Lesson   0:00:25\u20130:00:34</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-144\"><p>Annotation Title: Creating a Positive Learning Environment; Setting Expectations for Learning</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-145\"><p>Annotation Text: I connect the new writing task with the animal reports students have already completed. Beginning with familiar work helps them understand the lesson&#x27;s expectations and gives them an established experience to draw on.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-146\"><p>Clip 1-Opening the Lesson   0:00:48\u20130:01:00</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-147\"><p>Annotation Title: Monitoring for Student Understanding</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-148\"><p>Annotation Text: I ask students to signal with a thumb if they know what an opinion is. Most indicate that they do, following our earlier read-aloud discussion. Their responses lead me to give a brief review of the term before explaining the writing task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-149\"><p>Clip 1-Opening the Lesson   0:02:21\u20130:02:31</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-150\"><p>Annotation Title: Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-151\"><p>Annotation Text: After identifying my own opinion, I ask whether students may disagree with it. They must consider the difference between their judgment and mine and recognize that another opinion is possible.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-152\"><p>Clip 2-Middle of the Lesson   0:00:02\u20130:00:12</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-153\"><p>Annotation Title: Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-154\"><p>Annotation Text: I give students time to decide whether they would want to live in Chewandswallow before speaking to a partner. They use information from the book to evaluate the town and form a view they can explain aloud.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-155\"><p>Clip 2-Middle of the Lesson   0:00:38\u20130:00:48</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-156\"><p>Annotation Title: Setting Expectations for Learning; Creating a Positive Learning Environment; Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-157\"><p>Annotation Text: When I notice a student is not participating, I direct him to the classmate beside him. This gives him an immediate partner and a clear way to join the discussion about living in Chewandswallow.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-158\"><p>Clip 2-Middle of the Lesson   0:00:56\u20130:01:50</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-159\"><p>Annotation Title: Monitoring for Student Understanding; Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-160\"><p>Annotation Text: I ask students to report ideas from their partner conversations. Their explanations let me check understanding, acknowledge their views, and help them clarify what they mean.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-161\"><p>Clip 2-Middle of the Lesson   0:01:27\u20130:01:45</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-162\"><p>Annotation Title: Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-163\"><p>Annotation Text: I invite students with another viewpoint to contribute. Hearing different responses helps the class recognize that people can form distinct opinions about the same situation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-164\"><p>Clip 3-Closing the Lesson   0:01:28\u20130:01:38</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-165\"><p>Annotation Title: Creating a Positive Learning Environment; Setting Expectations for Learning</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-166\"><p>Annotation Text: I use a paced countdown to signal the move to the carpet. Pausing between numbers gives students time to make the transition and settle into the meeting area.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-167\"><p>Clip 3-Closing the Lesson   0:02:42\u20130:03:05</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-168\"><p>Annotation Title: Monitoring for Student Understanding; Creating a Positive Learning Environment</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-169\"><p>Annotation Text: I revisit the learning goal and ask students to recall the name of the writing we practiced. Having the class say opinion together reinforces the term and checks their connection to the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-170\"><p>Clip 3-Closing the Lesson   0:03:56\u20130:04:26</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-171\"><p>Annotation Title: Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-172\"><p>Annotation Text: I ask students to consider other subjects they could have opinions about. This extends the concept beyond the book and asks them to apply it in another setting.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-173\"><p>Clip 3-Closing the Lesson   0:04:08\u20130:04:26</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-174\"><p>Annotation Title: Engaging Students in Content-Specific Higher-Order Thinking; Creating a Positive Learning Environment</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-175\"><p>Annotation Text: Volunteers suggest further topics. I restate their ideas so everyone can hear and ask them to clarify the particular opinion they could express. This acknowledges their contributions while helping them make their thinking more precise.</p></div>"}, {"id": "G", "title": "Part G Reflection", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-176\"><p>Step 3: Reflect Reflection on What You Learned Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-historical-177\"><p>Directions: Respond to the following prompts (up to 3 pages), citing evidence from your submission from Step 1 and/or Step 2 (e.g., narrative, lesson plan, lesson plan rationale, video clips, annotations) for each response. Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt. Do not delete or alter the prompts. 1. How did learning deeply about your students\u2019 assets and needs a. inform/shape your lesson plan for the whole class?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-178\"><p>The information in Step 1 helped me connect new learning to work students had already done. Their animal reports provided a foundation for paragraph organization, while the weather unit and their enjoyment of read-alouds guided my choice of book. Their interest in working with peers led me to include repeated think-pair-share opportunities. Together, those decisions made the move into opinion writing familiar and manageable.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-179\"><p>b. support student access to and engagement with the content?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-180\"><p>The gradual move from listening and discussion to independent writing gave students time to work with the new concept before recording it. Reading aloud made the story available to students with different reading abilities. Partner exchanges let them rehearse ideas, and the displayed model and sentence starters supported the language demands of writing. These choices followed the needs and interests described in Step 1.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-181\"><p>2. Was your lesson plan sufficiently flexible to support all students\u2019 learning, or did you need to incorporate specific instructional, in-the-moment adaptations for particular students?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-182\"><p>The plan gave me enough room to respond during teaching. Partner conversations and class responses showed where an idea needed clarification. Circulating during writing let me ask guiding questions, scaffold a response, or adjust a student&#x27;s working conditions. The closing discussion also gave me time to address points that arose in individual conferences. I could make these changes while keeping the lesson focused on its original goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-183\"><p>3. How did your instructional approach support learning for each of the 3 focus students to achieve the content-specific learning goal(s)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-184\"><p>FS1 used her stronger speaking and listening skills to develop an opinion before writing. I read the model and sentence starters aloud, displayed them, and checked her progress. Her finished paragraph used three of those starters, showing how the language support helped her complete the task.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-185\"><p>FS2&#x27;s seating and the microphone supported access to the story and directions. Listening to his partner discussion helped me check his understanding. Those planned accommodations allowed him to participate in the same opinion-writing work as his classmates.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-186\"><p>FS3 needed additional support during instruction and independent work. Near the end of the directions, he was lying on his back. Later, when his table became distracting, I moved him to a less crowded space. His paper was still blank at my next check-in, so I helped him explain his opinion orally and use the displayed sentence starters to begin writing. The planned supports and those immediate adjustments helped him finish the opinion task.</p></div>"}, {"id": "H", "title": "Part H Next Steps", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-187\"><p>Step 4: Apply Application of What You Learned Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-historical-188\"><p>Directions: Respond to the following prompts (up to 3 pages), citing evidence from your submission from Step 1, Step 2, and/or Step 3 (e.g., narrative, lesson plan, lesson plan rationale, video clips, annotations) for each response. Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt. Do not delete or alter the prompts. 1. Explain what you would do next to advance the learning of this group of students. a. Do you need to reteach any part of the lesson? Explain why.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-189\"><p>The completed work showed 15 students meeting expectations, six exceeding them, and two below them. Thus, 21 of the 23 demonstrated the lesson goals. I would move the class forward while meeting individually with the two students whose paragraphs stated an opinion without a reason. I would ask each to explain the thinking orally, clarify as needed, and add a supporting detail to the paragraph.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-190\"><p>b. Based on what they learned about the content you were teaching, what will you teach next?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-191\"><p>Students are ready to build a more fully developed opinion piece under W.3.1. That means introducing the topic and position, organizing reasons, connecting them with linking language, and providing a conclusion. I would use Writer&#x27;s Workshop because students respond well to collaboration. Short lessons, purposeful grouping, peer review, revision, and teacher conferences would address one part at a time. An early workshop could focus on the introduction and opinion; the next could develop reasons and connections. Students would gradually assemble a piece that meets the full standard.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-historical-192\"><p>2. If you were to develop and teach this lesson again, what would you do the same or differently to improve learning of content and academic language for this group of students and why?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-193\"><p>I would keep the read-aloud and partner discussions. Reviewing the recording showed me several changes that would improve access and clarity. I would project the detailed illustrations so students at the back could see them, and add a word bank for story and weather vocabulary that students misspelled.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-historical-194\"><p>I would also state and display the three-to-five-sentence expectation. I said several sentences during teaching, and most students wrote only two: an opinion and a reason. Having students repeat the requirement and highlighting it beside the model would make the task clearer. Finally, I would build an opinion anchor chart with the definition and examples students contribute, giving them a visual reference as well as oral practice.</p></div>"}], "images": ["assets/examples/ms-historical-image1.png", "assets/examples/ms-historical-image2.png"], "responseCount": 72, "words": 5639, "historical": true}, "mmsn-literacy": {"id": "mmsn-literacy", "path": "mmsn-literacy", "title": "Grades 4\u20135 word study", "file": "02_MMSN_Literacy_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 MMSN LPA Sample Submission.docx", "parts": [{"id": "A", "title": "Part A Contextual Information", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-1\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 1: Plan\u2014Part A: Written Narrative: Contextual Information Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-2\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 4 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 8\u201311 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-3\"><p>Contextual Information About the Group of Students Age range(s) and grade level(s):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-4\"><p>I work with a fourth- and fifth-grade Special Day Class. Students are approximately nine to eleven years old.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-5\"><p>Student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to the literacy content you plan to teach:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-6\"><p>Students enjoy sharing family traditions and community experiences. Food, celebrations, nature, popular culture, and practical problems often make literacy activities more meaningful to them. Many are developing bilingual skills and draw on home-language vocabulary, cognates, visuals, and context. Sentence frames and organizers help them turn those resources into oral and written responses. I will connect the word study to these familiar experiences and interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-7\"><p>Literacy knowledge, skills, and/or abilities:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-8\"><p>Students&#x27; literacy skills vary. With support, several decode single- and multisyllabic words, identify important ideas in read-alouds, and use visuals or context to understand informational text. Oral discussion and shared writing are strengths. Many can write a short, organized paragraph with a frame or organizer. Continuing needs include fluency, vocabulary, main ideas, summaries, textual evidence, and applying word analysis independently. Some also need support beginning a task and sustaining attention during reading and writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-9\"><p>Identified English learners (see SB 210 for Deaf and Hard of Hearing students):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-10\"><p>The English learners are working at Emerging through Expanding levels. Vocabulary previews, visuals, sentence frames, and structured oral practice support them. Most of the class is fluent English proficient, but the integrated language work will also strengthen these students&#x27; academic vocabulary and comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-11\"><p>Identified learning needs (e.g., special education eligibility of students):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-12\"><p>Students receive services under classifications including SLD, Autism Spectrum Disorder, Other Health Impairment associated with ADHD, and Emotional Disturbance. Some also have language impairments affecting expression and comprehension. Explicit models, predictable routines, visual supports, and scaffolded practice are needed for access to grade-level reading, particularly multisyllabic words and academic vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-13\"><p>Instructional delivery model (e.g., co-teaching, inclusion, self-contained classrooms, small group, state special schools, itinerant):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-14\"><p>I teach in a self-contained fourth- and fifth-grade Special Day Class. Small-group instruction includes targeted literacy intervention, differentiated tasks, and individual supports connected to students&#x27; IEP goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-15\"><p>Instructional support personnel who will assist you in this lesson (e.g., paraprofessionals, interpreters, bilingual instructional assistants, Designated Instructional Services [DIS] providers, speech and language pathologists):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-16\"><p>Classroom paraprofessionals will assist with small groups, provide planned prompts, and monitor engagement and behavior. Behavioral support staff will help as needed with regulation strategies and other interventions. Their support will be coordinated with the literacy goals and individual IEP needs.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-17\"><p>Language(s) of instruction to be used in the learning segment (e.g., biliteracy, developmental bilingual [one-way immersion], dual language [two-way immersion], English only):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-18\"><p>Reading, writing, and discussion will take place in English. Visuals, sentence frames, and scaffolded instruction will support access to the grade-level literacy work and the integrated language goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-19\"><p>Contextual Information About the Focus Student (FS) Age and grade level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-20\"><p>FS is a ten-year-old fifth grader.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-21\"><p>Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to literacy and language:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-22\"><p>FS enjoys science, animals, adventure stories, and texts reflecting different cultures. He sometimes connects discussion to family traditions. He expresses ideas in English and is curious about vocabulary and word meanings, especially when prompted to use academic language. Discussion and meaningful texts will let him connect those strengths with the word-analysis work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-23\"><p>If your FS is a district-/school-identified English learner, Fluent English Proficient (reclassified English learner), Heritage language speaker, and/or multilingual, describe the FS&#x27;s literacy knowledge and skills in their primary language(s):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-24\"><p>English is the language used for FS&#x27;s literacy instruction and assessment. He has no English-learner, heritage-language, or multilingual identification. He receives support to access grade-level material in English.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-25\"><p>Literacy knowledge, skills, and abilities:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-26\"><p>FS reads grade-level material with some fluency and can identify main ideas, summarize short passages, and answer literal and inferential questions. He decodes many multisyllabic words and is beginning to use context and word parts to clarify meaning. He writes three-to-five-sentence paragraphs with a topic and some details but needs help developing the support and including a conclusion. Oral language and curiosity about words are strengths. Organizers and frames help him apply those strengths to more complete writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-27\"><p>Special education eligibility and the impact on the FS&#x27;s literacy knowledge, skills, and/or abilities:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-28\"><p>FS receives services for Specific Learning Disability and is also classified as ED. His needs affect reading, writing, language processing, independent word analysis, and paragraph organization. Individualized instruction and accommodations help him access grade-level content while developing these skills.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-29\"><p>If your FS is an English learner, learning needs related to their English language development:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-30\"><p>This does not apply because FS is not an English learner. His literacy support is provided in English.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-31\"><p>IEP goal(s) related to the foundational reading skills, additional themes, and/or ELD that will be addressed in the lesson:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-32\"><p>By 02/17/26, FS is expected to determine unfamiliar word meanings in reading and writing by applying grade-level prefixes, roots, and suffixes with 80% accuracy in three of four trials. Student work and teacher records will provide the evidence for that goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-33\"><p>Additional supports reflected in the FS&#x27;s IEP that may support their progress toward the ELA/Literacy and ELD learning goals (e.g., behavior plan, current services, assistive technology, and/or augmentative and alternative communication [AAC]):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-34\"><p>FS&#x27;s behavior plan includes planned movement breaks, reinforcement for staying with a task, and a quiet space when he becomes frustrated. He receives specialized literacy instruction and small-group paraprofessional support. Text-to-speech, digital organizers, and word-prediction tools help with comprehension, vocabulary, and paragraph writing. These supports are intended to increase access and independence; he does not require AAC.</p></div>"}, {"id": "B", "title": "Part B Lesson Planning", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-35\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 1: Plan\u2014Part B: Learning Segment Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-36\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt. Do not delete or alter the prompts. You may provide up to 6 pages per lesson. For more information, see pages 11\u201316 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. Across the 3\u20135 literacy lessons: Scope and Sequence Explain how your 3\u20135 lessons fit into the larger scope and sequence of the unit (systematic instruction).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-37\"><p>The three lessons form a word-structure sequence within our larger literacy unit. We begin with prefixes, move to the meaning carried by roots, and then examine suffixes. Each lesson uses explicit models, guided practice, and checks before independent application. Students will combine these parts to read unfamiliar words, understand vocabulary, and use it in writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-38\"><p>Summarize how your 3\u20135 lessons apply findings from previous literacy assessments. 1</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-39\"><p>Observations and screenings identified needs in multisyllabic decoding and comprehension. Classroom assessments and state results also showed difficulty with vocabulary and word analysis. I will respond with explicit morphology instruction, word banks, frames, and differentiated practice. Information from families and other educators will help me choose relevant vocabulary and texts that sustain students&#x27; interest.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-40\"><p>Identify the selected foundational reading skill(s) 2 students will learn and/or apply within a lesson and/or across your lesson activities.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-41\"><p>The selected skill is morphological awareness. Students will identify and work with prefixes, roots, and suffixes to explain unfamiliar words and apply that understanding in reading and writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-42\"><p>If you are providing supplemental support for students for the selected foundational reading skill(s) , explain why these skills are needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-43\"><p>The group will use word cards, anchor charts, graphic organizers, sentence frames, and guided think-alouds. Oral reading, partner practice, and independent application will provide repetition across settings. FS needs additional modeling, processing support, immediate feedback, and chances to correct his work because independent decoding and use of word structure remain difficult. These supports will help him move toward more confident application.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-44\"><p>Identify the selected additional theme(s) 3 from the ELA/ELD Framework you plan to teach within a lesson and/or across your lesson activities.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-45\"><p>Meaning making will come from explaining how the parts contribute to a word&#x27;s meaning and checking that meaning in context. Effective expression will come from using the new words accurately in speech and writing. Language development and content connections will be supported through vocabulary discussion and relevant examples.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-46\"><p>Explain how your lessons align with one another to create a progression of learning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-47\"><p>The progression begins with students&#x27; existing phonics and decoding knowledge. Prefix work introduces how one part changes a familiar word; root work makes the central meaning more explicit; suffix work then extends analysis to the ending and grammatical role. I will move from modeling to guided and independent practice, using checks at each stage. Discussion and sentence writing will help students explain and apply the analysis.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-48\"><p>Lesson 1 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-49\"><p>Fourth and fifth grades.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-50\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-51\"><p>How Prefixes Change Word Meaning</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-52\"><p>Strands/Standards and Learning Goals Grade-level CA ELA/Literacy strand(s)/standard(s) 4</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-53\"><p>Reading \u2013 Foundational Skills (Word Analysis) CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.4.3 / RF.5.3 \u25cf Know and apply grade-level phonics and word analysis skills in decoding words. Alignment: Students apply prefix knowledge when reading unfamiliar words and working out their meanings. Reading \u2013 Vocabulary Acquisition and Use CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.4 / L.5.4 \u25cf Determine or clarify the meaning of unknown and multiple-meaning words and phrases based on grade-level reading and content, choosing flexibly from a range of strategies. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.4.b / L.5.4.b \u25cf Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word. Alignment: Students examine the effect of a prefix on the base word. Language \u2013 Vocabulary Development CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.5 / L.5.5 \u25cf Demonstrate understanding of figurative language, word relationships, and nuances in word meanings. Alignment: Students describe a prefix\u2019s contribution and demonstrate the resulting meaning in a sentence. Speaking and Listening CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.SL.4.1 / SL.5.1 \u25cf Engage effectively in a range of collaborative discussions with diverse partners, building on others\u2019 ideas and expressing their own clearly. Alignment: Sentence frames support explanations of prefixes during partner and class conversation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-54\"><p>If the students are in an ATP , you should use the CA CCSS ELA/Literacy Standards.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-55\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-56\"><p>If the students are eligible for the California Alternate Assessments (CAA) for ELA/Literacy , you may use the Core Content Connectors (CCCs) aligned with the grade level of the students in order to plan your lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-57\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-58\"><p>If you are in a bilingual classroom where the language of instruction is Spanish and you plan on providing instruction in Spanish for the Literacy Cycle, you may provide the grade-level California CCSS en Espa\u00f1ol (ELA/Literacy Standard[s]).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-59\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-60\"><p>ELA/Literacy learning goal(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-61\"><p>Students will analyze prefixes, roots, and suffixes to explain words and determine the meaning of unfamiliar grade-level vocabulary in reading and writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-62\"><p>If you are in a bilingual classroom where the language of instruction is Spanish and you plan on providing instruction in Spanish for the Literacy Cycle, you may provide developmentally appropriate ELA/Literacy learning goal(s) based on the selected California CCSS en Espa\u00f1ol (ELA/Literacy Standard[s]).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-63\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-64\"><p>Grade-level CA ELD strand(s)/standard(s) 5</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-65\"><p>California ELD Standards, Grade 5 \u2014 Part I: Interacting in Meaningful Ways A. Collaborative, 1. Exchanging information and ideas with others through oral collaborative discussions on a range of social and academic topics. California ELD Standards, Grade 5 \u2014 Part II: Learning About How English Works B. Expanding, 4. Using knowledge of morphology (e.g., roots, affixes) to determine the meaning of words and to support comprehension of texts.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-66\"><p>Strands/Standards and Learning Goals ELD learning goal(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-67\"><p>Students will explain unfamiliar academic words through discussion and writing, using their knowledge of prefixes, roots, and suffixes.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-68\"><p>If you are in a bilingual classroom where the language of instruction is Spanish and you plan on providing instruction in Spanish for the Literacy Cycle, you may provide developmentally appropriate SLD learning goal(s) based on the selected CA SLD Standard(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-69\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-70\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-71\"><p>I will choose vocabulary from school routines, games, technology, and social situations. Partner matching will draw on students&#x27; enjoyment of learning together. For FS, familiar interests and classroom responsibilities will help make the word analysis relevant and encourage participation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-72\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-73\"><p>I will introduce un-, re-, pre-, and mis- and model how to separate a word and explain the prefix&#x27;s contribution. Partners will match prefix and root cards, record the new words on whiteboards, and explain their meanings orally and in writing. I will circulate, prompt with sentence frames, and respond immediately to errors while recording observations.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-74\"><p>Describe how you plan to use a direct and explicit approach for teaching the selected foundational reading skill(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-75\"><p>I will explicitly define a prefix, name the selected examples, and demonstrate how each changes a root&#x27;s meaning. Think-alouds will make the reasoning visible. Students will move from guided word building to applying the process themselves, with feedback at each stage.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-76\"><p>Summarize formative assessment(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-77\"><p>The whiteboard check will require students to combine a prefix and root, record the word, and explain it with a frame. The construction shows morphological understanding; the explanation shows how students use academic language to communicate meaning. I will use both to plan the next prompt or example.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-78\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-79\"><p>Students will listen to models, discuss with partners, read unfamiliar words in a short passage, and write words and meanings. Sentence frames will support complete explanations. These collaborative, interpretive, and productive opportunities make language practice part of the morphology task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-80\"><p>Explain how you plan to collaborate with and/or facilitate instructional support personnel.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-81\"><p>Before teaching, I will review IEP goals, language demands, and behavioral supports with paraprofessionals and service providers. During the lesson, paraprofessionals will reinforce frames, support partner work, and prompt regulation while encouraging independence. Afterward, I will discuss oral explanations with the speech-language pathologist to identify any need for further expressive-language practice.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-82\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student (FS), based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-83\"><p>FS will work with familiar, high-interest words through hands-on matching, discussion, and whiteboard responses. I will provide explicit steps, models, sentence frames, and a structured group. These supports address language processing and task initiation while allowing him to show understanding orally and in writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-84\"><p>Lesson 2 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-85\"><p>Fourth and fifth grades.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-86\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-87\"><p>Using Roots to Understand Words</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-88\"><p>Strands/Standards and Learning Goals Grade-level CA ELA/Literacy strand(s)/standard(s) 6</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-89\"><p>CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.4 Determine or clarify the meaning of unknown and multiple-meaning words and phrases based on grade 4 reading and content. \u25cf L.4.4b: Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word (e.g., telegraph, photograph ). \u25cf L.4.4c: Consult reference materials (e.g., glossaries, word lists) to determine or clarify meaning as needed. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.6 Acquire and use accurately grade-appropriate general academic and domain-specific words and phrases. Reading: Foundational Skills \u25cf RF.5.3 \u2013 Know and apply grade-level phonics and word analysis skills in decoding words. \u25cb Use combined knowledge of all letter-sound correspondences, syllabication patterns, and morphology (e.g., roots and affixes) to read unfamiliar multisyllabic words. Language \u25cf L.5.4 \u2013 Determine or clarify the meaning of unknown and multiple-meaning words and phrases. \u25cb Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-90\"><p>If the students are in an ATP , you should use the CA CCSS ELA/Literacy Standards.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-91\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-92\"><p>If the students are eligible for the California Alternate Assessments (CAA) for ELA/Literacy , you may use the Core Content Connectors (CCCs) aligned with the grade level of the students in order to plan your lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-93\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-94\"><p>If you are in a bilingual classroom where the language of instruction is Spanish and you plan on providing instruction in Spanish for the Literacy Cycle, you may provide the grade-level California CCSS en Espa\u00f1ol (ELA/Literacy Standard[s]).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-95\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-96\"><p>ELA/Literacy learning goal(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-97\"><p>Students will locate roots in complex words and use their meanings to explain unfamiliar vocabulary in spoken and written responses.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-98\"><p>If you are in a bilingual classroom where the language of instruction is Spanish and you plan on providing instruction in Spanish for the Literacy Cycle, you may provide developmentally appropriate ELA/Literacy learning goal(s) based on the selected California CCSS en Espa\u00f1ol (ELA/Literacy Standard[s]).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-99\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-100\"><p>Grade-level CA ELD strand(s)/standard(s) 7</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-101\"><p>CA ELD Standard (Grade 5) \u2013 Part I: Interacting in Meaningful Ways ELD.PI.5.6.a \u2013 Reading/View Explain ideas, phenomena, processes, and relationships from texts by using key details and domain-specific words.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-102\"><p>ELD learning goal(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-103\"><p>Students will explain unfamiliar words in complete sentences during partner discussion and brief writing, using knowledge of the root.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-104\"><p>If you are in a bilingual classroom where the language of instruction is Spanish and you plan on providing instruction in Spanish for the Literacy Cycle, you may provide developmentally appropriate SLD learning goal(s) based on the selected CA SLD Standard(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-105\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-106\"><p>Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-107\"><p>I will select roots and examples connected with science, sports, technology, and daily school experiences. Collaborative word building will draw on students&#x27; interest in sharing ideas. Familiar vocabulary and classroom responsibilities will help FS connect the analysis with something meaningful.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-108\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-109\"><p>I will model how to find a root in a longer word and think aloud about its meaning. Partners will identify or underline roots and explain the whole words using frames. As I circulate, I will ask guiding questions, clarify a misunderstanding, and provide immediate feedback.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-110\"><p>Describe how you plan to use a direct and explicit approach for teaching the selected foundational reading skill(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-111\"><p>I will define roots clearly, introduce grade-appropriate examples, and demonstrate how the central meaning contributes to a longer word. Students will first practice with me, then with support, and finally apply the process independently. This sequence makes the morphology strategy explicit.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-112\"><p>Summarize formative assessment(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-113\"><p>Students will write the root and complete word on a whiteboard and explain the meaning orally. I will check whether they identified the part accurately and used it to make sense of the whole word. The frame-supported explanation will also show progress toward the language goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-114\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-115\"><p>Models, sentence frames, and guided discussions will help students talk and write about roots. They will analyze words both alone and in short texts, then explain how the root helps with meaning. The task combines reading and interpretation with collaborative and written expression.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-116\"><p>Explain how you plan to collaborate with and/or facilitate instructional support personnel.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-117\"><p>I will review objectives, vocabulary, and frames with the paraeducator and speech-language pathologist. We will plan where additional prompts are needed during practice. Afterward, we will compare formative work and oral responses to identify students who need further root-word instruction or language support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-118\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student (FS), based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-119\"><p>FS will use word cards, whiteboards, and visual examples connected with animals, school, and daily activities. I will model how prefixes work with roots, check understanding often, and provide sentence frames. The structured practice will support processing and independent starting while making oral and written responses possible.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-120\"><p>Lesson 3 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-121\"><p>Fourth and fifth grades.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-122\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-123\"><p>How Suffixes Change Meaning and Grammatical Function</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-124\"><p>Strands/Standards and Learning Goals Grade-level CA ELA/Literacy strand(s)/standard(s) 8</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-125\"><p>CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.4.4.b Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word (e.g., telegraph, photograph, autograph). CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.5.4.b Use common, grade-appropriate Greek and Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word (e.g., photograph, photosynthesis).</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-126\"><p>If the students are in an ATP , you should use the CA CCSS ELA/Literacy Standards.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-127\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-128\"><p>If the students are eligible for the California Alternate Assessments (CAA) for ELA/Literacy , you may use the Core Content Connectors (CCCs) aligned with the grade level of the students in order to plan your lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-129\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-130\"><p>If you are in a bilingual classroom where the language of instruction is Spanish and you plan on providing instruction in Spanish for the Literacy Cycle, you may provide the grade-level California CCSS en Espa\u00f1ol (ELA/Literacy Standard[s]).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-131\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-132\"><p>ELA/Literacy learning goal(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-133\"><p>Students will identify suffixes such as -ful, -less, -ment, -er, -est, and -ly and explain how the ending changes a base word&#x27;s meaning or grammatical form. They will demonstrate this in oral and written responses using the lesson vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-134\"><p>If you are in a bilingual classroom where the language of instruction is Spanish and you plan on providing instruction in Spanish for the Literacy Cycle, you may provide developmentally appropriate ELA/Literacy learning goal(s) based on the selected California CCSS en Espa\u00f1ol (ELA/Literacy Standard[s]).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-135\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-136\"><p>Grade-level CA ELD strand(s)/standard(s) 9</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-137\"><p>ELA.ELD.PI.4-5.1 Expanding and using grade-appropriate academic language in reading, writing, speaking, and listening for a range of purposes, including determining word meaning and using affixes to understand new vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-138\"><p>ELD learning goal(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-139\"><p>Students will name a suffix, explain its effect on a grade-level word, and communicate that analysis orally and in writing with sentence-frame support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-140\"><p>If you are in a bilingual classroom where the language of instruction is Spanish and you plan on providing instruction in Spanish for the Literacy Cycle, you may provide developmentally appropriate SLD learning goal(s) based on the selected CA SLD Standard(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-141\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-142\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-143\"><p>Examples involving animals, sports, technology, celebrations, games, and pets will connect suffixes with familiar language. Students can use words they know from books, media, or conversation in their own sentences. For FS, choices tied to his hobbies and daily life will encourage participation in building and explaining words.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-144\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-145\"><p>I will model the suffix in careful, quickly, and teacher and explain what changes when it is added. Students will create new words on whiteboards, then explain them to a partner using frames. I will circulate to check the construction and meaning, reinforce precise vocabulary, and provide help where needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-146\"><p>Describe how you plan to use a direct and explicit approach for teaching the selected foundational reading skill(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-147\"><p>I will teach -ful, -less, -ly, -er, -est, and -ment directly, showing how to separate a base from its ending and interpret the result. Think-alouds will model the reasoning before students practice on whiteboards and with partners. They will then apply the strategy more independently.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-148\"><p>Summarize formative and/or summative assessment(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-149\"><p>The planned word-building task uses ten to twelve base words. Students will add a suitable suffix, record the new word, and explain how its meaning or grammatical role changed. They will complete the task independently, using the provided language supports for an oral or written explanation. The product and explanation together assess morphology and academic communication.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-150\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-151\"><p>Word banks, frames, models, and partner discussions will support the language needed to identify a suffix and explain its effect. Students will practice orally and in writing, both with classmates and independently. I will adjust the amount of support to their responses.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-152\"><p>Explain how you plan to collaborate with and/or facilitate instructional support personnel.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-153\"><p>The paraeducator, speech-language pathologist, and I will review suffix vocabulary, expected responses, and planned scaffolds before instruction. During practice, support will focus on access and meaningful student participation. Afterward, we will examine work and assessment results to plan any additional morphology or language instruction.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-154\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student (FS), based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-155\"><p>FS will use engaging vocabulary, hands-on word manipulation, partner work, and choices that connect with his interests. Sentence frames, visual suffix references, smaller instruction steps, extra processing time, and frequent check-ins will support access. Guided practice before independent work will help reduce frustration while drawing on his oral-language strengths.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-156\"><p>Template notes</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-157\"><p>1 Recent observations, assessments (e.g., screenings; formative, summative, state testing), literacy and/or language profiles, student work samples, IEP information, and/or information provided by your cooperating teacher, supervising faculty, service providers, and/or families/guardians</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-158\"><p>2 Foundational reading skills also include creating literacy environments that are print rich and that foster interest in print; and games, books, poetry, oral storytelling, and songs that draw attention to print, the manipulation of sounds, and alphabet letters.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-159\"><p>3 Additional themes: meaning making, effective expression, language development, content knowledge</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-160\"><p>4 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-161\"><p>5 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-162\"><p>6 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-163\"><p>7 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-164\"><p>8 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-165\"><p>9 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-166\"><p>If needed, copy blank tables from Lesson 3 above for Lesson 4 and Lesson 5.</p></div>"}, {"id": "C", "title": "Part C Planning Narrative", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-167\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 1: Plan\u2014Part C: Written Narrative: Description of Assessments Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-168\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 5 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 16\u201318 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-169\"><p>Formative Assessment</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-170\"><p>\u25cf Describe one formative assessment you plan to implement during the learning segment (e.g., checks for understanding, exit ticket, self-assessment).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-171\"><p>I will use a whiteboard morphology check during guided practice in each lesson. After modeling a word such as rebuild, transportation, or helpful, I will present a new word orally and visually. Students will identify and underline the relevant part, write its meaning, and explain the reasoning when asked.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-172\"><p>I will review responses immediately, clarify misconceptions, and provide prompts, visual references, or frames where needed while still expecting students to identify the part themselves. Partner explanations will add evidence of language use. These checks will guide reteaching, grouping, additional scaffolds, or extension throughout the sequence.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-173\"><p>\u25cf Describe what you expect the students to know and be able to demonstrate in their responses to the selected formative assessment and how you plan to monitor student understanding of the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals during the learning segment.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-174\"><p>Students should be able to identify a prefix, root, or suffix, explain its meaning, and connect it with the whole word. I will check the accuracy of their whiteboards and listen to their explanations. For the language goal, I will look for complete, meaningful responses using morphology vocabulary and the available frames. Patterns in those responses will help me decide whether to model again, provide more practice, or move forward.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-175\"><p>\u25cf If you chose to use a student self-assessment , explain how the student self-assessment provides opportunities for the students to build an awareness of what they have learned, what they need to learn, and what they need additional support with in relation to making progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-176\"><p>I did not include a student self-assessment.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-177\"><p>Summative Assessment</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-178\"><p>\u25cf Describe the summative assessment you plan to implement during the final lesson of your learning segment.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-179\"><p>Students will complete the teacher-created Word Structure Checkpoint independently. I will read the directions and objective aloud and model a separate example before they begin. During the assessment, I will monitor engagement and use only neutral prompts.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-180\"><p>The assessment has three parts: separating and defining word parts, explaining target words in complete sentences, and constructing a real or realistic word from a bank of parts. The rubric assigns 40 total points for accurate analysis, meaning, sentence use, and logical word creation. I will use the results to identify patterns and plan reteaching or extension, including support for FS.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-181\"><p>\u25cf What ELA/Literacy and ELD goals are evaluated by the summative assessment (e.g., knowledge, skills, or abilities your students will demonstrate)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-182\"><p>The assessment checks whether students can identify and analyze word parts, use them to explain meaning, and apply them to create a word. Complete sentences and precise vocabulary show how well they communicate that understanding. Requiring independent written application links the literacy and language goals in the same task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-183\"><p>\u25cf What are you planning to collect or observe from each student as an assessment response (product, process, or performance)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-184\"><p>I will collect each completed Word Structure Checkpoint. It will contain identified parts, written definitions, sentences, and a created word with an explanation. This product gives me concrete evidence of accuracy and language use that can be scored consistently and examined for both class patterns and individual progress.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-185\"><p>\u25cf What evidence will you look for in the students\u2019 responses to indicate that the students are progressing toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-186\"><p>Progress will appear in accurate identification, complete definitions that combine the parts, and logical word creation. For language, I will look for precise vocabulary and complete explanations. The rubric and written responses will show whether students can apply morphological awareness and communicate the resulting meaning together.</p></div>"}, {"id": "F", "title": "Part F Teaching Commentary", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-187\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 2: Teach and Assess\u2014Part F: Commentary Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-188\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt that relates to each video clip that you will submit (up to 8 pages). You must address each prompt at least once across all the video(s) submitted. Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 25\u201326 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-189\"><p>In the commentary for your video clip(s), address the following prompts:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-190\"><p>\u25cf What direct and explicit approach did you use to teach the selected foundational reading skill(s), actively engage students, and support their progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals? Why did the selected approach support the literacy and language development of your students?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-191\"><p>\u25cf Describe the selected additional theme(s) you are teaching in the video clip(s). Explain why the selected theme(s) actively engage students and support their progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-192\"><p>\u25cf Describe the integrated ELD you used in the video clip(s). Explain why this instruction supported students in progressing toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-193\"><p>\u25cf Describe the strategy(ies) you used to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests. o Why did the strategy(ies) support your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD learning goals?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-194\"><p>\u25cf How did you use assessment(s) to monitor student progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goal(s) in the video clip(s)?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-195\"><p>\u25cf Given what you learned from the assessment(s), explain adjustments you made to your instruction and why they supported students\u2019 literacy and language development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-196\"><p>\u25cf What specific, actionable feedback related to literacy did you provide to students about what they did well and/or their misconceptions/gaps in knowledge? Why was this feedback able to support students\u2019 literacy and language development?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-197\"><p>Video Clip 1: Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-198\"><p>At 0:55\u20131:25, I invite familiar examples of words with prefixes, connecting the lesson with vocabulary students already know. At 1:25\u20131:50, my Google Slides model explains a prefix and shows how un- changes happy. Thinking aloud makes the relationship between a word part and meaning explicit while modeling the academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-199\"><p>From 2:02\u20133:10, partners explain prefixes and share examples. They can begin with their own words and use the frames to develop a clearer response. This draws on oral-language strengths and peer relationships while addressing meaning and language development.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-200\"><p>From 4:45\u20138:00, students build words with cards, record them on whiteboards, and explain them. I circulate to assess the choices, combinations, and reasoning. I acknowledge accurate use of a prefix and, when needed, return students to its meaning\u2014for example, asking them to reconsider whether re-, meaning again, fits the chosen root. Visual cues, simpler questions, and further examples provide different levels of feedback. Students who are ready explain another example or examine the meaning more fully. These observations let me adjust instruction while students are still working.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-201\"><p>Video Clip 2 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-202\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-203\"><p>Video Clip 3 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-204\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-205\"><p>Video Clip 4 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-206\"></div>"}, {"id": "H", "title": "Part H Summative Assessment Feedback", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-207\"><p>Step 3: Reflect \u2014 Part H: Focus Student\u2019s</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-208\"><p>Summative Assessment Actionable Feedback</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-209\"><p>I gave the focus student feedback that recognized accurate identification of prefixes, roots, and suffixes and the ability to combine them into new words. I explained that these were strengths to keep using.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-210\"><p>The next step was to explain the whole word rather than the meaning of only one part. I asked the student to say what each part means and then bring those meanings together in a single clear explanation. I also modeled a sentence frame that links the whole-word definition to the meanings of its prefix and root.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-211\"><p>This feedback built on the student&#x27;s word-building skills while targeting greater precision in meaning and academic language. The spoken rehearsal and sentence frame provided a practical way to improve the next explanation.</p></div>"}, {"id": "I", "title": "Part I Assessment Reflection", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-212\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 3: Reflect\u2014Part I: Written Narrative: Reflection and Analysis of</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-213\"><p>Summative Assessment Results Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-214\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 4 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. Be sure to include specific references to the results to support your reflections: What in the results led you to your conclusions? For more information, see pages 34\u201335 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-215\"><p>Analysis and Reflection for the Group</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-216\"><p>\u25cf Identify and describe understandings, gaps in knowledge, and/or misconceptions related to the students\u2019 literacy and language development.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-217\"><p>Students generally identified word parts and created words successfully. Their discussions also showed growing vocabulary and ability to explain the process. The weaker area was combining all the parts into a precise definition. Some explanations addressed only one part or remained vague.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-218\"><p>The rubric pattern supports that conclusion: most students received full or nearly full credit for identification and creation, while incomplete meanings cost points in Parts 1 and 2. I would therefore continue applying the morphology strategy while giving more attention to a complete explanation of the whole word.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-219\"><p>\u25cf Describe what was effective about your instructional approach(es) and student activity(ies) for the group.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-220\"><p>Explicit models and sentence frames gave students a clear process, while cards, whiteboards, and partner exchanges let them apply it actively. Strong performance on word-part identification and creation suggests those approaches supported learning. Students&#x27; oral explanations also showed that the activity gave them a meaningful way to use academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-221\"><p>\u25cf Describe instructional changes you would make that could have better supported the group if you taught this learning segment again.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-222\"><p>I would add guided practice combining the meanings of the prefix, root, and suffix into one definition. An organizer showing all three meanings together would make that synthesis visible. More partner rehearsal before writing would help students develop a complete explanation rather than list separate parts.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-223\"><p>Analysis and Reflection for the Focus Student (FS)</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-224\"><p>\u25cf Identify and describe understandings, gaps in knowledge, and/or misconceptions related to the FS\u2019s literacy and language development.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-225\"><p>FS earned 16/20 for word-part identification, 9/10 for meaning, and 10/10 for creation, totaling 35/40. He can identify and combine parts and use a frame to explain them. Some definitions, however, are only partly complete. The next step is greater precision in bringing all the parts together into the whole word&#x27;s meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-226\"><p>\u25cf How will the specific, actionable feedback you provided the FS help them to improve or advance their understanding to continue their progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-227\"><p>I recognized FS&#x27;s successful word analysis and creation, then identified complete definitions as the next area of work. Naming each part&#x27;s meaning before combining them into a sentence gives him a concrete process to follow. The language frame supports a more precise explanation and helps him apply morphology in future reading and writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-228\"><p>\u25cf Given your knowledge of the FS, describe what was effective about your instructional approach(es) and student activity(ies). Refer to the FS\u2019s summative assessment response.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-229\"><p>The explicit steps and modeled explanations provided a structure FS could follow. Hands-on construction, whiteboard work, and discussion let him apply the strategy and receive immediate feedback. His strong word creation and knowledge of word structure show the value of those supports. I would retain them while increasing the demand for a complete meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-230\"><p>\u25cf Describe instructional changes you would you make for the FS if you taught this learning segment again. Refer to the FS\u2019s summative assessment response.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-231\"><p>I would add more guided examples of synthesizing all the parts into a precise definition. In a Word Detective activity, FS could break down an unfamiliar word, explain its full meaning aloud, and then write. That sequence would target the gap shown in his assessment while building on his oral reasoning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-232\"><p>\u25cf What feedback will you provide to the family/guardian(s) of the FS specific to the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals to support caregivers in understanding the assessment results and how they can support the FS\u2019s learning beyond the classroom? Refer to evidence from their assessment results.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-233\"><p>I will share the results through a progress report and a parent-teacher conference. Examples from the assessment will show FS&#x27;s strengths in identifying and creating words and the need for fuller definitions. I will explain how his sentence frames and vocabulary support English expression and comprehension. At home, caregivers can build familiar words with him, discuss new meanings, and encourage complete oral explanations before writing.</p></div>"}, {"id": "J", "title": "Part J Follow Up Activity", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-234\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs (MMSN) Step 4: Apply\u2014 Part J: Written Narrative: Re-Teaching or Extension Activity Description Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-235\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 5 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 39\u201340 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-236\"><p>\u25cf Activity (place an \u201cX\u201d in the appropriate bracket):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-237\"><p>] re-teaching activity OR [X] extension activity</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-238\"><p>\u25cf Provide the developmentally appropriate ELA/Literacy learning goal(s). 1</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-239\"><p>The extension connects with RF.4.3.A and RF.5.3.A, applying sound-symbol knowledge, syllabication, and morphology to unfamiliar multisyllabic words. Students will analyze prefixes, roots, and suffixes in a grade-level passage, explain how the parts contribute to meaning, and use the words accurately in spoken and written sentences.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-240\"><p>\u25cf Provide the developmentally appropriate ELD learning goal(s). 2 The follow-up activity must include an ELD learning goal even if your focus student (FS)/group of students does not include an English learner.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-241\"><p>The language connection is ELD.PI.4\u20135.1: participating in discussion by listening, taking turns, asking questions, and building on ideas. Students will use academic vocabulary and sentence frames to explain how word parts contribute to unfamiliar meanings, then apply that language when discussing and using the new words in context.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-242\"><p>\u25cf Summarize your activity and explain how you will check for understanding.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-243\"><p>I will model word analysis with a think-aloud and a visual chart. Students will identify unfamiliar words in a grade-level passage, separate the parts on whiteboards, discuss meanings, and write a sentence using an analyzed word. Questions, partner talk, whiteboards, and the final sentence will provide immediate evidence of both morphological reasoning and language use.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-244\"><p>\u25cf For this activity, describe how you will leverage the FS\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-245\"><p>A passage about volcanoes will connect the extension with students&#x27; interest in science and discovery. They can use prior knowledge and familiar vocabulary while examining new words. Discussion, frames, and whiteboard work will provide several ways to contribute, making the reading and writing relevant to FS&#x27;s strengths and interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-246\"><p>\u25cf Determine adaptations to address the FS\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014learning needs (e.g., accommodations and/or modifications, language supports, IEP supports/services).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-247\"><p>FS will have a modeled analysis chart, sentence frames, partner support, and additional guided practice. Highlighters or colored pencils can distinguish prefixes, roots, and suffixes. Oral and written examples, processing time, and immediate feedback will support precise meaning and sentence use while building independence with grade-level text.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-248\"><p>\u25cf Explain why this activity supports the FS\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014literacy progress. Refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 to support your instructional decision making.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-249\"><p>Earlier work and discussion show that students can identify parts but still need practice applying them independently in context. The extension uses that foundation and asks them to explain complete meanings in authentic reading and writing. It targets vocabulary, comprehension, and precision without restarting skills they have already demonstrated.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-250\"><p>\u25cf Provide an analysis of the recent literacy assessments 3 used in Step 1 and the formative and summative assessments used in Steps 2 and 3 to support your instructional decision making.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-251\"><p>Work samples, oral reading checks, and observations informed the original sequence. Formative work showed growing accuracy with isolated parts, while the final assessment showed readiness to extend the strategy with support for fuller meanings. I chose an extension so students could transfer those skills to a passage, original sentences, and discussion. The new checks will show whether they can use morphology more independently in context.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-252\"><p>Template notes</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-253\"><p>2 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development .</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-254\"><p>1 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-255\"><p>3 For example, screenings; formative, summative, state testing; literacy and/or language profiles; student work samples; IEP information; observations; as well as information provided by your cooperating teacher, supervising faculty, service providers, and/or families/guardians</p></div>"}, {"id": "L", "title": "Part L Follow Up Commentary", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-256\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 4: Apply\u2014Part L: Commentary Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-257\"><p>Directions: Type your response within the brackets provided (up to 2 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 40\u201341 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-258\"><p>In your commentary, address one of the following prompts:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-259\"><p>For a re-teaching activity:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-260\"><p>\u25cf Identify the targeted intervention and/or individualized strategy being used. Explain why this supports your focus student (FS)\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student(s)\u2014in making progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals. OR</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-261\"><p>For an extension activity:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-262\"><p>\u25cf Identify the instruction provided that deepened or advanced your FS\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014learning. Explain why this activity supports your FS\u2014and if applicable, the additional student(s)\u2014to take the next step in learning related to the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals. Video Clip: Address the commentary prompt that relates to this video. Describe what is happening and provide an explanation for what you are doing and why.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-263\"><p>From 0:45\u20131:35, I model how un-, forget, and -able contribute to unforgettable. The think-aloud shows students how to use the parts together rather than stop at identifying them. It connects word analysis with meaning in reading.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-264\"><p>From 1:35\u20133:30, students separate the word on their whiteboards and explain the meanings with sentence frames. Oral and written practice helps them form complete explanations and use the vocabulary precisely. The activity advances the literacy and language goals by requiring students to apply the strategy and communicate their reasoning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-265\"><p>Retained Source Materials</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-literacy-266\"><p>The following source pages retain the instructional materials, assessment tools, and student work as supplied.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image1.png\" data-caption=\"Grades 4\u20135 word study: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image1.png\" alt=\"Grades 4\u20135 word study: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image2.png\" data-caption=\"Grades 4\u20135 word study: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image2.png\" alt=\"Grades 4\u20135 word study: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image3.png\" data-caption=\"Grades 4\u20135 word study: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image3.png\" alt=\"Grades 4\u20135 word study: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image4.png\" data-caption=\"Grades 4\u20135 word study: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image4.png\" alt=\"Grades 4\u20135 word study: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure>"}], "images": ["assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image1.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image2.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image3.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-literacy-image4.png"], "responseCount": 105, "words": 7409, "historical": false}, "ms-literacy": {"id": "ms-literacy", "path": "ms-literacy", "title": "Grade 3 roots and affixes", "file": "03_MS_Literacy_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 MS LPA Sample Submission.docx", "parts": [{"id": "A", "title": "Part A Contextual Information", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-1\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014 Part A: Written Narrative: Contextual Information Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-2\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 4 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 7\u20139 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-3\"><p>Contextual Information About the Group of Students</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-4\"><p>\u2022 Age range(s) and grade level(s):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-5\"><p>The students are in third grade and range from eight to nine years old.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-6\"><p>\u2022 Student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to the literacy content you plan to teach:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-7\"><p>Students bring Hispanic/Latino, Venezuelan, White, and African American backgrounds to our literacy work. Many use both Spanish and English. Their experiences give them different ways to discuss a text and connect new vocabulary to words they already know. They have worked with prefixes, suffixes, and roots, so this unit builds on that knowledge. Spanish-English cognates and related word parts offer another entry into word analysis.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-8\"><p>I will pair visual examples and explicit modeling with guided practice. In partner conversations, students can explain a word&#x27;s structure, compare language patterns, and hear another person&#x27;s reasoning. These routines make room for their home languages and experiences while developing the vocabulary they need for reading and discussion.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-9\"><p>\u2022 Literacy knowledge, skills, and/or abilities:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-10\"><p>Most students read grade-level material with suitable pace and expression and understand main ideas, supporting details, and event sequence. They identify characters, setting, problems, solutions, and themes and compare these features across texts. Their growing knowledge of roots and affixes helps them read unfamiliar words; many also draw on English-Spanish cognates.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-11\"><p>Students write complete sentences and paragraphs and use grade-appropriate conventions. Brainstorming, story maps, and other organizers help them plan. Sentence frames and discussion routines support their explanations. They check comprehension by summarizing, questioning, and making inferences. They also work well together during reading and writing and offer peers useful feedback. Visual references, guided work, and oral rehearsal allow them to use these strengths while extending their literacy skills.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-12\"><p>\u2022 Identified English learners (see SB 210 for Deaf and Hard of Hearing students):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-13\"><p>Five students are identified English learners. Two are at Bridging and need occasional language support as they approach grade-level proficiency. One is at Expanding and needs focused support with academic language. Two are at Emerging; visuals, demonstrations, and structured speaking opportunities help them participate in grade-level instruction.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-14\"><p>\u2022 I dentified learning needs (e.g., number of students with an IEP, a 504 plan, supplemental or intensified support through MTSS):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-15\"><p>One student with Autism Spectrum Disorder has a 504 plan. A second student is being evaluated for ASD and has no formal diagnosis at this point. Three students work outside the classroom with an intervention specialist on phonics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-16\"><p>\u2022 L anguage(s) of instruction to be used in the learning segment (e.g., biliteracy, developmental bilingual [one-way immersion], dual language [two-way immersion], English only):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-17\"><p>Instruction and materials will be in English. I will support English learners through demonstrations, pictures, gestures, and sentence frames so that language demands do not prevent them from understanding or participating.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-18\"><p>\u2022 Staff member(s) who will assist you in the learning segment and their role in the classroom, if applicable (e.g., co-teachers, paraprofessionals, interpreters, bilingual instructional assistants, education specialists):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-19\"><p>My mentor teacher will teach alongside me.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-20\"><p>Contextual Information About the Focus Student</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-21\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-22\"><p>The focus student is eight years old and in third grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-23\"><p>\u2022 Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to literacy and language:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-24\"><p>The focus student is an English learner at Expanding whose primary language is Spanish. He uses his knowledge of Spanish as he develops English literacy. He readily asks questions and shares ideas, showing confidence in classroom conversation. Soccer is an interest, and he connects teamwork on the field with working together in class. Books, science, and adventure also draw him into reading and new vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-25\"><p>His curiosity about math and science supports problem solving, analysis, and following several steps. These strengths carry into comprehension and writing. He is beginning to recognize how prefixes and suffixes work and can use that awareness to approach unfamiliar words.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-26\"><p>\u2022 If your focus student is a district-/school-identified English learner, Fluent English Proficient (reclassified English learner), Heritage language speaker, and/or multilingual, describe the student\u2019s literacy knowledge and skills in their primary language(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-27\"><p>He has made steady progress in English reading and writing this year. His reading of grade-level texts is becoming more fluent, and he answers comprehension questions in both small and whole groups. He identifies central ideas and details, summarizes narratives, and connects texts. He is also beginning to use roots and affixes when working out unfamiliar vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-28\"><p>He plans with story maps and graphic organizers and writes short, organized paragraphs using complete sentences and grade-appropriate conventions. In discussions, he explains his reasoning and contributes ideas that help classmates learn.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-29\"><p>\u2022 Literacy knowledge, skills, and abilities:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-30\"><p>He reads grade-level narrative and informational texts with increasing fluency and understands main ideas, important details, and sequence. With appropriate support, he answers questions orally and in writing. He is learning to identify bases and affixes and use them to decode words and explain meaning. His explanations use simple academic language, though he does not yet connect each affix to its base consistently.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-31\"><p>His sentences and short paragraphs generally communicate a clear, organized idea. Spelling and punctuation are usually appropriate for the grade, with occasional errors. New vocabulary is easier for him to discuss than to apply consistently in writing. He participates readily with partners and groups and is becoming more confident about explaining his thinking.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-32\"><p>\u2022 Learning needs related to the focus student\u2019s literacy and language development:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-33\"><p>He needs continued explicit practice breaking unfamiliar words into meaningful parts and putting those meanings together. Complex vocabulary and directions with several steps sometimes require clarification. He also needs help arranging ideas into connected sentences and paragraphs and using new words accurately with appropriate grammar.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-34\"><p>I will use word banks, sentence frames, visuals, and think-alouds to show the process. Guided conversations and partner or small-group practice will let him rehearse explanations before writing them. The purpose is to help him carry decoding and vocabulary strategies across reading and writing with less assistance.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-35\"><p>\u2022 If your focus student is an English learner, learning needs related to their English language development:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-36\"><p>As an English learner at Expanding, he contributes willingly to class activities and reads aloud with growing fluency. His next steps include decoding unfamiliar affixed words, using academic vocabulary precisely, and expanding and organizing written sentences. He benefits from practice explaining ideas in complete sentences and from support with multistep directions and independent strategy use.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-37\"><p>During reading and writing, I will provide models, visual references, organizers, sentence frames, and think-alouds. Partner and small-group talk will give him repeated chances to rehearse academic language, check meaning, and build confidence before working independently.</p></div>"}, {"id": "B", "title": "Part B Lesson Planning", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-38\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014 Part B: Learning Segment Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-39\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt. Do not delete or alter the prompts. You may provide up to 5 pages per lesson. For more information, see pages 10\u201314 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. Across the 3\u20135 literacy lessons: Scope and Sequence Explain how your 3\u20135 lessons fit into the larger scope and sequence of the unit (systematic instruction).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-40\"><p>This four-lesson sequence extends earlier work with single-syllable words and basic affixes into multisyllabic word analysis. Phonics and word recognition remain the foundational focus; language development runs through the sequence as students explain meanings aloud and in writing. Integrated ELD support is included in each lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-41\"><p>Lesson 1 uses tract to introduce how a root combines with prefixes and suffixes. I model with a pocket chart before students build words with support. Lesson 2 expands the work to pre-, re-, -ful, and -less through class discussion and guided word study. Lesson 3 asks students to apply their learning independently with pre-, re-, dis-, un-, -ing, -less, -ful, and -able. Lesson 4 extends the work into a passage, where students use both word parts and context to understand vocabulary. The sequence moves from demonstration to supported practice, independent application, and use in connected text.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-42\"><p>Summarize how your 3\u20135 lessons apply findings from previous literacy assessments.1</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-43\"><p>Earlier observations, vocabulary tasks, writing samples, and formative checks showed that students could read grade-level material but differed in how well they used affixes to determine meaning. Some recognized roots only tentatively; others decoded and explained words more confidently.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-44\"><p>Lesson 1 therefore makes the structure of tract words explicit. Lesson 2 gives students supported practice with additional affixes and oral explanations. Lesson 3 checks whether they can identify parts, spell words, explain meanings, and use vocabulary on their own. Lesson 4 addresses the difficulty some students had transferring word analysis into comprehension. Across the sequence, assessment findings guide the amount of modeling, language support, and independent work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-45\"><p>Identify the selected foundational reading skill(s)2 students will learn and/or apply within a lesson and/or across your lesson activities.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-46\"><p>Students will develop phonics and word recognition by studying morphology: bases, prefixes, and suffixes. They will break multisyllabic words into meaningful parts, build and spell words, and explain how an affix changes meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-47\"><p>In Lesson 1, I demonstrate word building before students manipulate parts and discuss their meanings. Lesson 2 carries that work into print through worksheets, definitions, and sentences. Students connect recognizable affixes with accurate reading and spelling. In Lesson 3, they choose words, identify each part, explain the whole, write sentences, draw representations, and generate further examples. This independent task shows whether they can transfer the strategy to other words and communicate their understanding.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-48\"><p>If you are providing supplemental support for students for the selected foundational reading skill(s), explain why these skills are needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-49\"><p>Assessment evidence showed that some students, particularly English learners and students still developing decoding skills, had difficulty analyzing multisyllabic words without help. They did not consistently connect an affix with the meaning of the base. Additional practice is needed so they can use a repeatable strategy instead of guessing from part of a word.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-50\"><p>Word banks, pictures, teacher demonstrations, sentence frames, and supported peer or small-group work will make the steps manageable. Students can see recurring patterns, connect structure with meaning, and gradually take more responsibility for the analysis. These supports help them access grade-level vocabulary and explain their thinking in speaking and writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-51\"><p>Identify the selected additional theme(s)3 from the ELA/ELD Framework you plan to teach within a lesson and/or across your lesson activities.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-52\"><p>Language Development is the additional theme. Students will expand vocabulary by examining how roots and affixes contribute to meaning. They will explain and compare related words and use new vocabulary in oral and written contexts. Structured conversations ask them to share decoding strategies and put definitions into their own words. Sentence frames, word banks, and visual references support precise explanations, especially for English learners.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-53\"><p>Explain how your lessons align with one another to create a progression of learning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-54\"><p>The lessons build on one another by changing how much support students receive and where they apply the strategy. Lesson 1 makes word structure visible through tract, a pocket chart, and guided word building. Lesson 2 introduces additional affixes and uses a shared recording sheet, discussion, and guided worksheets to strengthen meaning-making. Lesson 3 asks students to analyze and use words independently. Lesson 4 places those skills in a story, requiring students to combine word-part knowledge with context. Each lesson uses the same underlying process while increasing the demands on students&#x27; reading and language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-55\"><p>Lesson 1 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-56\"><p>Third grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-57\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-58\"><p>Using prefixes and suffixes to build phonics and word-recognition skills.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-59\"><p>Strands/Standards and Learning Goals Grade-level CA ELA/Literacy4 strand(s)/standard(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-60\"><p>CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.3.3.A: Identify and know the meaning of the most common prefixes and derivational suffixes. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.3.3.B: Decode words with common Latin suffixes. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.3.3.C: Decode multisyllable words. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.2.E: Use conventional spelling for high-frequency and other studied words for adding suffixes to base words (e.g., sitting, smiled, cried, happiness).</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-61\"><p>ELA/Literacy learning goal(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-62\"><p>Students will build and read multisyllabic words from tract, the prefixes re-, dis-, sub-, and de-, and the suffixes -ed, -ing, -ion, and -er. They will explain the contribution each affix makes to the word&#x27;s meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-63\"><p>ELD strand(s)/standard(s)5</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-64\"><p>Part I: Interacting in Meaningful Ways (A. Collaborative): 1. Exchanging information and ideas with others through oral collaborative conversations on a range of social and academics topics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-65\"><p>ELD learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-66\"><p>work with peers to identify word parts, build words, and explain their meanings using the academic vocabulary of roots, prefixes, and suffixes.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-67\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-68\"><p>I will connect word study to students&#x27; home languages, community experiences, and prior learning. Subtract, distract, and tractor are examples they may know from mathematics, conversation, or daily life. Starting with familiar words gives them a concrete way to discuss a root and its affixes. Students can contribute their own connections and use what they already know to approach the new vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-69\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-70\"><p>I will introduce tract with a tractor picture and explain its meaning: to pull, drag, or draw. I will then show how adding a suffix produces tractor and explain the roles of prefixes and suffixes. At the pocket chart, I will combine tract with re-, dis-, sub-, de-, -ed, -ing, -ion, and -er, saying the parts, spelling the resulting word, and explaining its meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-71\"><p>Pairs of volunteers will then build words at the chart with my guidance. In small groups, students will use flashcard kits to form words with the same parts and record them on a sheet. Their recorded words and conversations will show how they are applying the strategy.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-72\"><p>Describe how you plan to use a direct and explicit approach for teaching the selected foundational reading skill(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-73\"><p>I will model one step at a time: identify tract, explain its meaning with the picture, add an affix, read and spell the new word, and use it in a sentence. Students will then build words in pairs at the pocket chart while I ask about the affix and the resulting meaning. In small groups, they will repeat the process with flashcards, record the parts, and explain their choices aloud. Seeing, handling, reading, and discussing the parts will connect decoding with meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-74\"><p>Summarize formative assessment(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-75\"><p>During guided work, I will observe pairs at the pocket chart and ask them to identify the affix, explain its effect, and use the word in a sentence. I can respond immediately to confusion about spelling, pronunciation, or meaning. During group work, I will listen to explanations and review the recording sheets for accurate roots, prefixes, suffixes, and spelling. These checks will show who is ready to apply the strategy and who needs another model.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-76\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-77\"><p>An anchor chart will define bases, prefixes, and suffixes, and the tractor image will make tract&#x27;s meaning concrete. I will pronounce and explain each part as I model word building. Students will then use the same vocabulary in guided discussion and small-group work. The chart and picture will remain available as they explain words, check spelling, and complete their recording sheets. This gives English learners a consistent link between spoken language, print, and meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-78\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student, based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-79\"><p>I will provide the focus student with individual guidance at the pocket chart so he can identify each part and explain the resulting word. During small-group practice, he will work with a peer performing at or above grade level. The definition chart and tract picture will remain available for checking meaning and spelling. These supports build on his prior knowledge and willingness to participate while making word analysis more manageable.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-80\"><p>Lesson 2 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-81\"><p>Third grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-82\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-83\"><p>Forming and explaining words with prefixes and suffixes.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-84\"><p>Strands/Standards and Learning Goals Grade-level CA ELA/Literacy6 strand(s)/standard(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-85\"><p>CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.R.F.3.3.A: Identify and know the meaning of most common prefixes and derivational suffixes. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.A: Determine the meaning of a word formed when a known affix is added to a known word. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.B: Determine the meaning of new words formed when a known affix is added to a known word. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.C: Use a known root word as a clue to the meaning of an unknown word with the same root word.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-86\"><p>ELA/Literacy learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-87\"><p>build words with pre-, re-, -ful, and -less, contribute examples in class discussion, and explain word meanings on a guided word-study sheet.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-88\"><p>ELD strand(s)/standard(s)7</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-89\"><p>Part I: Interacting in Meaningful Ways (A. Collaborative): 1. Exchanging information and ideas with others through oral collaborative conversations on a range of social and academics topics. Part 1: Interacting in Meaningful Ways (B. Interpretive): 5. Listening actively to spoken English in a range of social and academic contexts.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-90\"><p>ELD learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-91\"><p>use target affixes in word building, explain the resulting meanings to partners and the class, and communicate those meanings orally or in writing on the word-study task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-92\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-93\"><p>I will invite students to offer words they know from home, school, and community life. Preorder may connect to a family&#x27;s shopping, while helpful may describe someone they know. Partners and groups will discuss these connections and explain meanings to each other. I will also draw on familiar routines, hobbies, and community experiences. An anchor chart will help students connect the words they contribute with their structure and meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-94\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-95\"><p>I will introduce pre- as before, re- as again, -ful as full of, and -less as without. On the anchor chart, I will write examples such as preorder, prearrange, redo, refresh, careful, helpful, fearless, and hopeless and model sentences that show their meanings.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-96\"><p>The class will generate more examples. I will ask students to explain the affix, identify the base&#x27;s meaning, and use the whole word in a sentence. Students will then complete two guided worksheets, one for prefixes and one for suffixes, choosing words from the banks and writing their own definitions. The anchor chart will remain available for spelling and meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-97\"><p>Describe how you plan to use a direct and explicit approach for teaching the selected foundational reading skill(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-98\"><p>I will explicitly name each target affix and demonstrate its meaning through a familiar word and a complete sentence. My think-aloud will connect the base, the affix, and the whole word. During class practice, students will propose examples and explain their reasoning with guiding questions. They will then work with a partner on the prefix and suffix sheets, using the word banks and chart to write definitions. The repeated sequence makes the analysis visible before students try it themselves.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-99\"><p>Summarize formative assessment(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-100\"><p>The class discussion will let me hear whether students understand the target affixes and can apply them to new examples. I will ask about the affix, the base, and a sentence using the word, then clarify errors as they arise. During worksheet practice, I will review spelling and definitions and listen to partners&#x27; explanations. Together, the oral and written evidence will guide further support or a more demanding task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-101\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-102\"><p>I will consistently model the terms prefix, suffix, base word, and meaning and connect them to examples on the chart. Class discussion will give students practice explaining how an affix changes a word. The worksheets will extend that language into written definitions. English learners will work with peers reading at or above grade level so they can rehearse explanations and learn from a partner&#x27;s language as they complete the task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-103\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student, based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-104\"><p>The focus student will have an anchor chart, modeled examples, and clear steps for connecting affixes with meaning. I will check his understanding during both guided and individual work. I will invite him to draw on familiar words, including words from his home language, and connect examples to his experiences. Partner talk will give him a supported place to practice the academic language before writing.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-105\"><p>Lesson 3 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-106\"><p>Third grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-107\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-108\"><p>Analyzing affixed words and creating related examples.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-109\"><p>Strands/Standards and Learning Goals Grade-level CA ELA/Literacy8 strand(s)/standard(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-110\"><p>CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.R.F.3.3.A: Identify and know the meaning of most common prefixes and derivational suffixes. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.A: Determine the meaning of a word formed when a known affix is added to a known word. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.B: Determine the meaning of new words formed when a known affix is added to a known word. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.L.3.4.C: Use a known root word as a clue to the meaning of an unknown word with the same root word.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-111\"><p>ELA/Literacy learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-112\"><p>choose a word, identify and explain its base and affix, show its meaning in a sentence and picture, and produce other words with that affix.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-113\"><p>ELD strand(s)/standard(s)9</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-114\"><p>Part I: Interacting in Meaningful Ways (C. Productive): Express information and ideas in writing using academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-115\"><p>ELD learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-116\"><p>communicate the meanings of affixed words through clear written explanations and visual representations.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-117\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-118\"><p>Students will use the words and shared recording sheet from Lesson 2 as a starting point. The new banks include pre-, re-, dis-, un-, -ing, -less, -ful, and -able. I will encourage connections to home-language words and cognates. Students may use sports, games, family experiences, or hobbies in their sentences and pictures so that the vocabulary connects with something meaningful to them.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-119\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-120\"><p>Students will complete two word-study sheets as the summative task. I will first model the process with unhappy for prefixes and careful for suffixes: identify the parts, explain the word, write a sentence, and draw its meaning. Students will then work individually with words from the banks. They will analyze each chosen word, provide a sentence and visual, and generate further words using the same affix. The completed work will show how they apply the skills taught in the earlier lessons.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-121\"><p>Describe how you plan to use a direct and explicit approach for teaching the selected foundational reading skill(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-122\"><p>Using the chart, I will think aloud through unhappy and careful. I will show where the affix occurs, identify the base, explain how the parts combine, and demonstrate a sentence and picture. Each step will be named and modeled before students complete their own sheets. This provides a clear process for word analysis without leaving students to infer the task from directions alone.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-123\"><p>Summarize formative and/or summative assessment(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-124\"><p>Lesson 2&#x27;s sheets provide guided practice and formative evidence; Lesson 3&#x27;s sheets ask students to apply the strategy independently as a summative task. Students will select words from the banks or shared recording sheet, identify the base and affix, and explain the whole meaning. Their sentences and visuals will show whether they understand the words in use. Additional examples with the same affix will show whether they can transfer the pattern.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-125\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-126\"><p>I will model the language for explaining a base and affix and demonstrate a sentence using the whole word. The definition chart will provide a visual reference, and I will introduce key words in the bank before students begin. Their written definitions, pictures, and additional examples will then give them several ways to communicate understanding and practice academic vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-127\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student, based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-128\"><p>Charts, step-by-step models, and guided examples will support access to the task. For the focus student, I will preteach vocabulary and check in during independent work. I will also invite connections with familiar words from home and daily life. These supports are intended to help him use his existing language knowledge as he analyzes and explains new words.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-129\"><p>Lesson 4 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-130\"><p>Third grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-131\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-132\"><p>Using affixes to understand words within a passage.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-133\"><p>Strands/Standards and Learning Goals Grade-level CA ELA/Literacy10 strand(s)/standard(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-134\"><p>CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.R.F.3.3.A: Identify and know the meaning of most common prefixes and derivational suffixes CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RF.3.4.A Read grade-level text with purpose and understanding. CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.RL.3.1 Ask and answer questions to demonstrate understanding of a text, referring explicitly to the text as the basis for the answers CCSS.ELA-LITERACY.SL.3.1 Engage effectively in a range of collaborative discussions (one-on-one, in groups, and teacher-led) with diverse partners on grade 3 topics and texts, building on others&#x27; ideas and expressing their own clearly.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-135\"><p>ELA/Literacy learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-136\"><p>read a grade-level passage with fluency, explain affixed words in context, and support comprehension answers with evidence from the text.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-137\"><p>ELD strand(s)/standard(s)11</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-138\"><p>Part I: Interacting in Meaningful Ways (C. Productive): Express information and ideas in writing using evidence from texts and academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-139\"><p>ELD learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-140\"><p>use context and word parts to explain vocabulary and communicate those explanations orally and in writing with complete sentences and appropriate academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-141\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-142\"><p>The focus student and other students showed understanding on the summative word-study task, so I planned an extension that applies the same skills in a story. A Day at the Park includes family interactions, sports, and a park visit. These familiar situations allow students to connect new words with their own experiences while taking on a more demanding reading task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-143\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-144\"><p>We will read A Day at the Park together. I will model fluent reading and pause to explain my thinking about affixed words. Students will return to the passage to mark examples such as prepacked, skillful, useless, recheck, and disorganized. We will then discuss the words and answer comprehension questions using both their parts and clues from the story. This extends the earlier word study into reading for meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-145\"><p>Describe how you plan to use a direct and explicit approach for teaching the selected foundational reading skill(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-146\"><p>I will first model fluent reading as students follow the passage. Then I will guide them to mark prefixes and suffixes and examine how those parts affect meaning. During the shared comprehension work, individual students will explain their reasoning. I will prompt them to refer to both the word structure and the surrounding text. This keeps the strategy explicit while giving students practice using it in context.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-147\"><p>Summarize formative and/or summative assessment(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-148\"><p>I will watch how accurately students mark affixes and listen as they explain the words and answer comprehension questions. Their responses will show whether they can connect the affix, the base, and contextual clues. I will also attend to their oral language and use of vocabulary. These observations will guide clarification and further practice.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-149\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-150\"><p>I will model the academic language of word analysis during reading and discussion. Marking affixes will provide a visual reference. Students will explain meanings in complete sentences and point to supporting clues in the passage. Guided conversation will combine listening, speaking, and reading practice while supporting English learners&#x27; access to the text.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-151\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student, based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-152\"><p>The familiar park setting will give students background knowledge to draw on. The focus student will receive clear steps, repeated models, and visible markings of prefixes and suffixes. I will use prompts and sentence frames when he needs help connecting context to meaning. These adaptations support word recognition and language while keeping the reading task appropriately challenging.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-153\"><p>Template notes</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-154\"><p>1 Recent observations, assessments (e.g., screenings; formative, summative, state testing), literacy and/or language profiles, student work samples, and/or information provided by your cooperating teacher and/or supervising faculty</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-155\"><p>2 Foundational reading skills: print concepts, including letters of the alphabet; phonological awareness, including phonemic awareness; phonics, spelling, and word recognition; decoding and encoding; morphology/morphological awareness; text reading fluency, including accuracy, prosody [expression], and rate [an indicator of automaticity]. For candidates in TK\u20133 settings, foundational reading skills also include creating literacy environments that are print rich and that foster interest in print; and games, books, poetry, oral storytelling, and songs that draw attention to print, the manipulation of sounds, and alphabet letters.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-156\"><p>3 Additional themes: meaning making, effective expression, language development, content knowledge</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-157\"><p>4 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-158\"><p>5 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-159\"><p>6 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-160\"><p>7 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-161\"><p>8 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-162\"><p>9 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-163\"><p>10 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-164\"><p>11 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF): Language and Literacy Development.</p></div>"}, {"id": "C", "title": "Part C Planning Narrative", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-165\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014 Part C: Written Narrative: Description of Assessments Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-166\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 5 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 14\u201315 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-167\"><p>Formative Assessment</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-168\"><p>\u2022 Describe one formative assessment you plan to implement during the learning segment (e.g., checks for understanding, exit ticket, self-assessment).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-169\"><p>In Lesson 2, I will use the whole-class word discussion as a formative check. Students will propose words with pre-, re-, -ful, and -less and explain the effect of each affix. I will ask about the affix&#x27;s meaning, the base&#x27;s meaning, and how they fit together. Their oral explanations will show whether they understand the structure and can use the vocabulary. I can then clarify misconceptions and revisit difficult terms before students begin independent work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-170\"><p>\u2022 Describe what you expect the students to know and be able to demonstrate in their responses to the selected formative assessment and how you plan to monitor student understanding of the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals during the learning segment.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-171\"><p>I expect students to identify the four target affixes, explain them, and use the resulting words accurately in spoken sentences. As students share, I will listen for correct analysis and clear language, ask follow-up questions, and respond to errors. These checks address the literacy goal of word recognition through morphology and the ELD goal of explaining ideas with academic vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-172\"><p>\u2022 If you chose to use student self-assessment, explain how the student self-assessment provides opportunities for the students to build an awareness of what they have learned, what they need to learn, and what they need additional support within relation to making progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-173\"><p>Not applicable.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-174\"><p>Summative Assessment</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-175\"><p>\u2022 Describe the summative assessment you plan to implement during the final lesson of your learning segment.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-176\"><p>The summative task takes place in Lesson 3, before the extension. Students will independently complete word study using banks containing pre-, re-, dis-, un-, -ing, -less, -ful, and -able. They will identify the base and affix, explain the whole word, use it in a complete sentence, draw its meaning, and produce other words with the same affix. This work will show their application of word analysis and language skills.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-177\"><p>\u2022 What ELA/Literacy and ELD goals are evaluated by the summative assessment (e.g., knowledge, skills, or abilities your students will demonstrate)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-178\"><p>The assessment checks whether students can identify bases and affixes, combine their meanings, and use the resulting words in sentences, visuals, and further examples. These tasks address the listed RF.3.3.A and L.3.4.A\u2013C goals for word analysis and vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-179\"><p>For language development, I will examine how students explain meaning and use vocabulary in context. Their oral and written explanations connect word structure with academic language and the collaborative and interpretive language goals identified in this assessment description.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-180\"><p>\u2022 What are you planning to collect or observe from each student as an assessment response (product, process, or performance)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-181\"><p>I will collect each completed word-study sheet, including its analysis, definition, sentence, visual, and additional examples. I will also observe the strategies students use while working. The product and process together will provide evidence of their word-recognition skills and ability to communicate meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-182\"><p>\u2022 What evidence will you look for in the students\u2019 responses to indicate that the students are progressing toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-183\"><p>I will look for accurate identification of parts and an explanation that connects the affix with the base. Sentences, pictures, and further examples should agree with the word&#x27;s meaning. Clear vocabulary in written definitions and sentences will also indicate progress in academic language. These features will show how well students connect decoding, understanding, and expression.</p></div>"}, {"id": "F", "title": "Part F Teaching Commentary", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-184\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 2: Teach and Assess\u2014Part F: Commentary Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-185\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt that relates to each video clip that you will submit (up to 8 pages). You must address each prompt at least once across all the video(s) submitted. Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 22\u201323 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-186\"><p>In the commentary for your video clip(s), address the following prompts:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-187\"><p>\u2022 What direct and explicit approach did you use to teach the selected foundational reading skill(s), actively engage students, and support their progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals? Why did the selected approach support the literacy and language development of your students? answered in video clip 1</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-188\"><p>\u2022 Describe the selected additional theme(s) you are teaching in the video clip(s). Explain why the selected theme(s) actively engage students and support their progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals. answered in video clip 2</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-189\"><p>\u2022 Describe the integrated ELD you used in the video clip(s). Explain why this instruction supported students in progressing toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals. answered in video clip 1</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-190\"><p>\u2022 Describe the strategy(ies) you used to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests. answered in video clip 2</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-191\"><p>\u2022 How did you use assessment(s) to monitor student progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goal(s) in the video clip(s)? answered in video clip 3</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-192\"><p>\u2022 Given what you learned from the assessment(s), explain adjustments you made to your instruction and how they supported students\u2019 literacy and language development. answered in video clip 4</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-193\"><p>\u2022 What specific, actionable feedback related to literacy did you provide to students about what they did well and/or their misconceptions/gaps in knowledge? Why was this feedback able to support students\u2019 literacy and language development? answered in video clip 3 o Why did the strategy(ies) support your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD learning goals? answered in video clip 3</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-194\"><p>\u2022 Video Clip 1: Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video. What direct and explicit approach did you use to teach the selected foundational reading skill(s), actively engage students, and support their progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals? Why did the selected approach support the literacy and language development of your students?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-195\"><p>Video Clip 1 shows the modeling and guided practice from Lesson 1. I used the gradual-release sequence to make word analysis explicit before students tried it themselves. At 00:03\u201300:24, I explained dis-, tract, and -ion while building a word and thinking aloud about how the parts contribute to meaning. The affix chart gave students a stable visual reference. At 00:25\u20131:05, think-pair-share allowed them to propose tract words and rehearse explanations with a peer.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-196\"><p>At 01:37\u201302:21 and 2:39\u20132:58, the student in pink built subtracting. I guided her through its prefix, root, and suffix and connected the parts to the whole meaning. When she could not recall sub- at 02:22\u201302:39, I directed her to the chart and acknowledged her successful use of it. At 03:00\u201303:44, the student in black built distracting and worked through its parts with me. At 03:45\u201304:24, we compared distract and distracting; her sentence also gave me a chance to address verb tense and the form distracted.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-197\"><p>Modeling, hands-on construction, questions, and immediate feedback linked word recognition with meaningful language use. Students could see the process, discuss it, and practice explaining it with support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-198\"><p>\u2022 Describe the integrated ELD you used in the video clip(s). Explain why this instruction supported students in progressing toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-199\"><p>In Clip 1, I paired explicit vocabulary with a visual chart and repeated oral practice. At 00:03\u201300:24, my think-aloud connected the names and meanings of word parts with the word being built. Think-pair-share at 00:25\u20131:05 gave students time to rehearse possible words before sharing. During the pocket-chart work at 01:37\u20134:24, I prompted explanations and sentence use and responded to errors. These routines developed decoding and vocabulary while giving English learners structured practice listening and speaking about the content.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-200\"><p>Video Clip 2 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video. Describe the selected additional theme(s) you are teaching in the video clip(s). Explain why the selected theme(s) actively engage students and support their progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-201\"><p>Language development was the additional theme in Lesson 2. Students generated examples, explained how an affix changed a base, and used the word in a sentence. Listening to peers and refining their own explanations made vocabulary work an oral-language task as well as a word-recognition task.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-202\"><p>In Clip 2 at 00:00\u201300:07, we read the definition of -less together. At 00:08\u201300:31, a student contributed homeless and identified the suffix and its meaning. At 00:42\u201301:29, another explained fearless and used her father&#x27;s willingness to enter the dark as an example. At 01:32\u201302:02, a student explained hairless and distinguished the whole word from hair. Other contributions included numberless at 02:44\u201303:11 and brainless at 3:36\u20133:49, with a scarecrow as the example. At 04:37\u201305:02, we moved to -ful; a student offered worthful and explained it through the meaning full of worth.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-203\"><p>Students recorded examples and used the chart for spelling. Their contributions connected word structure with familiar situations, and my questions helped them make their explanations more precise. This supported vocabulary, word recognition, and academic speaking.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-204\"><p>\u2022 Describe the strategy(ies) you used to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-205\"><p>I invited students to supply words and sentences from their own knowledge and experiences. Their -less and -ful examples gave us familiar contexts for discussing an abstract feature of language. Students thought aloud, listened to classmates, and adjusted their explanations. Partner work also let them draw on one another&#x27;s language strengths. These choices supported participation while giving English learners repeated opportunities to explain vocabulary in context.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-206\"><p>\u2022 How did you use assessment(s) to monitor student progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goal(s) in the video clip(s)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-207\"><p>During the Lesson 2 discussion, I asked students to identify an affix, explain the base, and use the whole word in a sentence. Their answers showed both word-analysis knowledge and the language they could use to express it. I then monitored the guided worksheets. Students defined words from the banks and completed a fuller analysis that included their own definition, a sentence, and a synonym and antonym. As I circulated, I checked affix identification, spelling, and meaning and provided help where needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-208\"><p>Video Clip 3 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video. How did you use assessment(s) to monitor student progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goal(s) in the video clip(s)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-209\"><p>Clip 3 shows students completing the Lesson 3 word-study task. I circulated, clarified directions, and encouraged use of the chart and completion of all parts. The clip also records supported exchanges that are relevant when interpreting the work.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-210\"><p>At 00:07\u201301:03, the student in blue explained disappear as something being gone. I asked him to consider appear by itself, acknowledged his explanation, and referred him to the chart for spelling and meaning. At 01:30\u201302:35, I helped the student in black analyze penniless by asking about penny and -less and reminding him where a suffix occurs. At 02:48\u201303:05, the student in blue generated disagree as another dis- word. At 03:58\u201304:35, a student explained colorful by distinguishing color from the complete word, and I reminded her to check spelling.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-211\"><p>I used these conversations and the written sheets to examine students&#x27; identification of parts, explanations, sentences, pictures, and additional examples. The evidence showed their developing understanding and where prompting was still useful; it also informed the next instruction.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-212\"><p>\u2022 What specific, actionable feedback related to literacy did you provide to students about what they did well and/or their misconceptions/gaps in knowledge? Why was this feedback able to support students\u2019 literacy and language development?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-213\"><p>My feedback focused on the particular part of the analysis each student needed to revisit. At 00:07\u201301:03, I asked the student working with disappear to consider appear separately and then connect dis- to the changed meaning. At 01:30\u201302:35, I used questions and the chart to help the student working with penniless identify the base and suffix. At 03:58\u201304:35, I checked the explanation of colorful and prompted attention to spelling.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-214\"><p>These exchanges acknowledged what students understood while giving them a specific next action. Revisiting the parts and then the whole helped clarify vocabulary, spelling, and sentence use and gave students practice explaining the process aloud.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-215\"><p>o Why did the strategy(ies) support your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD learning goals?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-216\"><p>Students had opportunities to attempt the analysis and then receive focused help where their explanations broke down. The chart and earlier discussions gave them resources to revisit, and my questions directed attention to the relationship between a base and its affix. This supported decoding and vocabulary while giving English learners meaningful practice with academic language in speaking and writing. The combination helped students move toward more independent application.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-217\"><p>Video Clip 4 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video. Given what you learned from the assessment(s), explain adjustments you made to your instruction and how they supported students\u2019 literacy and language development.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-218\"><p>Clip 4 returns to Lesson 2, when students were working in pairs. I noticed that several did not understand the lower portion of the sheet: writing a definition and sentence and supplying a synonym and antonym. I brought the class to the rug and modeled the task with delightful.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-219\"><p>At 00:28\u201303:08, students helped read the model definition and sentence and offered other -ful words, synonyms, and antonyms. I confirmed accurate contributions, clarified meanings, and restated the steps when needed. This adjustment gave students a concrete example to follow when they returned to their paired work. It also connected the word-analysis task with the language needed to explain and use the vocabulary.</p></div>"}, {"id": "H", "title": "Part H Summative Assessment Feedback", "html": "<h3>Part H: Focus Student\u2019s</h3><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-220\"><p>Summative Assessment Actionable Feedback</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-221\"><p>Directions: You will document the specific, actionable feedback you provided to the FS through:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-222\"><p>\u25cf a written summary of the feedback you provided to the FS</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-223\"><p>After reviewing the focus student&#x27;s Lesson 3 prefix and suffix sheets, I provided individual feedback on both successful work and the next step. I linked the feedback to identifying word parts and explaining meaning with academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-224\"><p>For word selection and identification, I recognized the student&#x27;s appropriate choice of repaint and accurate identification of paint and re-. The student understood re- as again and connected that meaning to the whole word. I explained how this strategy can help with unfamiliar vocabulary. The definition met the rubric&#x27;s Excellent criterion because it accounted for both the prefix and the complete word. I encouraged the student to make that connection explicit in future explanations.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-225\"><p>The sentence, \u201cI had to repaint my wall cause it was dirty,\u201d conveyed the meaning of repaint. I acknowledged that success and asked the student to replace cause with because to make the written language more precise. The detailed picture of repainting a wall also supported the definition and met the highest visual criterion. I explained that a clear picture can help communicate and check a word&#x27;s meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-226\"><p>On the suffix sheet, the student identified color and -ful and connected full of with the meaning of colorful. I recognized the accurate sentence, \u201cThe rainbow was colorful,\u201d and the matching rainbow illustration. Tearful, careful, and fearful provided further evidence that the student could apply the same suffix to other bases.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-227\"><p>By addressing each component, I made the feedback manageable and specific. The student could see which strategies were working and had a clear language revision to make. This supported word analysis and written explanation while maintaining confidence.</p></div>"}, {"id": "I", "title": "Part I Assessment Reflection", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-228\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 3: Reflect\u2014Part I: Written Narrative: Reflection and Analysis of</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-229\"><p>Summative Assessment Results Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-230\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 4 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. Be sure to include specific references to the results to support your reflections: What in the results led you to your conclusions? For more information, see pages 32\u201333 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-231\"><p>Analysis and Reflection for the Group</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-232\"><p>\u2022 I dentify and describe understandings, gaps in knowledge, and/or misconceptions related to the students\u2019 literacy and language development.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-233\"><p>The completed prefix and suffix tasks showed strong overall understanding. Most students identified bases and affixes accurately, and many connected the parts in a complete definition, used the words appropriately, and generated further examples. A few confused similar affixes or made spelling errors, but these were limited rather than a general pattern of misunderstanding. Most students earned high rubric ratings across the components, supporting my conclusion that they had met the learning goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-234\"><p>\u2022 Describe what was effective about your instructional approach(es) and student activity(ies) for the group.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-235\"><p>The sequence of modeling, supported practice, and application helped the group. In Lesson 1, the pocket chart, tract examples, and affix chart made the analysis visible. Flashcard groups then gave students a way to build and record words themselves. Lesson 2 extended the vocabulary through a shared chart and discussion before partners completed definitions and fuller word analyses.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-236\"><p>Lesson 3 brought the components together in individual word-study work. Students identified parts, defined words, wrote sentences, drew representations, and generated examples. My check-ins and feedback helped address confusion during the task. Across the lessons, visuals, oral rehearsal, peer work, and explicit demonstrations connected word structure with meaning and academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-237\"><p>\u2022 D escribe instructional changes you would make that could have better supported the group if you taught this learning segment again.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-238\"><p>I would add more sentence use to the Lesson 1 group activity so students did more than record the words they built. I would also include additional small-group discussion during guided practice, giving students time to compare meanings and explain their reasoning before working individually. For Lesson 2, I would model the full worksheet more clearly at the outset, especially definitions, sentences, synonyms, and antonyms. These changes would improve oral rehearsal and make the written expectations clearer.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-239\"><p>Analysis and Reflection for the Focus Student</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-240\"><p>\u2022 Identify and describe understandings, gaps in knowledge, and/or misconceptions related to the focus student\u2019s literacy and language development.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-241\"><p>The focus student&#x27;s continuing needs include unfamiliar multisyllabic words, consistent analysis of affixes, and accurate academic vocabulary in speaking and writing. He sometimes applies an affix meaning too broadly or confuses similar parts. He also needs practice organizing complete sentences and connected paragraphs and using new vocabulary with appropriate grammar. Visuals, sentence frames, think-alouds, and guided discussion help him apply the strategies and explain his thinking more clearly.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-242\"><p>\u2022 How will the specific, actionable feedback you provided the focus student help them to improve or advance their understanding to continue their progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-243\"><p>I recognized his successful word choices, identification of bases and affixes, definitions, examples, sentences, and pictures. I then identified a manageable next step: refining written academic language, including using because in place of cause. I also encouraged him to make the connection between the affix and base explicit in explanations. This feedback tells him what to keep doing and what to revise in his next response.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-244\"><p>\u2022 Given your knowledge of the focus student, describe what was effective about your instructional approach(es) and student activity(ies). Refer to the focus student\u2019s summative assessment response.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-245\"><p>Explicit models and a gradual move from guided to individual practice suited his need for a clear process. Charts, frames, and think-alouds helped him explain the relationship among word parts. Partner and small-group talk gave him additional rehearsal.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-246\"><p>His summative work correctly identified bases and affixes and included definitions, sentences, and pictures that showed understanding. Feedback on because addressed the precision of his written language. Together, these features suggest that structured word analysis and language support helped him apply vocabulary knowledge while identifying a next step in expression.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-247\"><p>\u2022 Describe instructional changes you would make for the focus student if you taught this learning segment again. Refer to the focus student\u2019s summative assessment response.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-248\"><p>I would provide more oral rehearsal with sentence frames before he writes, especially when an explanation requires precise academic language. His assessment showed good understanding of word parts but room to improve sentence expression. I would also offer more complex affixes as an extension and structure partner or small-group work to include timely feedback. This would build on his success while helping him carry word-study skills into comprehension and writing.</p></div>"}, {"id": "J", "title": "Part J Follow Up Activity", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-249\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 4: Apply\u2014Part J: Written Narrative: Re-Teaching or Extension Activity Description Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-250\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 5 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 37\u201338 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-251\"><p>\u2022 Activity (p lace an \u201cX\u201d in the appropriate bracket):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-252\"><p>] re-teaching activity OR [x] extension activity</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-253\"><p>\u2022 Provide the developmentally appropriate ELA/Literacy learning goal(s).1</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-254\"><p>Students will use prefixes, suffixes, and context to explain vocabulary in grade-level text and apply that analysis during reading and discussion.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-255\"><p>\u2022 Provide the developmentally appropriate ELD learning goal(s).2 The follow-up activity must include an ELD learning goal even if your focus student/group of students does not include an English learner.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-256\"><p>Students will explain affixed words in complete sentences, support meanings with contextual clues, and use academic vocabulary in discussion with peers.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-257\"><p>\u2022 Summarize your activity and explain how you will check for understanding.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-258\"><p>I will model fluent reading of A Day at the Park, including pacing, expression, and attention to affixed words. Students will then reread independently and mark examples such as prepacked, skillful, useless, recheck, and disorganized. We will discuss their selections and answer comprehension questions together.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-259\"><p>I will ask students to connect an affix&#x27;s meaning with evidence from the story, such as the role of pre- in prepacked or clues that explain disorganized. I will check their markings, listen to explanations, and review comprehension answers. These checks will show whether students can use word parts and context together and where clarification is needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-260\"><p>\u2022 For this activity, describe how you will leverage the focus student\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014assets (cultural and/or linguistic ) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-261\"><p>The focus student&#x27;s Spanish knowledge offers a way to connect English word parts with familiar language patterns. The story&#x27;s soccer and park experiences also connect with his interests and everyday life. I will invite him and his peers to use those connections when discussing vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-262\"><p>After hearing a fluent model, students will reread, mark affixes, and explain their selections. Shared comprehension work will ask them to refer to the text and rehearse academic language with peers. The combination of familiar content, modeling, independent reading, and discussion gives students several ways to apply their knowledge and communicate meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-263\"><p>\u2022 Determine adaptations to address the focus student\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014learning needs (e.g., accommodations and/or modifications, language supports, IEP supports/services).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-264\"><p>I chose a familiar setting to make the passage accessible while keeping the vocabulary work challenging. The focus student will receive clear steps, repeated models, and visual cues from marked word parts. During discussion, guided questions and sentence frames will help him connect context with meaning and express a complete explanation. These supports address his need for structure and language rehearsal while extending his word-recognition skills.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-265\"><p>\u2022 Explain why this activity supports the focus student\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014literacy progress. Refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 to support your instructional decision making.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-266\"><p>I selected five students, including the focus student, whose Lesson 3 word-study work showed that they were ready for an extension. A Day at the Park asks them to transfer the analysis from isolated words into a passage. They must use affixes, consider context, and explain their reasoning with evidence. Working with this small group allows me to provide focused questions and feedback as students take that next step in comprehension and academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-267\"><p>\u2022 Provide an analysis of the recent literacy assessments3 used in Step 1 and the formative and summative assessments used in Steps 2 and 3 to support your instructional decision making.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-268\"><p>The Step 1 evidence showed that students often recognized an affix but did not consistently explain its contribution to the whole word. Observations, earlier assessments, and work samples therefore led me to emphasize modeling and supported explanation.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-269\"><p>During Step 2, flashcard word building, pocket-chart work, recording sheets, and think-pair-share gave me frequent checks on students&#x27; analysis. When a student could not recall sub-, I directed attention to the chart. In Lesson 2, paired worksheets revealed difficulty with full definitions, synonyms, and antonyms, so I paused for another model, as shown in Clip 4.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-270\"><p>The Step 3 word-study products showed that most students could identify parts, explain meanings, and apply words in sentences, visuals, and further examples. The focus student also demonstrated these understandings while needing more precise written language. This evidence supported an extension for students ready to apply morphology in connected text. Lesson 4 therefore retained language support but added contextual clues and text-based explanations, linking assessment findings with the next instructional step.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-271\"><p>Template notes</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-272\"><p>1 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF).</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-273\"><p>2 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF).</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-274\"><p>3 For example, screenings; formative, summative, state testing; literacy and/or language profiles; student work samples; observations; information provided by your cooperating teacher and/or supervising faculty</p></div>"}, {"id": "L", "title": "Part L Follow Up Commentary", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-275\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 4: Apply\u2014Part L: Commentary Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-276\"><p>Directions: Type your response within the brackets provided (up to 2 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 38\u201339 of the Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-277\"><p>In your commentary, address one of the following prompts:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-278\"><p>For a re-teaching activity:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-279\"><p>\u2022 Identify the targeted intervention and/or individualized strategy being used. Explain why this supports your focus student\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student(s) \u2014in making progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals. OR</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-280\"><p>For an extension activity:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-281\"><p>\u2022 Identify the instruction provided that deepened or advanced your focus student\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014learning. Explain why this activity supports your focus student\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student(s) \u2014 to take the next step in learning related to the ELA/Literacy and ELD goals. Video Clip: Address the commentary prompt that relates to this video. Describe what is happening and provide an explanation for what you are doing and why.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-282\"><p>The clip shows the small-group extension for students whose word-study work demonstrated strong understanding of bases and affixes. They had already practiced re- and dis- in guided and individual tasks. Here, the next step was to use that knowledge with contextual evidence while reading.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-283\"><p>At 00:00, the student in blue reads Question 4 about Noah rechecking the hallway. I ask him to explain re- and return to the story for evidence. Questions about Noah&#x27;s action and what he is looking for help him connect checking again with the search for his sister&#x27;s shoes.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-284\"><p>At 01:10, the student in red reads Question 5 about disorganized. I direct him to locate the word and reread its sentence. His first explanation is broad, so I prompt him to consider dis- and the surrounding clues. I record the response on the chart and model a more precise explanation with a sentence frame.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-285\"><p>The activity extends the earlier work because identifying an affix is only the first step. Students must explain its effect in a particular story and support their interpretation with evidence. Guided questions and complete-sentence practice connect vocabulary and comprehension with academic language and prepare students to use the strategy with less support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-286\"><p>Retained Source Materials</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-literacy-287\"><p>The following source pages retain the instructional materials, assessment tools, and student work as supplied.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-literacy-image1.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 3 roots and affixes: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-literacy-image1.png\" alt=\"Grade 3 roots and affixes: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-literacy-image2.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 3 roots and affixes: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-literacy-image2.png\" alt=\"Grade 3 roots and affixes: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-literacy-image3.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 3 roots and affixes: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-literacy-image3.png\" alt=\"Grade 3 roots and affixes: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-literacy-image4.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 3 roots and affixes: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-literacy-image4.png\" alt=\"Grade 3 roots and affixes: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure>"}], "images": ["assets/examples/ms-literacy-image1.png", "assets/examples/ms-literacy-image2.png", "assets/examples/ms-literacy-image3.png", "assets/examples/ms-literacy-image4.png"], "responseCount": 100, "words": 9729, "historical": false}, "ms-math": {"id": "ms-math", "path": "ms-math", "title": "Grade 2 measurement", "file": "04_MS_Grade_2_Math_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 MS MPA (2nd Grade) Sample Submission.docx", "parts": [{"id": "A", "title": "Part A Contextual Information", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-1\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014Part A: Written Narrative: Contextual Information Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-math-2\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 7 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-3\"><p>Contextual Information About the Group of Students</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-math-4\"><p>Directions: Provide contextual information for the group of students you plan to teach during this math lesson. For more information, see pages 8\u20139 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-5\"><p>\u2022 Age range(s) and grade level(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-6\"><p>Second graders, ages seven and eight.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-7\"><p>\u2022 Student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to the math content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-8\"><p>Students participate in a dual-language program and use both Spanish and English to work through ideas. That flexibility gives them several ways to explain their mathematics. They also enjoy collaboration and hands-on tasks, especially opportunities to test an idea with classmates.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-9\"><p>\u2022 Math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-10\"><p>The class has been working on place value, addition and subtraction within 100, and explaining a strategy with evidence. Students recognize that a problem can be approached in more than one way. They choose methods they understand, use drawings to represent the work, and are beginning to include more mathematical vocabulary in their explanations.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-11\"><p>\u2022 Identified English learners (see SB 210 for Deaf and Hard of Hearing students)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-12\"><p>Fourteen students are identified as English learners: nine girls and five boys, all ages seven or eight and Spanish-speaking. Overall ELPAC results were level 4 for eight students, level 3 for five, and level 1 for one.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-13\"><p>\u2022 Identified learning needs (e.g., how many students with an IEP, a 504 plan, MTSS)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-14\"><p>No student currently has an IEP or 504 plan. SST meetings have taken place or are scheduled for some students, so additional supports may be considered.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-15\"><p>\u2022 Language(s) of instruction (e.g., biliteracy, developmental bilingual [one-way immersion], dual language [two-way immersion], English only) to be used in the lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-16\"><p>I taught this lesson in English.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-17\"><p>Focus Students</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-math-18\"><p>Directions: Provide contextual information for the three focus students you selected. For more information, see pages 9\u201310 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. Focus Student 1 (FS1)</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-19\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-20\"><p>FS1 is seven and in second grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-21\"><p>\u2022 Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-22\"><p>FS1 is an emerging bilingual learner who asks questions about words across languages and uses what she knows to help peers. She enjoys working with a partner and using movement or gestures to communicate and remember ideas. Those interests give her useful ways to enter a mathematical discussion.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-23\"><p>\u2022 If FS1 is an English learner, current proficiency in English and proficiency in their primary language</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-24\"><p>FS1 is Hispanic and has lived in the United States since birth. She has taken the ELPAC four times since entering school in 2022. Her 2024\u20132025 overall result was 4; the reported domain results were 2 in listening and writing and 3 in speaking and reading. Her Spanish reading level moved from 12 to 14 during the year, with level 14 described as the end of first grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-25\"><p>\u2022 Math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-26\"><p>FS1 can add and subtract, count accurately, and draw representations that help her solve and check equations. She understands ones, tens, and hundreds. With appropriate support, she can translate most word problems into equations and drawings that show her thinking.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-27\"><p>\u2022 Learning needs related to their academic language development (ALD)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-28\"><p>FS1 needs support with the language of word problems. Vocabulary previews, sentence frames, and visuals will help her focus on the mathematical situation. Connecting terms with Spanish will also let her draw on familiar language while noticing how the two languages express the same idea.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-29\"><p>Focus Student 2 (FS2)</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-30\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-31\"><p>FS2 is seven and in second grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-32\"><p>\u2022 Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-33\"><p>FS2 uses both languages to make connections and is curious about how schoolwork relates to everyday life. She often considers how a real situation could become a math problem or how a problem might occur outside class.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-34\"><p>\u2022 Math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-35\"><p>FS2 understands basic addition and subtraction and checks her work carefully. When a detail no longer fits her reasoning, she revises it. She also explains her steps thoroughly to others and asks questions to make sure they understand.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-36\"><p>\u2022 Learning needs related to mathematical ability</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-37\"><p>FS2 needs a more deliberate start when approaching word problems. A consistent process for rereading, identifying the task, and planning steps will help her slow down before calculating. That structure should help her organize the numbers around the meaning of the problem.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-38\"><p>Focus Student 3 (FS3)</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-39\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-40\"><p>FS3 is seven and in second grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-41\"><p>\u2022 Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-42\"><p>FS3 is a bridging bilingual learner who uses both languages to clarify ideas for peers. He connects mathematics to daily life and likes drawing detailed representations. His visuals help him and his classmates see the situation behind a problem.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-43\"><p>\u2022 Math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-44\"><p>FS3 reasons confidently, notices patterns, and uses strategies such as decomposing numbers and choosing friendly numbers. He checks whether an answer makes sense and explains his process in detail. He can clarify questions and connect ideas across content areas.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-45\"><p>\u2022 Learning needs related to life experiences that may result in a need for additional adaptation(s) to support FS3\u2019s well-being and/or behavior during the math lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-46\"><p>FS3 needs meaningful challenge to sustain engagement. I will connect problems to familiar situations and offer tasks that ask him to extend his reasoning. Opportunities for movement and purposeful breaks will also help him stay ready to participate.</p></div>"}, {"id": "B", "title": "Part B Lesson Planning", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-47\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014Part B: Sample Math Lesson Plan Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-math-48\"><p>Directions: If you do not have access to a school or district lesson plan format, consider using this sample math lesson plan template. Use the knowledge gathered about your students to establish grade-level appropriate math-specific learning goals: one goal that supports math content and practice and one goal that supports math ALD. Based on those learning goals, plan one asset-based, UDL-focused math lesson. Provide answers to the following prompts by typing your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 10 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 10\u201313 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. Title of lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-49\"><p>Estimating and Measuring in Centimeters</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-50\"><p>Subject</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-51\"><p>Mathematics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-52\"><p>Grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-53\"><p>Second grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-54\"><p>1. What are your goals for the math lesson? a. Select Math Strand/Standard and Math Practice: Based on the students\u2019 prior learning, recent information you reviewed, student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests, and learning needs: i. For TK: From the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations, select:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-55\"><p>\u2022 one age-level developmental strand:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-56\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-57\"><p>\u2022 one Mathematical Practice (MP):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-58\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-59\"><p>ii. For K\u201312: From the California Common Core State Standards Math, select:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-60\"><p>\u2022 one grade-level Mathematical Content Standard:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-61\"><p>CCSS.MATH.2.MD.A.1 (Measure the length of an object by selecting and using appropriate tools such as rulers, yardsticks, meter sticks, and measuring tapes) and CCSS.MATH.2.MD.A.3 (Estimate lengths using units of inches, feet, centimeters, and meters)</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-62\"><p>\u2022 one Mathematical Practice (MP):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-63\"><p>MP5 (Use appropriate tools strategically)</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-64\"><p>b. Develop a Math Content and Practice Learning Goal: Based on the age-/grade-level strand/standard and math practice you selected, write one developmentally appropriate math learning goal that includes both math content and practice (see examples on pages 12\u201313 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-65\"><p>Students will estimate and measure classroom objects in centimeters, using a ruler or base-ten materials strategically. This work addresses 2.MD.A.1, 2.MD.A.3, and MP5.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-66\"><p>2. Develop a Math ALD Learning Goal: Based on the relevant math academic vocabulary needed for the lesson, create a math academic language development goal that supports the math content and practice learning goal (see examples on page 13 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-67\"><p>Students will explain an estimate or measurement in simple sentences, using terms such as estimate, measure, length, centimeter, and ruler during the activities.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-68\"><p>3. What will the students do in the math lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-69\"><p>I will begin with a ten-minute Which One Doesn&#x27;t Belong warm-up showing four images of measured shapes. Students will think quietly, discuss a choice with a partner, and explain it to the class. We will consider reasons for each image rather than look for a single acceptable answer.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-70\"><p>In the first activity, students will estimate lengths in centimeters. They will draw on their knowledge that a ten rod is 10 centimeters and use it as a reference for judging whether an estimate is too short, too long, or about right. In the second activity, they will measure classroom objects and compare the results with their estimates. Discussion and the recorded work will make their reasoning visible.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-71\"><p>a. What instructional materials and/or resources will you use in the math lesson? Why?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-72\"><p>Students will use 30-centimeter rulers, 10-centimeter rods, pencils, worksheets, and classroom objects. The rods provide a familiar reference as students move toward a standard measuring tool. The worksheets organize the estimates and measurements, while real objects give students something concrete to compare and measure.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-73\"><p>4. What UDL-focused instructional strategies will you use during the math lesson to support student learning? Describe how you will: a. Connect the content of this lesson to students\u2019 recent math learning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-74\"><p>Students already know that a unit cube measures one centimeter and a ten rod measures ten. I will connect those familiar base-ten materials to a ruler and reinforce centimeters as a standard unit. The estimating work will also extend their earlier practice judging whether a quantity is reasonable.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-75\"><p>b. Create a safe, positive learning environment.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-76\"><p>I will value different explanations during the open-ended warm-up and make clear that each image can be justified. During measurement work, I will describe an estimate as a reasoned guess that may need revision. This should help students share ideas without treating an initial estimate as a failure.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-77\"><p>c. Leverage your students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-78\"><p>Planned partner talk will let students use the language that best helps them reason, with support for making connections across Spanish and English. Measuring real objects will draw on their interest in trying things themselves. Collaboration will give them opportunities to compare approaches and explain what they notice.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-79\"><p>d. Support the math ALD of your students.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-80\"><p>I will model measurement vocabulary accurately, ask students what the terms mean, and connect the words with the tools and actions. Sentence frames will help students organize an explanation while still supplying their own mathematical thinking.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-81\"><p>e. Ask questions to engage students in higher-order thinking to deepen their understanding of math content.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-82\"><p>I will ask students to justify why an estimate is high, low, or reasonable and reconsider it after using a familiar reference. We will also examine what happens when tools differ or an object is not aligned at zero. These questions ask students to evaluate the measurement process as well as the answer.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-83\"><p>f. Monitor the students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math content and practice and math ALD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-84\"><p>I will look for complete estimates, appropriate units, and correct alignment of the object and tool. Students still developing the skill may omit units, estimate incompletely, or measure inaccurately. Meeting the goal means applying the measurement process correctly; stronger evidence includes revising an estimate and explaining the comparison that prompted the change.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-85\"><p>For language, I will distinguish vague statements from explanations that use the vocabulary and frames meaningfully. More developed responses will connect an estimate with a reference\u2014for example, recognizing that an object is longer than two ten rods\u2014and explain why the estimate changed.</p></div>"}, {"id": "C", "title": "Part C Planning Narrative", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-86\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014Part C: Written Narrative: Math Lesson Adaptation(s) for Focus Students Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-math-87\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 7 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see page 14 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-88\"><p>\u2022 Adaptation(s): Determine what adaptation(s) you will make to support Focus Student 1 (FS1), Focus Student 2 (FS2), and Focus Student 3 (FS3) to access and engage with the content of the math lesson. o FS1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-89\"><p>FS1 will have images, ten rods, rulers, and sentence frames throughout the lesson. If a spoken explanation is difficult to follow, the concrete and visual information will give her another way to reason. The frames will help her communicate an estimate without having to construct all the English language on her own.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-90\"><p>o FS2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-91\"><p>I will give FS2 explicit directions and reminders to pause before acting. Comparing a range of estimates in Activity 1 will require her to consider reasonableness. During Activity 2, I will return her attention to the measurement process and how to record it. These prompts will help her organize the task before trying to finish it.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-92\"><p>o FS3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-93\"><p>FS3 will have extension tasks after the initial partner work, including finding differences between estimates or measured lengths. These challenges will give him a reason to extend his thinking. Hands-on activity, movement opportunities, and check-ins about whether he needs a break will also help sustain engagement.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-94\"><p>\u2022 Connections to Recent Math Learning: Explain how your adaptation(s) for FS1, FS2, and FS3 are informed by and connected to recent observations and/or math assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing) as well as information related to the students\u2019 math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities provided by your cooperating teacher or supervising faculty. o FS1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-95\"><p>Observations, formative work, and individual interviews show that FS1 can solve numerical and visually represented problems. Word problems and oral directions become more accessible when I clarify the language. The planned visuals and Spanish-English connections respond to that pattern while building vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-96\"><p>o FS2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-97\"><p>FS2&#x27;s observations, assessments, and interview show the needed mathematical foundation, along with a tendency to begin before fully processing the task. I will prompt a step-by-step approach and use partner discussion to give her time to compare, explain, and revise her reasoning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-98\"><p>o FS3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-99\"><p>FS3&#x27;s work and interviews show strong reasoning, and I have observed greater engagement when a task offers a new challenge. Extensions will ask him to apply that strength. The practical activities and movement will help him maintain attention while he explores the ideas.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-100\"><p>\u2022 Leveraging Student Assets (Cultural and/or Linguistic) and/or Interests: Explain how the math lesson leverages (builds on) FS1\u2019s, FS2\u2019s, and FS3\u2019s assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests to support math learning and ALD learning goals. o FS1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-101\"><p>FS1 already uses drawings and concrete models to solve problems. This lesson makes those strengths useful through measuring tools and visual examples. She will also be encouraged to use the language that supports her thinking, with help connecting it to English. That combination supports the content goal and her developing academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-102\"><p>o FS2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-103\"><p>FS2&#x27;s interest in practical situations will be useful when estimating and measuring real objects. Comparing her own and a partner&#x27;s estimates will encourage her to pause, check reasonableness, and ask for clarification. Those exchanges will also give her a purpose for using the math vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-104\"><p>o FS3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-105\"><p>FS3 enjoys exploring and testing ideas. Estimating an object&#x27;s length before measuring it gives him a problem to investigate, while the extensions offer further challenge. Explaining his choices will connect that independent exploration with the language goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-106\"><p>\u2022 Learning Needs: Explain how your planned adaptation(s) will address the individual learning needs of each focus student. o FS1: Describe how the adaptation(s) will support FS1\u2019s academic language development (ALD) within the math lesson through reading, writing, listening, and/or speaking.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-107\"><p>FS1 will read the worksheet directions, hear vocabulary modeled, and discuss the difference between an estimate and a measurement. Examples of high, low, and reasonable estimates will make the terms concrete. Partner discussion and sentence frames will help her turn that understanding into a spoken explanation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-108\"><p>o FS2: Describe how the adaptation(s) will support FS2\u2019s specific math learning need(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-109\"><p>The sequence asks FS2 to pause, identify the task, try a method, and explain it. Hearing a partner&#x27;s thinking will give her another opportunity to check or revise the process. During measurement, the need for accurate alignment will reinforce the value of moving through the steps carefully.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-110\"><p>o FS3: Describe how you will adapt the environment to support FS3\u2019s well-being and/or behavior in the math lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-111\"><p>FS3 will have hands-on choices, extension problems, and access to a break. Estimating and testing a length should hold his interest, while comparisons will offer added challenge. I will check how he is feeling and provide space to stretch or rest when needed so he can return ready to work.</p></div>"}, {"id": "D", "title": "Part D Math Lesson Resources and Materials", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-112\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014Part D: Math Lesson Resources and/or Materials</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-113\"><p>Resources or Material</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-114\"><p>Measuring Tools</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-115\"><p>Description and Image(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-116\"><p>Students will measure in centimeters with rulers and ten rods.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image1.jpg\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image1.jpg\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image2.jpg\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image2.jpg\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-117\"><p>Resources or Material</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-118\"><p>Worksheet</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-119\"><p>Description and Image(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-120\"><p>The worksheet asks students to estimate object lengths and then record their measurements.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image3.jpg\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image3.jpg\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image4.jpg\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image4.jpg\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image5.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image5.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-121\"><p>Resources or Material</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-122\"><p>Objects Around the Classroom</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-123\"><p>Description and Image(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-124\"><p>Students will measure a sticky note, a small book, a spoon, a paper clip, and a pen.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image6.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image6.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image7.jpg\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image7.jpg\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image8.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image8.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image9.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image9.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image10.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image10.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-125\"><p>Resources or Material</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-126\"><p>Content Slides/Presentation</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-127\"><p>Description and Image(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-128\"><p>A projected slide deck will guide the warm-up and activities. Its tasks correspond to the worksheet.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image11.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image11.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image12.jpg\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image12.jpg\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image13.jpg\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image13.jpg\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image14.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 measurement: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ms-math-image14.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 measurement: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-129\"><p>Copyright \u00a9 2025 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. May Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved.</p></div>"}, {"id": "F", "title": "Part F Teaching Commentary", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-130\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 2: Teach and Assess\u2014Part F: Commentary Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-math-131\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt for the video clip(s) that you will submit (up to 8 pages). You must address each prompt at least once across all the video(s) submitted. Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 25\u201326 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-132\"><p>In the commentary for your video clip(s), address the following prompts:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-133\"><p>\u2022 Describe what you did to create and sustain a safe, positive learning environment. Were these strategies effective? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-134\"><p>\u2022 Describe the UDL-focused strategy(ies) you used to actively engage the students in an asset-based math lesson. o Why did the strategy(ies), including promoting higher-order thinking, support students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math content and practice learning goal? o Why did the strategy(ies), including promoting higher-order thinking, support students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math ALD learning goal?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-135\"><p>\u2022 What strategies did you use to monitor students\u2019 progress to support and/or advance their math content knowledge and/or practice? Why?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-136\"><p>\u2022 How did you adjust your instruction, and how did you respond intentionally to support student learning? Why were these intentional interactions supportive in helping the students make progress and/or advance their math content knowledge and/or math practice and math ALD?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-137\"><p>Video Clip 1: Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-138\"><p>00:16, 00:21, and 00:23: I move students from quiet consideration of the warm-up into familiar partner talk, listening to their interpretations and interactions. At 00:31, I invite whole-class responses. At 00:52 and 00:56, I restate an idea and recognize shared observations. At 01:01 and 01:22, I invite another choice and make the explanation clear to the class.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-139\"><p>01:48, 02:12, and 02:39: My questions draw attention to units, an emerging misconception, and how the image represents measurement. At 02:48, I bring in the remaining choice. At 03:19 and 03:43, I recognize an extension and reconnect an explanation to the visual. At 03:54, students recall earlier measurement learning. At 04:00 and 04:23, I emphasize that different choices can be justified, supporting participation without fear of a single wrong selection.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-140\"><p>04:31 and 04:37: I organize worksheet collection by rows and use students&#x27; home language to clarify. At 05:01 and 05:10, students think independently and then follow a structured partner exchange. At 05:12, I provide targeted language support, followed by class sharing at 05:28. At 05:56, I connect the vocabulary with a familiar phrase, too short.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-141\"><p>06:00, 06:02, 06:04, and 06:13: I repeat the cycle of individual thought, low-pressure partner discussion, bilingual support, and whole-group sharing. At 06:33, I connect about right with the familiar idea of making sense. At 06:40 and 06:42, I ask about measurement rules and listen more closely to emerging bilingual students&#x27; reasoning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-142\"><p>07:02 and 07:06: I make the material accessible and connect it with previous learning. At 07:22, I link adjust with change. Students reconsider their estimates at 07:32, with additional language support at 07:38. I check time and support needs at 07:50 and invite explanations at 07:58. At 08:29, I use the visual to clarify; at 08:41 and 08:55, I state the goal and revisit vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-143\"><p>09:04, 09:18, and 9:38: Before students leave the carpet, I explain partnerships and have them consider how they will work together and what steps come next. At 09:51, I circulate. At 10:16, I review work with students, and at 10:41, I remind them that estimates may differ. At 10:49, I return to the precision and rules needed for measurement.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-144\"><p>11:23 and 11:25: I check understanding and continue the process with other objects. At 11:30, I address difficulties measuring the spoon. At 11:51, students can raise differences in their results or ask for clarification. At 12:07, 12:13, and 12:28, I invite reflection on the lesson in a supportive discussion.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-145\"><p>13:15: I recognize progress toward the goal. At 13:23 and 13:42, students connect the work with everyday life and possible use beyond class. At 13:51, I make sure a classmate&#x27;s contribution is heard. At 14:09, I connect different measurement approaches with previous learning. At 14:26, I restate an outside-school application. Finally, at 14:39, I use the thumbs check to notice students who may still feel uncertain.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-146\"><p>Video Clip 2 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-147\"></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-148\"><p>Video Clip 3 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-149\"></div>"}, {"id": "G", "title": "Part G Reflection", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-150\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 3: Reflect\u2014Part G: Written Narrative: Reflection on What You Learned Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-math-151\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 7 pages). In your reflection, refer to evidence from Step 1 and/or Step 2 (e.g., Parts A\u2013F). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 30\u201331 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. 1. How effective was your asset-based, UDL-focused instructional approach in advancing your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math content and practice learning goal? How did you monitor and support students\u2019 progress? Explain why or why not the approach was effective.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-152\"><p>The different tools and representations helped students work toward estimating and measuring in centimeters. Rulers and ten rods made the ideas concrete and gave students practice selecting an appropriate tool under MP5. I observed their work, listened to explanations, and reviewed what they recorded. Those checks showed how they were using estimates, alignment, and measurement and helped me provide support during the activity.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-153\"><p>2. How effective was your asset-based, UDL-focused instructional approach in advancing your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math ALD learning goal? How did you monitor and support students\u2019 progress? Explain why or why not the approach was effective.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-154\"><p>Quiet thinking time and structured partner talk gave students space to prepare an explanation before sharing with the class. Sentence frames and guiding questions supported that preparation. I connected unfamiliar terms with words students already understood and modeled the vocabulary in context. Listening to their discussions helped me judge how well they were using the language to explain the mathematics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-155\"><p>3. Analyze the effectiveness of the adaptation(s) used to support each focus student\u2019s learning needs: a. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 1\u2019s language learning needs effectively support their progress toward the learning goals? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-156\"><p>FS1 used the visual tools to support her understanding and participated in discussions about estimates that were high, low, or reasonable. She also used language to compare an estimate with a measurement. The combination of home-language support, English models, and concrete representations helped her show the content knowledge while continuing to develop mathematical English.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-157\"><p>b. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 2\u2019s math learning need(s) effectively support their progress toward the learning goals? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-158\"><p>The practical task and timed discussion steps helped FS2 slow down. Accurate measurement required attention to alignment and the process, while individual thinking time and partner explanation gave her a reason to check her reasoning. Those supports improved access to the content and language goals, even though estimation still needs targeted practice.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-159\"><p>c. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 3\u2019s well-being and/or behavior in the math lesson effectively support their progress toward the learning goals? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-160\"><p>FS3 stayed highly involved, explained his reasoning, responded to peers, and helped clarify confusion. He also used the available breaks: stretching while on the rug and resting his head after finishing the worksheet while others continued. The combination of challenge, hands-on work, and opportunities to regulate supported his participation and progress.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-161\"><p>4. How did the safe, positive learning environment you created support the students\u2019 ability to engage in the math lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-162\"><p>Students were willing to offer an idea, hear another approach, and add to classmates&#x27; explanations. Treating different perspectives as useful helped them take risks during discussion. Working together also gave them support when they needed another way to understand the task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-163\"><p>5. How did the students\u2019 information you provided in Part A (Written Narrative: Contextual Information) inform your math lesson planning and teaching? a. Prior math learning</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-164\"><p>I built on students&#x27; experience with Which One Doesn&#x27;t Belong routines and measurement activities. Familiar formats let them focus on the new connections among tools and units. During teaching, I explicitly recalled earlier learning, clarified where needed, and checked how students were managing the activities.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-165\"><p>b. Students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-166\"><p>Knowing that all students use more than one language led me to plan varied representations and opportunities for language support. Their interest in collaboration and practical work shaped the activities. I also included time to connect measurement with their lives beyond school so their experiences could contribute to the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-167\"><p>c. Students\u2019 learning needs</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-168\"><p>Students could choose tools that made the process clear to them, including rulers and ten rods. I selected objects with whole-centimeter lengths so partial units would not become an additional demand. Structured discussions supported language needs, and I remained flexible about pacing and the amount of help students required.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-169\"><p>6. How did you respond intentionally to your students and adjust your instruction to provide \u201cjust-in-time\u201d support to advance their math development?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-170\"><p>I used observations and discussion to identify a misconception as it arose. When students needed clarification, I modeled the process again or rephrased the explanation. Responding at that point helped them continue with a clearer understanding rather than carry the same error into the next object.</p></div>"}, {"id": "H", "title": "Part H Next Steps", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-171\"><p>Multiple Subject Step 4: Apply\u2014Part H: Narrative: Application of What You Learned Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ms-math-172\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 4 pages). Refer to evidence from Step 1, Step 2, and/or Step 3 (e.g., Parts A\u2013G) to support your responses. Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see page 34 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. 1. Based on what you learned from the math lesson you taught during this cycle, do you need to re-teach the students any part of the lesson in the next lesson you plan to teach? Explain why or why not. a. For the group of students</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-173\"><p>Most students demonstrated the estimating and measuring goals, using tools, units, and alignment appropriately and sometimes extending the work through comparisons. I would continue with a review and more practice rather than repeat the whole lesson. Their explanations about high, low, and reasonable estimates provide a foundation for moving forward.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-174\"><p>b. For Focus Student 1</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-175\"><p>FS1 understands the estimation and measurement concepts and does not need the lesson retaught. She would benefit from more support in explaining that understanding fully in English. I would build on the reasoning she can express in her primary language and strengthen the connections to English vocabulary, including language for comparisons.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-176\"><p>c. For Focus Student 2</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-177\"><p>FS2 showed stronger measurement skills than estimation skills. I would provide focused estimation practice rather than reteach all the content. A deliberate sequence of looking, comparing with a reference, estimating, and explaining would help her slow down and judge the task before responding.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-178\"><p>d. For Focus Student 3</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-179\"><p>FS3 completed the work accurately, used the language confidently, and helped classmates. His questions and ideas extended measurement into everyday situations and comparisons. He does not need reteaching; further challenge will help him apply and deepen what he knows.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-180\"><p>2. How will you continue to use UDL to engage students in math learning (content and practice)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-181\"><p>I will continue combining visuals, discussion, and practical activities, with choices about how students access and express the work. Collaboration will remain central because students enjoy learning with peers. Giving them some control over tools and participation will support engagement while maintaining the mathematical goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-182\"><p>3. What additional higher-order thinking skill(s) will you engage students in to advance their math content and practice?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-183\"><p>I will ask students to analyze a problem, connect it with other learning, evaluate a method, and explain why the method makes sense. Creating their own situations or representations will extend that reasoning. Connections with mathematics outside school will give those tasks a meaningful context.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-184\"><p>4. How will you continue to advance these students\u2019 math ALD?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-185\"><p>I will model vocabulary and restate students&#x27; ideas using mathematical language, then provide sentence frames when needed. Discussion will continue to move from private thinking to partner talk and whole-class sharing. Over time, I will reduce those scaffolds so students can show greater independence in both reasoning and language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ms-math-186\"><p>5. How will you continue to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to their math learning?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ms-math-187\"><p>I will use familiar situations and objects and ask students to explain where they encounter the mathematics outside school. Their language resources and interests will continue to shape access to the lesson. Making those home-school connections explicit will help the work feel relevant and give students useful examples to reason about.</p></div>"}], "images": ["assets/examples/ms-math-image1.jpg", "assets/examples/ms-math-image2.jpg", "assets/examples/ms-math-image3.jpg", "assets/examples/ms-math-image4.jpg", "assets/examples/ms-math-image5.png", "assets/examples/ms-math-image6.png", "assets/examples/ms-math-image7.jpg", "assets/examples/ms-math-image8.png", "assets/examples/ms-math-image9.png", "assets/examples/ms-math-image10.png", "assets/examples/ms-math-image11.png", "assets/examples/ms-math-image12.jpg", "assets/examples/ms-math-image13.jpg", "assets/examples/ms-math-image14.png"], "responseCount": 77, "words": 5120, "historical": false}, "ss-c2": {"id": "ss-c2", "path": "ss-c2", "title": "Grade 7 rational numbers", "file": "05_SS_Cycle_2_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 SS CalTPA Cycle 2 Sample Submission.docx", "parts": [{"id": "A", "title": "Part A Contextual Information", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-1\"><p>Single Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014Part A: Written Narrative: Contextual Information Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c2-2\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 4 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 6\u20139 of the Instructional Cycle 2 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-3\"><p>Contextual Information About the Group of Students</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-4\"><p>\u2022 Age range(s) and grade level(s):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-5\"><p>The students are seventh graders, ages eleven through thirteen.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-6\"><p>\u2022 Student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to the content you plan to teach:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-7\"><p>About half the class identifies as Caucasian; other students have Hispanic, Black, Asian, and Middle Eastern backgrounds. Spanish and Chinese are the most common home languages among bilingual students. The class includes students receiving English-language support and one RFEP student with strong oral communication. Most families are middle class and actively support school achievement. These varied cultural, language, and family experiences give students resources to draw on in discussion and practical mathematics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-8\"><p>\u2022 Content-specific knowledge, skills, and/or abilities:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-9\"><p>Most students have completed sixth-grade mathematics and know the basic operations. Accuracy is less consistent when they work without a calculator or multiplication chart. Word problems are another area of need: students often require help identifying relevant information, understanding the question, and representing the situation mathematically. The lesson sequence will address these demands alongside rational-number procedures.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-10\"><p>\u2022 Identified English learners:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-11\"><p>One identified English learner speaks Spanish.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-12\"><p>\u2022 Identified learning needs (e.g., number of students with an IEP, a 504 plan, supplemental or intensified support through MTSS):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-13\"><p>Two students have 504 plans, and one has an IEP.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-14\"><p>\u2022 Language(s) of instruction to be used in the learning segment (e.g., biliteracy, developmental bilingual [one-way immersion], dual language [two-way immersion], English only):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-15\"><p>NA</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-16\"><p>\u2022 Staff member(s) who will assist you in the learning segment and their role in the classroom, if applicable (e.g., co-teachers, paraprofessionals, interpreters, bilingual instructional assistants, education specialists):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-17\"><p>A co-teacher will circulate, observe student work, and help students maintain attention and continue with the task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-18\"><p>Contextual Information About the Focus Student</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-19\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-20\"><p>The focus student is twelve and in seventh grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-21\"><p>\u2022 Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to the content and language:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-22\"><p>FS comes from a large blended family and values respect. He enjoys talking and working with peers and can explain his ideas comfortably aloud. Sports and games are strong interests, especially when a problem involves scores or statistics. Purposeful interaction and movement will help connect those strengths with his 504 supports and the mathematics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-23\"><p>\u2022 If your focus student is a district-/school-identified English learner, Fluent English Proficient (reclassified English learner), Heritage language speaker, and/or multilingual, describe the student\u2019s content-specific knowledge and skills in their primary language(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-24\"><p>NA</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-25\"><p>\u25cf Content-specific knowledge, skills, and abilities:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-26\"><p>FS completed sixth-grade math with an average of B\u2013. He brings knowledge of whole numbers, fractions, decimals, ratios, proportions, and early algebra. He learns new ideas quickly and reasons well, particularly when working with a teacher or partner. Independent work is harder to sustain, even when he can explain the same process accurately in conversation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-27\"><p>\u25cf Learning needs related to the focus student\u2019s content-specific and language development:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-28\"><p>FS&#x27;s ADHD affects attention, impulse control, and the ability to remain seated for long periods. He may call out or lose interest when he understands a concept before classmates are ready to move on. Organization and completing work at home are also concerns. His oral reasoning is a strength, but he sometimes substitutes everyday terms for mathematical ones, such as top and bottom for numerator and denominator. He needs explicit vocabulary practice alongside attention and work-completion supports.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-29\"><p>\u25cf If your focus student is an English learner, learning needs related to their English language development:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-30\"><p>NA</p></div>"}, {"id": "B", "title": "Part B Lesson Planning", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-31\"><p>Single Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014 Part B: Learning Segment Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c2-32\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt. Do not delete or alter the prompts. You may provide up to 5 pages per lesson. For more information, see pages 9\u201311 of the Instructional Cycle 2 Performance Assessment Guide. Across the 3\u20135 lessons: Scope and Sequence Explain how your 3\u20135 lessons fit into the larger scope and sequence of the unit (systematic instruction).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-33\"><p>The four lessons move from representing rational numbers in different forms to operating with them and applying them in context. Students first convert fractions and decimals, then add and subtract, then multiply and divide. The final lesson brings those skills together in real situations requiring them to interpret a problem, choose operations, and explain the result.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-34\"><p>Summarize how your 3\u20135 lessons apply findings from previous content- specific assessments.1</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-35\"><p>Earlier integer assessments showed that students could apply the four operations. I will build on those procedures as the number forms expand to fractions and decimals. This preserves a familiar foundation while increasing the demands of calculation and practical problem solving.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-36\"><p>Explain how your lessons align with one another to create a progression of learning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-37\"><p>Conversion establishes how fractions and decimals represent rational numbers. Addition and subtraction then require attention to signs, common denominators, and decimal placement. Multiplication and division extend the same number understanding to new procedures. In the final lesson, students decide which operations fit a situation and interpret the answer in that context.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-38\"><p>Lesson 1 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-39\"><p>Seventh grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-40\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-41\"><p>Converting Between Forms of Rational Numbers</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-42\"><p>Standard(s)/Framework(s) and Learning Goals Grade-level CA standard(s)/framework(s)2</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-43\"><p>CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.NA.2.d: Convert a rational number to a decimal using long division; know that the decimal form of a rational number terminates in 0s or eventually repeats.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-44\"><p>Content-specific learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-45\"><p>Students will use long division to express fractions as decimals and place value to express decimals as fractions in simplest form.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-46\"><p>Grade-level CA ELD standard(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-47\"><p>ELD.PI.7.5.Ex: Listening actively: Demonstrate active listening in oral presentation activities by asking and answering detailed questions, with occasional prompting and moderate support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-48\"><p>ELD learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-49\"><p>Students will listen to conversion explanations and ask or answer detailed questions using the mathematical vocabulary needed to describe the process.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-50\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-51\"><p>I will invite explanations in students&#x27; own words, then connect their language to the mathematical terms. Number lines and visible long-division steps will support that connection. Familiar uses of fractions and decimals, such as money, discounts, and measurement, will give the procedures a practical purpose.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-52\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-53\"><p>Students will begin by ordering positive and negative fractions and decimals independently. Volunteers will explain their choices, and I will use questions or a number-line model to clarify comparisons. I will then demonstrate fraction-to-decimal conversion through long division. Students will try a problem and explain the steps. Next, I will model converting a decimal to a fraction using place value and reducing the result. Discussion and immediate feedback will address errors as they appear.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-54\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Summarize your formative assessment(s). Include how the assessment(s) connect to the content-specific and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-55\"><p>During independent conversion and ordering practice, I will circulate, review work, and ask students to explain a decision. I will model again when needed and ask students who are ready to justify a comparison or strategy more fully. Whole-class review will let me assess both the procedure and the language used to explain it. Those observations will guide additional support or extension.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-56\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-57\"><p>I will connect familiar integer vocabulary with rational and irrational numbers, then introduce terminating and repeating decimals through examples. As I model long division on the board, I will explain each step using the terms students need. Questions and student explanations will give them opportunities to use the language while doing the mathematics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-58\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student, based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-59\"><p>Clear visuals, step-by-step models, and consistent vocabulary will support the class. FS will have guided notes with partly completed examples so he can attend to the process without an excessive reading or copying demand. Purposeful partner interaction will let him explain his reasoning, remain engaged, and receive clarification while practicing the skill.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-60\"><p>Lesson 2 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-61\"><p>Seventh grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-62\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-63\"><p>Addition and Subtraction with Rational Numbers</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-64\"><p>Standard(s)/Framework(s) and Learning Goals Grade-level CA standard(s)/framework(s)3</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-65\"><p>CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.NS.1: Apply and extend previous understandings of addition and subtraction to add and subtract rational numbers.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-66\"><p>Content-specific learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-67\"><p>Students will calculate sums and differences involving integers, fractions, and decimals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-68\"><p>Grade-level CA ELD standard(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-69\"><p>ELD.PI.7.1.Ex: Exchanging information/ideas: Contribute to class, group, and partner discussions by following turn-taking rules, asking relevant questions, affirming others, adding relevant information, and paraphrasing key ideas. ELD.PI.7.5.Ex: Listening actively: Demonstrate active listening in oral presentation activities by asking and answering detailed questions, with occasional prompting and moderate support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-70\"><p>ELD learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-71\"><p>Students will follow my explanations and those of their peers, then use mathematical vocabulary to ask and answer questions about adding and subtracting rational numbers.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-72\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-73\"><p>A volcano problem will ask students to consider the distance between a summit and an underground reservoir. Another example will combine gravity and thrust acting on a rocket. These contexts connect rational-number operations with interests in Earth science, natural events, and physical systems.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-74\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-75\"><p>The warm-up will revisit comparisons and integer addition and subtraction. We will recall the meanings of integer and rational number, then apply familiar sign rules to fractions and decimals. I will model the examples and give students practice using the same reasoning. Circulating during work will let me check understanding and provide support where a step is unclear.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-76\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Summarize your formative assessment(s). Include how the assessment(s) connect to the content-specific and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-77\"><p>Students will solve projected examples in their workbooks. I will check accuracy and ask how the signs helped them decide what to do. Their explanations will show both procedural understanding and use of the lesson vocabulary. Feedback will identify correct reasoning and help students revise an error or make an explanation more precise.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-78\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-79\"><p>I will revisit integer and rational number and display terms including sum, difference, absolute value, and commutative property. Modeling the vocabulary while working through examples will connect what students hear with what they see. Discussion will provide opportunities to use the terms in their own explanations.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-80\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student, based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-81\"><p>I will combine visible examples, oral explanations, and opportunities to respond. FS may take a one- or two-minute break, such as getting water or briefly walking outside, when needed for attention and regulation. I will read directions and problems aloud and use partner interaction to support reasoning and engagement. Consistent vocabulary and guided steps will help students follow the process.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-82\"><p>Lesson 3 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-83\"><p>Seventh grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-84\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-85\"><p>Multiplication and Division with Rational Numbers</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-86\"><p>Standard(s)/Framework(s) and Learning Goals Grade-level CA standard(s)/framework(s)4</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-87\"><p>CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.NS.2: Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication and division and of fractions to multiply and divide rational numbers.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-88\"><p>Content-specific learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-89\"><p>Students will find products and quotients involving integers, fractions, and decimals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-90\"><p>Grade-level CA ELD standard(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-91\"><p>ELD.PI.7.1.Ex: Exchanging information/ideas: Contribute to class, group, and partner discussions by following turn-taking rules, asking relevant questions, affirming others, adding relevant information, and paraphrasing key ideas. ELD.PI.7.5.Ex: Listening actively: Demonstrate active listening in oral presentation activities by asking and answering detailed questions, with occasional prompting and moderate support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-92\"><p>ELD learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-93\"><p>Students will listen to explanations of rational-number multiplication and division and respond to clarifying questions with appropriate mathematical terms.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-94\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-95\"><p>The descending-hikers example connects changes in elevation with physical activity and familiar outdoor situations. A rain-barrel problem connects division with water draining over time. These settings give students a way to picture the quantities before working with the abstract rational-number operations.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-96\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-97\"><p>We will review integer multiplication and division in the warm-up and revisit the distinction between integers and rational numbers. I will model a hiking problem and emphasize how the integer sign rules carry into rational-number multiplication. Students will highlight that connection in their workbooks. After the division model, they will solve examples independently, compare with a partner, and explain the procedures using the lesson vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-98\"><p>Summarize your formative assessment(s). Include how the assessment(s) connect to the content-specific and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-99\"><p>I will review workbook solutions and ask students how they determined the sign of a product or quotient. Sharing solutions at the board will make their reasoning available for feedback. This will help me distinguish a computational error from uncertainty about the rule while checking the language used to explain the operation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-100\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-101\"><p>I will review integer and rational number and model product, quotient, and related operation vocabulary in context. Displayed terms and worked examples will support comprehension. Partner and whole-class explanations will require students to use the vocabulary while describing a method and responding to questions.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-102\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student, based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-103\"><p>Visible steps, spoken directions, and consistent terminology will provide several ways to follow the lesson. FS may use a one- or two-minute regulation break when needed. Partner work will draw on his oral reasoning and social strengths, while guided examples and immediate clarification support sustained attention to the calculation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-104\"><p>Lesson 4 Grade Level:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-105\"><p>Seventh grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-106\"><p>Lesson Topic:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-107\"><p>Using Rational Numbers to Model Situations</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-108\"><p>Standard(s)/Framework(s) and Learning Goals Grade-level CA standard(s)/framework(s)5</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-109\"><p>CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.NS.3: Solve real-world and mathematical problems involving the four operations with rational numbers</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-110\"><p>Content-specific learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-111\"><p>Students will interpret practical situations, choose among the four operations with rational numbers, and solve the resulting problems in context.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-112\"><p>Grade-level CA ELD standard(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-113\"><p>ELD.PI.7.6.A Ex: Explain ideas, phenomena, processes, and text relationships (e.g., compare/contrast, cause/effect, problem/solution) based on close reading of a variety of grade-level texts and viewing of multimedia, with moderate support. ELD.PI.7.6.C Ex: Use knowledge of morphology (e.g., affixes, roots, and base words), context, reference materials, and visual cues to determine the meaning of unknown and multiple-meaning words on familiar and new topics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-114\"><p>ELD learning goal(s) Students will be able to\u2026</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-115\"><p>Students will use context, operation words, and visual information to clarify unfamiliar language in a word problem and explain which mathematical operations fit the situation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-116\"><p>Lesson Activities and Delivery Describe how you plan to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-117\"><p>The examples involve a boat being lowered through a lock and Kevin&#x27;s score in a trivia game. Engineering systems and games provide concrete settings for interpreting changing quantities. A shared word bank and sentence frames will support explanations using terms such as unit rate, total score, increase, decrease, and change in temperature.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-118\"><p>Summarize the lesson activity(ies).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-119\"><p>Students will begin with independent review of rational-number operations while I check work and ask about reasoning. We will discuss selected answers before I model how to interpret the first contextual problem. For the second example, students will read, highlight important language, and discuss their reasoning with a partner before class discussion. I will circulate, give feedback, and scaffold the interpretation where needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-120\"><p>Summarize your formative assessment(s) and/or summative assessment(s). Include how the assessment(s) connect to the content-specific and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-121\"><p>Independent example problems will show how students interpret the language and select an operation. I will ask them to explain that choice as I review their work, then use shared responses to assess class understanding. Accuracy and reasoning together will provide evidence for the content and language goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-122\"><p>Describe how you plan to use integrated English language development (ELD).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-123\"><p>Vocabulary practice will be built into the calculations and word-problem discussions. I will ask students to explain an operation choice or the sign of a result and support them with models, partner talk, and written responses. The aim is to connect mathematical language with the reasoning it expresses.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-124\"><p>Describe how you plan to use adaptations to address the students\u2019 learning needs, including the learning needs of the focus student, based on their assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-125\"><p>I will use clear visuals, explicit steps, spoken directions, and consistent vocabulary. FS will have brief breaks when needed and opportunities to explain ideas with a partner. I will recognize completed work, constructive contributions, and accurate vocabulary use. These supports are intended to sustain engagement while keeping the focus on interpreting and solving the problem.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-126\"><p>Template notes</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-127\"><p>2 Content standards/frameworks and learning goals may be the same across all lessons of the learning segment or may build with each successive lesson (activities and assessments). For your lesson, you will choose one or more grade-level California Content Standards and/or Curriculum Frameworks and current California English Language Development Standards (CA ELD Standards) to write your learning goals. Be sure to write out the content and ELD standards and not refer to only the content or ELD standard number.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-128\"><p>3 Content standards/frameworks and learning goals may be the same across all lessons of the learning segment or may build with each successive lesson (activities and assessments). For your lesson, you will choose one or more grade-level California Content Standards and/or Curriculum Frameworks and current California English Language Development Standards (CA ELD Standards) to write your learning goals. Be sure to write out the content and ELD standards and not refer to only the content or ELD standard number.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-129\"><p>4 Content standards/frameworks and learning goals may be the same across all lessons of the learning segment or may build with each successive lesson (activities and assessments). For your lesson, you will choose one or more grade-level California Content Standards and/or Curriculum Frameworks and current California English Language Development Standards (CA ELD Standards) to write your learning goals. Be sure to write out the content and ELD standards and not refer to only the content or ELD standard number.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-130\"><p>5 Content standards/frameworks and learning goals may be the same across all lessons of the learning segment or may build with each successive lesson (activities and assessments). For your lesson, you will choose one or more grade-level California Content Standards and/or Curriculum Frameworks and current California English Language Development Standards (CA ELD Standards) to write your learning goals. Be sure to write out the content and ELD standards and not refer to only the content or ELD standard number.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-131\"><p>If needed, copy blank tables from Lesson 3 above for Lesson 4 and Lesson 5.</p></div>"}, {"id": "C", "title": "Part C Planning Narrative", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-132\"><p>Single Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014 Part C: Written Narrative: Description of Assessments Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c2-133\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 5 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 11\u201312 of the Instructional Cycle 2 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-134\"><p>Formative Assessment</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-135\"><p>\u2022 Describe one formative assessment you plan to implement during the learning segment (e.g., checks for understanding, exit ticket, self-assessment).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-136\"><p>Students will spend about seven minutes independently solving examples of rational-number multiplication and division. We will then review their answers and explanations as a class.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-137\"><p>\u2022 Describe what you expect the students to know and be able to demonstrate in their responses to the selected formative assessment and how you plan to monitor student understanding of the content-specific and ELD goals during the learning segment.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-138\"><p>I will look for accurate sign rules, fraction simplification using the greatest common factor, and division by multiplying by the second fraction&#x27;s reciprocal. While circulating, I will check both the written process and the student&#x27;s explanation. A random selector will broaden participation during review. Questions about same or different signs will help students reason through an error, and feedback will reinforce accurate use of numerator, denominator, product, quotient, and reciprocal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-139\"><p>\u2022 If you chose to use student self-assessment, explain how the student self-assessment provides opportunities for the students to build an awareness of what they have learned, what they need to learn, and what they need additional support with in relation to making progress toward meeting the content-specific and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-140\"><p>NA</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-141\"><p>Summative Assessment</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-142\"><p>\u2022 Describe the summative assessment you plan to implement during the final lesson of your learning segment.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-143\"><p>The cumulative assessment will contain 20 free-response questions across the four lessons. Two will convert fractions to decimals, including one terminating and one repeating result; two will convert decimals to simplified fractions; and two will compare rational numbers using the appropriate symbol. The test will also include eight computation problems, four integer sum-and-difference problems, and contextual questions using language such as above, below, distance, loss, share equally, and quotient. Together, these tasks assess procedures and interpretation in practical situations.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-144\"><p>\u2022 What content-specific and ELD goals are evaluated by the summative assessment (e.g., knowledge, skills, or abilities your students will demonstrate)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-145\"><p>Students will demonstrate conversion, comparison, and the four operations with rational numbers, including their application in word problems. The language component involves interpreting mathematical terms and translating a written situation into symbols or equations. The assessment will therefore show procedural knowledge alongside the ability to understand and represent the language of a problem.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-146\"><p>\u2022 What are you planning to collect or observe from each student as an assessment response (product, process, or performance)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-147\"><p>I will examine each student&#x27;s written solutions and the steps used to reach them. I will check signs, operation procedures, simplification, and the representation of a word problem. Looking beyond the final answer will help me distinguish a minor calculation error from uncertainty about the method or meaning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-148\"><p>\u2022 What evidence will you look for in the students\u2019 responses to indicate that the students are progressing toward meeting the content-specific and ELD goals?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-149\"><p>For conversion, I will check that the numerator becomes the dividend and that decimal place value is represented correctly. Fraction work should show common denominators where needed, appropriate simplification, and use of a reciprocal for division. Decimal calculations should show correct alignment or placement, and mixed numbers should be converted when the method requires it. I will also look for correct interpretation of signs and terms such as distance and quotient, since those language decisions affect the chosen calculation.</p></div>"}, {"id": "F", "title": "Part F Teaching Commentary", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-150\"><p>Single Subject Step 2: Teach and Assess\u2014Part F: Commentary Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c2-151\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt that relates to each video clip that you will submit (up to 8 pages). You must address each prompt at least once across all the video(s) submitted. Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 18\u201319 of the Instructional Cycle 2 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-152\"><p>In the commentary for your video clip(s), address the following prompts:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-153\"><p>\u25cf Describe how you engaged students in deep learning of the content to enhance their content-specific knowledge, skills, and/or abilities. Explain why this instruction supported the students in progressing toward meeting the content-specific goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-154\"><p>\u25cf Describe the integrated ELD you used in the video clip(s). Explain why this instruction supported students in progressing toward meeting the content-specific and ELD goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-155\"><p>\u25cf Describe the strategy(ies) you used to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests. o Why did the strategy(ies) support your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the content-specific and ELD learning goals?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-156\"><p>\u25cf How did you use assessment(s) to monitor student progress toward meeting the content-specific and ELD goal(s) in the selected video clip(s)?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-157\"><p>\u25cf Given what you learned from the assessment(s), explain adjustments you made to your instruction and how they supported students\u2019 content-specific and ELD.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-158\"><p>\u25cf What specific, actionable feedback related to the content did you provide to students about what they did well and/or their misconceptions/gaps in knowledge? Why was this feedback able to support students\u2019 content-specific and English language development?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-159\"><p>Video Clip 1: Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-160\"><p>Students begin by reading the highlighted rule in their workbooks. At 00:87, I ask how integer addition and subtraction connect to other rational numbers; at 1:13, partners discuss that connection. At 1:36, students record a response while I read it aloud. I circulate at 2:04 to check the written explanations and clarify as needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-161\"><p>At 2:58 and 5:18, questions about same and different signs return students to the reasoning behind the rule. I model the terms at 3:42 and 6:15, then have students record the rules and vocabulary at 3:58 and 6:24. The combination of discussion, listening, writing, and feedback reinforces both the procedure and the language used to explain it.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-162\"><p>Video Clip 2 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-163\"><p>Independent work showed that some students still needed help adding and subtracting rational numbers, so I model the process again. At 00:20, I use a problem with one decimal and one fraction and explain the need for a common form. At 00:35, I explain why I prefer a decimal representation for that example. At 00:46 and 00:50, I draw attention to place value and ask students to name the quantity represented.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-164\"><p>At 1:35, we revisit the sign rules. When students cannot recall a rule, I pause and restate it. These questions give me immediate information about understanding and let me connect the correction to precise mathematical language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-165\"><p>Video Clip 3 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-166\"><p>The game awards +2\u00bc points for a correct response and \u2212\u00bd point for an incorrect one. This familiar scoring context gives students a reason to combine rational numbers. At 00:11, I record and mark important words; from 00:18\u201300:37, I ask how those words relate to the calculation. At 00:44, a student explains the operation, and I reinforce the connection with multiplication.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-167\"><p>At 1:08, I acknowledge the reasoning that a total requires a sum. At 1:23, students receive a similar problem with a different player and the same scoring rules. While circulating from 1:41\u20133:00, I ask targeted questions when a student becomes stuck. This lets me assess whether students can transfer the reasoning and use the problem&#x27;s language to support their decisions.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-168\"><p>Video Clip 4 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-169\"><p>Students first attempt the unit-review problems independently, then explain their work during a discussion using a random name selector. At 00:10 and 00:18, my questions focus on how to begin and convert a mixed number to an improper fraction. At 00:42, another student identifies a common denominator. At 00:58, I record the work while modeling numerator and denominator.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-170\"><p>From 1:17\u20131:53, I use smaller guiding questions when a student hesitates. At 2:44, I respond to a calculation error, then ask at 2:54 why addition is appropriate. These exchanges make the steps and reasoning visible and provide immediate support for both calculation and mathematical explanation.</p></div>"}, {"id": "H", "title": "Part H Summative Assessment Feedback", "html": "<h3>Part H: Focus Student\u2019s</h3><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-171\"><p>Summative Assessment Actionable Feedback Feedback:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-172\"><p>You showed that you remember when to add or subtract integers and rational numbers. Some answers used the correct operation but had a sign error, so I deducted half a point for those mistakes. Before combining the numbers next time, circle their signs. That visual reminder can help you keep positive and negative values straight.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-173\"><p>I deducted a full point on problems where you appeared unsure how to begin. In class and partner work, you have explained these ideas successfully with support. On the test, some steps were lost. We will practice carrying out the sequence independently, and I will give you a short checklist of the rules for operations with rational numbers.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-174\"><p>Converting fractions to decimals was also difficult for you and several classmates. I will revisit long division with the class and provide additional guided practice through the conversion steps. Explaining your thinking aloud is a strength, so the next activity will begin with a partner. You will talk through the steps before trying them on your own. This will help you use what you know more confidently during independent work.</p></div>"}, {"id": "I", "title": "Part I Assessment Reflection", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-175\"><p>Single Subject Step 3: Reflect\u2014Part I: Written Narrative: Reflection and Analysis of</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-176\"><p>Summative Assessment Results Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c2-177\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 4 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. Be sure to include specific references to the results to support your reflections: What in the results led you to your conclusions? For more information, see pages 26\u201327 of the Instructional Cycle 2 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-178\"><p>Analysis and Reflection for the Group</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-179\"><p>\u2022 Identify and describe understandings, gaps in knowledge, and/or misconceptions related to the students\u2019 content development and ELD.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-180\"><p>The class average was about 77%. Students generally handled familiar operations and could often convert mixed numbers or identify multiplication and division in context. The results also exposed important gaps.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-181\"><p>Several students set up fraction-to-decimal division by placing the larger number inside, rather than using the numerator as the dividend. On question 7, some treated 15 feet below the surface and 2 feet above it as a distance of 13 feet rather than 17, overlooking direction and signs. Sign errors also affected otherwise appropriate procedures. Some students unnecessarily sought common denominators for multiplication or division. On question 16, confusion about quotient led a few students to choose the wrong operation. These patterns show a need to strengthen both conceptual understanding and interpretation of mathematical language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-182\"><p>\u2022 Describe what was effective about your instructional approach(es) and student activity(ies) for the group.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-183\"><p>Guided questions and repeated explanations helped students connect sign rules with their reasoning. Partner discussion gave them time to say the process aloud before sharing with the class. Those approaches sustained engagement and gave me opportunities to clarify vocabulary and procedures, supporting the progress seen for much of the group.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-184\"><p>\u2022 Describe instructional changes you would make that could have better supported the group if you taught this learning segment again.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-185\"><p>I would include more independent practice during the sequence. Students often succeeded with a partner or my guidance but did not apply the same steps consistently on the final assessment. I would also use a formative unit check or study guide before the summative test. The review emphasized later operations, while conversion gaps from earlier lessons remained. An earlier check would let me identify and reteach those foundations before students faced the cumulative assessment.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-186\"><p>Analysis and Reflection for the Focus Student</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-187\"><p>\u2022 Identify and describe understandings, gaps in knowledge, and/or misconceptions related to the focus student\u2019s content development and ELD.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-188\"><p>FS explained mathematical ideas well during guided and partner work, including the activities in Clips 2\u20134. The independent assessment showed a different pattern. He had difficulty recalling conversion steps, changing mixed numbers to improper fractions, and multiplying or dividing rational numbers. Rushing or losing focus may have contributed. He showed partial success in addition and subtraction but made sign errors. The analysis identifies question 5 as a successful contextual response, suggesting that he can select an operation when he understands the situation clearly.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-189\"><p>\u2022 How will the specific, actionable feedback you provided the focus student help them to improve or advance their understanding to continue their progress toward meeting the content and ELD goals?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-190\"><p>My feedback gives FS concrete ways to transfer oral reasoning into independent written work. Circling signs and following a short checklist will help him pause and track the process. I also explained that other students found conversions difficult and that we would revisit them together. Guided reteaching followed by independent practice should strengthen both accuracy and confidence.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-191\"><p>\u2022 Given your knowledge of the focus student, describe what was effective about your instructional approach(es) and student activity(ies). Refer to the focus student\u2019s summative assessment response.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-192\"><p>Partner and group discussion kept FS active and drew on his strong verbal reasoning. Combining listening, speaking, and writing helped him process the operations and maintain attention. This section of the assessment reflection identifies question 7 as evidence that instruction on contextual language and the modeling in Clip 3 supported his problem solving. I would retain those opportunities while strengthening the move to independent work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-193\"><p>\u2022 Describe instructional changes you would make for the focus student if you taught this learning segment again. Refer to the focus student\u2019s summative assessment response.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-194\"><p>Questions 1\u20134 and 12\u201315 show a need for more support transferring understanding to independent performance. I would check in with FS more frequently, provide a step checklist to make his reasoning visible, and structure independent work in short timed intervals. These changes would address attention and organization while giving him practice recording the procedures he can explain aloud.</p></div>"}, {"id": "J", "title": "Part J Follow Up Activity", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-195\"><p>Single Subject Step 4: Apply\u2014Part J: Written Narrative: Re-Teaching or Extension Activity Description Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c2-196\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 5 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 30\u201331 of the Instructional Cycle 2 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-197\"><p>\u2022 Activity (place an \u201cX\u201d in the appropriate bracket):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-198\"><p>X] re-teaching activity OR [ ] extension activity</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-199\"><p>\u2022 Provide the developmentally appropriate content-specific learning goal(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-200\"><p>Students will convert fractions to decimals through long division and decimals to fractions through place value, simplifying the resulting fraction.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-201\"><p>\u2022 Provide the developmentally appropriate ELD learning goal(s). The follow-up activity must include an ELD learning goal even if your focus student/group of students does not include an English learner.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-202\"><p>Students will listen actively and explain conversion steps during partner and class discussion using appropriate mathematical vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-203\"><p>\u2022 Summarize your activity and explain how you will check for understanding.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-204\"><p>I will reconnect the fraction bar with division, using 8/2 = 8 \u00f7 2 = 4 as an example. Students will set up division for another fraction independently, compare with a partner, and explain which number is the numerator and which is the denominator.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-205\"><p>For decimal-to-fraction conversion, I will review place value, model how the final decimal place determines the denominator, and simplify the fraction. Students will try another example, discuss it with a partner, and explain the process aloud. These checks will show whether they understand the setup as well as the calculation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-206\"><p>\u2022 For this activity, describe how you will leverage the focus student\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-207\"><p>I will connect conversions with sports statistics, game scores, money, and probability. References to finance, medicine, and STEM careers will draw on interests students have expressed. For FS, examples involving batting averages, shooting percentages, and win\u2013loss ratios will connect the task with sports and competition.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-208\"><p>\u2022 Determine adaptations to address the focus student\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014learning needs (e.g., accommodations and/or modifications, language supports, IEP supports/services).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-209\"><p>FS will sit near the front for convenient check-ins and work with a reliable peer during structured discussion. Visual steps and oral explanation will support processing, while the partnership will draw on his social strengths. I will monitor engagement and clarify the procedure before expecting independent application.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-210\"><p>\u2022 Explain why this activity supports the focus student\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014content-specific progress. Refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 to support your instructional decision making.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-211\"><p>The summative work identifies conversion as a specific need. Presenting a fraction as division and examining the quotient and remainder will give FS a clearer conceptual basis for remembering the steps. Structured partner talk will let him use his oral reasoning before applying the process independently in writing. This responds to the difference between his supported classroom performance and his test work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-212\"><p>\u2022 Provide an analysis of the recent assessments1 used in Step 1 and the formative and summative assessments used in Steps 2 and 3 to support your instructional decision making.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-213\"><p>Formative discussions showed that students, including FS, could explain and solve problems with support. The final assessment revealed conversion gaps that those checks had not fully captured because they emphasized later operations. I therefore selected conversion for reteaching and changed the explanation to make the division relationship explicit. Visual models, partner rehearsal, and focused checks will help me assess whether students can carry that understanding into independent work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-214\"><p>Template notes</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-215\"><p>1 For example, formative, summative, state testing; student work samples; observations; information provided by your cooperating teacher and/or supervising faculty</p></div>"}, {"id": "L", "title": "Part L Follow Up Commentary", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-216\"><p>Single Subject Step 4: Apply\u2014Part L: Commentary Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c2-217\"><p>Directions: Type your response within the brackets provided (up to 2 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see page 31 of the Instructional Cycle 2 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-218\"><p>In your commentary, address one of the following prompts:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-219\"><p>For a re-teaching activity:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-220\"><p>\u25cf Identify the targeted intervention and/or individualized strategy being used. Explain why this supports your focus student\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student(s) \u2014in making progress toward meeting the content-specific and ELD goals. OR</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-221\"><p>For an extension activity:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-222\"><p>\u25cf Identify the instruction provided that deepened or advanced your focus student\u2019s\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student\u2019s(s\u2019)\u2014learning. Explain why this activity supports your focus student\u2014and, if applicable, the additional student(s) \u2014 to take the next step in learning related to the content-specific and ELD goals. Video Clip: Address the commentary prompt that relates to this video. Describe what is happening and provide an explanation for what you are doing and why.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-223\"><p>I reteach conversion by connecting fractions with division and decimals with place value. At 00:19, I ask which number belongs inside the division setup, and students respond correctly. At 00:36, I address the larger-number misconception directly: the numerator is the dividend regardless of its size.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c2-224\"><p>I then ask students to identify the final decimal place and use it to construct a fraction. At 1:51, an incorrect answer leads me to write out the place values and guide the conversion visually. I finish by reinforcing simplification. These linked visual, spoken, and procedural cues give FS a clearer structure for understanding and explaining the steps.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-225\"><p>Retained Source Materials</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c2-226\"><p>The following source pages retain the instructional materials, assessment tools, and student work as supplied.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image1.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image1.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image2.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image2.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image3.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image3.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image4.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image4.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image5.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image5.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image6.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c2-image6.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 rational numbers: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure>"}], "images": ["assets/examples/ss-c2-image1.png", "assets/examples/ss-c2-image2.png", "assets/examples/ss-c2-image3.png", "assets/examples/ss-c2-image4.png", "assets/examples/ss-c2-image5.png", "assets/examples/ss-c2-image6.png"], "responseCount": 88, "words": 6507, "historical": false}, "ss-c1": {"id": "ss-c1", "path": "ss-c1", "title": "Grade 7 English", "file": "06_SS_Grade_7_English_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_V1 SS Cycle 1 (7th Grade English) Sample Submission.docx", "parts": [{"id": "A", "title": "Part A Contextual Information", "html": "<div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c1-1\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 7 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts.</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c1-2\"><p>Directions: Provide contextual information for the group of students you plan to teach during this content-specific lesson. For more information, see pages 5\u20136 of the Instructional Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-3\"><p>Single Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014Part A: Written Narrative: Contextual Information Template</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-4\"><p>Contextual Information About the Group of Students</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-5\"><p>\u2022 Age range(s) and grade level(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-6\"><p>Seventh graders, ages eleven and twelve.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-7\"><p>\u2022 Student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to the content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-8\"><p>The class includes 32 students from Hispanic, Caucasian, African American, Filipino, and Asian backgrounds. I learned about them through records, conversations, and consultation with my site support provider and resource teachers. Most were born and raised in California; two were born in Mexico. Their activities include athletics, art, music, and practical trades. These experiences bring varied perspectives into our reading and discussions. Classroom lessons have given students opportunities to recognize their own cultures and learn about others.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-9\"><p>\u2022 Content-specific knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-10\"><p>Students have been reading informational texts from our textbook and practicing how to identify a central idea. Gallery walks and Socratic Seminars give them experience discussing interpretations with peers. They have written summaries for different prompts and received regular feedback. I expect complete sentences, a response that restates the question, and enough detail to show understanding. This lesson continues that work with more attention to evidence and academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-11\"><p>\u2022 Identified English learners</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-12\"><p>Four students are working at EL level 3 and need continued practice with vocabulary and grammar. Visuals, supported texts, and explicit language instruction help them connect new concepts with what they know. Two students have been reclassified as fluent English proficient. I will differentiate the work to provide language scaffolds where needed and additional complexity where students are ready, drawing on the cultural and linguistic resources all six bring.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-13\"><p>\u2022 Identified learning needs (e.g., how many students with an IEP, a 504 plan, MTSS)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-14\"><p>The class description identifies six English learners, ten students with IEPs, and two with 504 plans among the 32 students.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-15\"><p>\u2022 Language(s) of instruction (e.g., biliteracy, developmental bilingual [one-way immersion], dual language [two-way immersion], English only) to be used in the lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-16\"><p>Instruction will be in English.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-17\"><p>Focus Students Focus Student 1 (FS1)</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-18\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-19\"><p>FS1 is eleven and in seventh grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-20\"><p>\u2022 Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-21\"><p>I have heard FS1 use English both in class and socially. He enjoys basketball, novels, library visits, and book talks. He asks about other people&#x27;s families but shares less about his own. He is eager to learn and often reminds classmates to treat one another and the teacher respectfully. His interest in reading gives me a strong starting point for this lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c1-22\"><p>Directions: Provide contextual information for the three focus students you selected. For more information, see pages 7\u20138 of the Instructional Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-23\"><p>\u2022 If FS1 is an English learner, current proficiency in English and proficiency in their primary language</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-24\"><p>FS1 is American Indian and lives in a shared-residency arrangement. His 2024\u20132025 English learner placement is level 3. On the STAR Reading assessment during the first month of school, his result was grade 2, month 8. These results indicate a need for language and reading support with seventh-grade work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-25\"><p>\u2022 Content-specific knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-26\"><p>FS1 asks questions and participates, and his earlier single-sentence summaries show progress in identifying important information. His work and STAR results suggest that he can engage with this lesson when support is available. He often needs directions clarified individually, so I will check that he understands the task before expecting independent work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-27\"><p>\u2022 Learning need(s) related to their academic language development (ALD)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-28\"><p>FS1 needs practice using words such as demonstrates, suggests, and indicates to explain a relationship between evidence and an idea. I want him to use the terms meaningfully in discussion and writing, rather than only recognize their definitions. The connection to ELD.PI.7.1Br is participation in discussion through relevant questions, information, and responses to others.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-29\"><p>Focus Student 2 (FS2)</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-30\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-31\"><p>FS2 is eleven and in seventh grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-32\"><p>\u2022 Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-33\"><p>FS2 enjoys drawing and often brings artwork to share or display. He also loves reading and library days. His shared interest in books with FS1 supports productive discussion, which is one reason they sit in the same desk group.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-34\"><p>\u2022 Content-specific knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-35\"><p>FS2 previously attended an Honors class because of his reading level. Teacher observations and administrative review suggested that a setting with more support would better meet his comprehension needs. He can identify a topic and details but needs help explaining how they form a central idea and developing that understanding in a summary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-36\"><p>\u2022 Learning needs related to content-specific ability</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-37\"><p>FS2 has ADHD and a 504 plan. His beginning-of-year STAR Reading result was grade 7, month 9. He sits near the front facing the teacher&#x27;s desk and has access to speech-to-text on a Chromebook and headphones when appropriate. These accommodations support attention and comprehension despite his stronger reading-level result.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-38\"><p>Focus Student 3 (FS3)</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-39\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-40\"><p>FS3 is eleven and in seventh grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-41\"><p>\u2022 Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-42\"><p>FS3 enjoys sharing stories about hobbies, trips, and favorite foods. Recently, she was excited about a cruise, especially its cheeseburgers. Her humor and interest in conversation can support collaborative work when she has a clear, constructive role.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-43\"><p>\u2022 Content-specific knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-44\"><p>FS3 completes work productively and contributes strong academic language in discussions. Her early-year STAR result was grade 6, month 6. She comprehends texts well when they match her reading level. She is kind, enjoys humor with peers, and often seems particularly comfortable interacting with adults.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-45\"><p>\u2022 Learning needs related to life experiences that may result in a need for additional adaptation(s) to support FS3\u2019s well-being and/or behavior during the content-specific lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-46\"><p>FS3 moves between family settings under a joint-custody arrangement and often describes her grandfather as her main provider. She has also had noticeable absences. She seeks acceptance and brings energy and humor to the class, while showing resilience as she adjusts to changing circumstances. I will provide consistency and individual check-ins after absences so she can catch up without feeling singled out.</p></div>"}, {"id": "B", "title": "Part B Lesson Planning", "html": "<div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c1-47\"><p>Directions: If you do not have access to a school or district lesson plan format, consider using the sample lesson plan template. Use the knowledge gathered about your students to establish grade-level appropriate, content-specific learning goals: one goal that supports content-specific learning and one goal that supports the content-specific ALD. Based on those learning goals, plan one asset-based, UDL-focused content-specific lesson. Provide answers to the following prompts by typing your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 10 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 8\u201310 of the Instructional Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-48\"><p>Single Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014Part B: Sample Lesson Plan Template Title of lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-49\"><p>Finding the Central Idea in Informational Text</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-50\"><p>Subject</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-51\"><p>English language arts, focusing on reading.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-52\"><p>Grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-53\"><p>Seventh grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-54\"><p>1. What are your goals for the content-specific lesson? a. Select a content-specific standard or framework: Based on the students\u2019 prior 1 learning, recent information you reviewed, student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/ or interests, and learning needs, select one grade-level, content-specific standard or framework.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-55\"><p>The STAR Reading information identifies a clear need for instruction on central ideas. In the reported group of 33, 22 students, or 66%, were beginning to understand the skill; eight, or 24%, were developing it; and three, or 9%, showed secure understanding. Students have practiced with leveled Freckle articles selected through STAR information, using Chromebooks and headphones, and have worked both independently and collaboratively.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-56\"><p>This lesson continues that practice with structured choices and support. The content standard is RI.7.2: determining two or more central ideas, analyzing their development, and providing an objective summary. The language connection is ELD.PI.7.10.b.Ex, which addresses increasingly concise summaries using complete sentences and key words. Choice and appropriate reading support are intended to help students engage with the task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-57\"><p>b. Develop a content-specific learning goal: Based on the age-/grade-level standard(s)/ framework(s) you selected, write one developmentally appropriate, content-specific learning goal (see example on page 10 of the Instructional Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-58\"><p>Students will identify a central idea in an informational text and write an accurate, objective summary. They will communicate the important information in their own words and use complete sentences and academic vocabulary. This brings the comprehension focus of RI.7.2 together with the summary-writing focus of ELD.PI.7.10.b.Ex.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-59\"><p>2. Develop a related ALD learning goal: Based on the relevant, related academic vocabulary needed for the lesson, create a content-specific academic language goal that supports the content-specific learning goal (see example on page 10 of the Instructional Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-60\"><p>Students will examine how two or more central ideas develop, identify relevant evidence, and explain the connections using words such as suggests, indicates, and represents. They will apply that language in an objective summary so the relationship between evidence and interpretation is clear.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-61\"><p>3. What will the students do in the content-specific lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-62\"><p>I will introduce the goal, display four central-idea choices, and model a sentence frame. After clarifying central idea and objective summary, I will organize group reading. Students will annotate, discuss meaning, and ask one another questions while I circulate and offer targeted prompts.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-63\"><p>Groups will select an option and record two or three supporting pieces of evidence. Sentence starters will be available when a group needs help beginning. Volunteers will then present their central idea and summary, and I will give focused feedback or clarify a misunderstanding.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-64\"><p>a. What instructional materials and/or resources will you use in the content-specific lesson? Why?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-65\"><p>Students will read a printed informational article from the Collections textbook. Paper copies allow annotation and notes during group reading. Four possible central ideas provide a starting point for analysis, and sentence frames support the written summary. These materials let students focus on connecting an interpretation with evidence while discussing the text with peers.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-66\"><p>4. What UDL-focused instructional strategies will you use during the content-specific lesson to support student learning? Describe how you will: a. Connect the content of this lesson to students\u2019 recent content-specific learning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-67\"><p>Group reading and discussion build on students&#x27; earlier collaborative text work. They can hear an idea, test it orally, and then write. The four choices narrow the initial task without removing the need to evaluate evidence. Sentence frames support organization for English learners and developing writers while leaving them responsible for what the summary says. These choices reflect the collaboration and representation supports identified in UDL 8.3 and 1.2.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-68\"><p>b. Create a safe, positive learning environment. 2</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-69\"><p>I will make group participation predictable and supportive, with space to ask questions and share an unfinished idea. Choice options and sentence frames provide guidance when students are unsure how to begin. Reading, discussion, and writing offer several ways to engage. The intention, consistent with the UDL supports identified as 4.1 and 5.4, is to make academic risk-taking more comfortable.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-70\"><p>c. Leverage your students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-71\"><p>Students bring experiences in sports, art, language, and community that can inform how they notice and discuss a text. Group work will let them contribute those perspectives and hear others. I will provide frames and choices so language demands do not prevent participation, while encouraging students to connect their own strengths with the analysis. This reflects the social and emotional supports identified in UDL 9.1\u20139.4.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-72\"><p>For example, take intellectual risks, engage in positive collaborative learning, foster positive attitudes toward learning, encourage 2 students\u2019 curiosity and/or persistence, physical safety d. Support the content-specific ALD of your students.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-73\"><p>Students will encounter the vocabulary while reading, hear it in discussion, and practice it in writing. I will model how a word such as suggests connects a piece of evidence to an interpretation. Frames and rehearsal will help students move toward a clear explanation rather than simply insert an academic term. These opportunities support the engagement and reflection described in UDL 8.4 and 9.3.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-74\"><p>e. Ask questions to engage students in higher-order thinking to deepen their 3 understanding of content.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-75\"><p>I will ask students to explain how central ideas relate, why a detail matters, and which evidence best supports an interpretation. I will also ask them to condense the article&#x27;s main point into two sentences and consider how the meaning would change if an important idea were removed. These questions require justification, attention to the author&#x27;s choices, and analysis beyond selecting an answer.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-76\"><p>f. Monitor the students\u2019 progress toward meeting the content-specific learning goal and the related content-specific ALD learning goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-77\"><p>I will compare what students say during group work with what they write independently. Their discussions will show how they identify and test a central idea; the summaries will show evidence use, organization, and academic language. Quick questions and redirection to the text will provide feedback during the task. The combined evidence will help me identify who is ready to move forward and who needs more practice.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-78\"><p>Template notes</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-79\"><p>You must use current grade-level California Content Standards and/or Curriculum Frameworks, as appropriate for your content 1 area.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-80\"><p>For example, application, analysis, synthesis, and evaluation3</p></div>"}, {"id": "C", "title": "Part C Planning Narrative", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-81\"><p>Single Subject Step 1: Plan\u2014Part C: Written Narrative: Lesson Adaptation(s) for Focus Students Template \u2022Adaptation(s): Determine what adaptation(s) you will make to support Focus Student 1 (FS1), Focus Student 2 (FS2), and Focus Student 3 (FS3) to access and engage in the content of the lesson. oFS1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-82\"><p>I will give FS1 short, visible directions and a checklist. Before the task, we will review the target vocabulary, and sentence frames will support his written explanation. A role in a supportive group will give him a reason to practice the language aloud. I will check his understanding individually and clarify directions when needed so he can concentrate on comprehension.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-83\"><p>oFS2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-84\"><p>FS2 will read the article in smaller sections with guiding questions. A graphic organizer will help him connect the topic and evidence to a central idea. An active role, such as group reader, will draw on his interest in books. He may use the shared sentence frames, with prompts to extend his explanation. Speech-to-text will help him record an idea before editing it, reducing a writing barrier while retaining the comprehension work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-85\"><p>oFS3:</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c1-86\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 7 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see page 11 of the Instructional Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-87\"><p>FS3 will have a purposeful group role, such as discussion leader or summarizer, that uses her confidence in speaking. I will check that her humor supports the group rather than distracts it. Predictability, reassurance, and a clear responsibility will help her feel included while she continues developing comprehension and constructive peer relationships.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-88\"><p>\u2022Connections to Recent Content-Specific Learning: Explain how your adaptation(s) for FS1, FS2, and FS3 are informed by and connected to recent observations and/or assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing) as well as information related to the students\u2019 content-specific knowledge, skills, and/or abilities provided by your cooperating teacher and/or supervising faculty. oFS1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-89\"><p>FS1&#x27;s STAR result of grade 2.8 and EL level 3 support the need for scaffolds with seventh-grade texts and language. His work shows that he can identify important ideas and write a short summary, but directions often need individual clarification. The checklist, smaller steps, vocabulary preview, and conferences respond to those observed needs while building on his effort and interest in learning.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-90\"><p>oFS2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-91\"><p>FS2&#x27;s STAR result of grade 7.9 shows reading strength, while classroom work shows difficulty connecting details to a central idea. My observations and those of his case managers also identify attention needs. Preferred seating, speech-to-text, and headphones support access; shorter reading sections and an organizer address the particular comprehension task. I want to challenge his reading while giving him a structure for understanding it.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-92\"><p>oFS3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-93\"><p>FS3&#x27;s grade 6.6 STAR result, completed work, and classroom contributions show a solid foundation, especially with appropriately leveled texts. Her home circumstances, absences, and need for acceptance also point to the importance of consistency. A meaningful group role and check-ins will support connection to class while keeping the academic expectations in place.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-94\"><p>\u2022Leveraging Student Assets (Cultural and/or Linguistic) and/or Interests: Explain how the lesson leverages (builds on) FS1\u2019s, FS2\u2019s, and FS3\u2019s assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests to support content-specific learning and ALD learning goals. oFS1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-95\"><p>Basketball and sports can provide familiar examples for FS1 when discussing a central idea. His enjoyment of books and library activities also gives him material he wants to talk about. I will use those interests to create opportunities for explaining a text with demonstrates, suggests, and indicates.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-96\"><p>oFS2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-97\"><p>FS2 may draw a representation of the central idea before writing. This uses his interest in art to organize comprehension. His reading habits and productive relationship with FS1 will also support evidence-based discussion. Their shared book interests give them a natural setting for practicing the academic vocabulary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-98\"><p>oFS3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-99\"><p>I will connect FS3&#x27;s interest in travel, food, and personal stories with opportunities to discuss a text. A structured role will let her contribute her ideas and feel included while practicing academic explanations. Her enthusiasm for conversation can become a resource for both comprehension and language development.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-100\"><p>\u2022Learning Needs: Explain how your planned adaptation(s) will address the individual learning needs of each focus student. oFS1: Describe how the adaptation(s) will support FS1\u2019s academic language development (ALD) within the lesson through reading, writing, listening, and/or speaking.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-101\"><p>FS1 will encounter the vocabulary in a model, hear it in discussion, practice it orally, and use it in writing. Short directions and sentence frames will reduce the work of organizing a sentence so he can focus on what the text means. Group participation and individual feedback will help him use the terms for a real explanatory purpose.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-102\"><p>oFS2: Describe how the adaptation(s) will support FS2\u2019s content-specific learning need(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-103\"><p>The four central-idea options will focus FS2&#x27;s attention on evaluating evidence. Preferred seating, manageable reading sections, and an organizer will support concentration and comprehension. Speech-to-text and headphones will remain available when appropriate. Together, these supports let him use his reading strengths to develop a fuller summary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-104\"><p>oFS3: Describe how you will adapt the environment to support FS3\u2019s well-being and/or behavior in the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-105\"><p>FS3 will have a predictable, supportive environment and structured opportunities to use her speaking strengths. Discussion roles, frames, and connections to her interests can help her direct her social energy toward the task. I will continue checking in after absences and provide consistency so she can participate without feeling left out.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-106\"><p>Retained Source Materials</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-107\"><p>The following source pages retain the instructional materials, assessment tools, and student work as supplied.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image1.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 English: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image1.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 English: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image2.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 English: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image2.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 English: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image3.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 English: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image3.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 English: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image4.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 English: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image4.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 English: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image5.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 7 English: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/ss-c1-image5.png\" alt=\"Grade 7 English: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure>"}, {"id": "F", "title": "Part F Teaching Commentary", "html": "<div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c1-108\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt for the video clip(s) that you will submit (up to 8 pages). You must address each prompt at least once across all the video(s) submitted. Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 21\u201322 of the Instructional Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-109\"><p>Single Subject Step 2: Teach and Assess\u2014Part F: Commentary Template</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-110\"><p>In the commentary for your video clip(s), address the following prompts: \u2022Describe what you did to create and sustain a safe, positive learning environment. Were these strategies effective? Why or why not? \u2022Describe the UDL-focused strategy(ies) you used to actively engage the students in an asset-based content-specific lesson. oWhy did the strategy(ies), including promoting higher-order thinking, support students\u2019 progress toward meeting the content-specific learning goal? oWhy did the strategy(ies), including promoting higher-order thinking, support students\u2019 progress toward meeting the related content-specific ALD learning goal? \u2022What strategies did you use to monitor students\u2019 progress to support and/or advance their content-specific knowledge, skills, or abilities? Why? \u2022How did you adjust your instruction, and how did you respond intentionally to support student learning? Why were these intentional interactions supportive in helping the students make progress and/or advance their content-specific knowledge and related content-specific ALD?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-111\"><p>Video Clip 1: Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-112\"><p>At :20, I invite a volunteer to read background information about the author. At 1:05, I explain expectations for collaborative work so students know how to participate. At 1:40, I introduce the language goal and build definitions from what students already know, expanding partial answers with a clear model and encouragement.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-113\"><p>At 3:47, I revisit group expectations and annotation. When groups begin at 4:45, I hear them assigning responsibilities and circulate to clarify questions. A group outside the camera&#x27;s view needs redirection, so I return its attention to the learning goal on the board. These adjustments keep the environment supportive while helping students focus on the reading and language work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-114\"><p>Video Clip 2 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-115\"><p>I begin by reviewing the reading and inviting clarification about possible central ideas. At the moment labeled 44:41, I read and point out the four choices. From 1:16\u20132:00, I explain both worksheet pages and their sentence frames. At 2:30, I connect the choices to the paragraph&#x27;s final sentence.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-116\"><p>At 3:07:22, I encourage students to share their reasoning with the group. At 3:22, I remind them that I will circulate and help. When group work begins at 3:55, I listen for understanding and participation. At 3:57, I redirect a group that is drifting from the task; at 4:48, I clarify for another group. The audio at 5:04:80 records further support outside the camera&#x27;s frame.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-117\"><p>Reading the choices aloud, modeling the frames, and providing discussion time give students several ways to enter the task. I slow down, repeat a direction, or offer an example when their responses show a need, keeping the support connected to evidence and explanation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-118\"><p>Video Clip 3 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-119\"><p>At :15, I recognize that class is nearly over but still invite answers so students receive feedback that day. At 28:84, one student says he selected his central idea because I had eliminated two alternatives. His response shows participation, but it also tells me that he needs more support explaining a choice from the text itself.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-120\"><p>At 50:93, I redirect the class as the period closes. The bell at 1:25:47 interrupts the discussion, so I return to it the next day. Listening to students at the close gives me information for that follow-up and helps preserve a sense that their thinking matters.</p></div>"}, {"id": "G", "title": "Part G Reflection", "html": "<div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c1-121\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 7 pages). In your reflection, refer to evidence from Step 1 and/or Step 2 (e.g., Parts A\u2013F). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 26\u201327 of the Instructional Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-122\"><p>Single Subject Step 3: Reflect\u2014Part G: Written Narrative: Reflection on What You Learned Template 1. How effective was your asset-based, UDL-focused instructional approach in advancing your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the content-specific learning goal? How did you monitor and support students\u2019 progress? Explain why or why not the approach was effective.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-123\"><p>The lesson helped most students progress toward identifying a central idea and writing an objective summary. Group discussion let them work through the text before writing, and sentence frames supported organization. I monitored conversations and reviewed the written responses. Sixty-five percent earned at least 75% on the content assessment; other students showed partial success. These results support continuing the approach while targeting the explanation and writing that remain difficult.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-124\"><p>2. How effective was your asset-based, UDL-focused instructional approach in advancing your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the content-specific ALD learning goal? How did you monitor and support students\u2019 progress? Explain why or why not the approach was effective.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-125\"><p>The language goal asked students to connect evidence and ideas using precise terms. Modeling, sentence frames, and opportunities to talk before writing supported that work. About 80% earned at least 75% on the academic-language assignment, with partial progress among the remaining students. Reading, listening, discussion, and writing provided several opportunities to use the vocabulary in context, consistent with the planned UDL supports.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-126\"><p>3. Analyze the effectiveness of the adaptation(s) used to support each focus student\u2019s learning needs: a. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 1\u2019s language learning needs effectively support their progress toward the learning goals? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-127\"><p>FS1&#x27;s checklist, short directions, vocabulary preview, sentence frames, and individual conferences helped him participate and complete the task. He earned 72% on the objective summary and 76% on academic language. Those results show useful progress while also indicating that the skills are not yet fully secure. I would retain support and provide more practice using the vocabulary to explain an idea independently.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-128\"><p>b. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 2\u2019s content-specific learning need(s) effectively support their progress toward the learning goals? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-129\"><p>Smaller reading sections, guiding questions, an organizer, and access to his accommodations helped FS2 focus on meaning. Speech-to-text gave him a way to record ideas before revising. He earned 82% on the summary and 78% on academic language. The structure appears to have supported both comprehension and expression while allowing him to use his reading strengths.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-130\"><p>c. Did the adaptation(s) to support Focus Student 3\u2019s well-being and/or behavior in the content-specific lesson effectively support their progress toward the learning goals? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-131\"><p>FS3&#x27;s role in discussion and the predictable support after absences helped her stay connected to the lesson. She earned 70% on the summary and 83% on academic language. Her stronger language result shows that she can use precise vocabulary, while the summary result points to a need for more organization and focus. I would continue the social-emotional supports alongside that targeted academic work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-132\"><p>4. How did the safe, positive learning environment you created support the students\u2019 ability to engage in the content-specific lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-133\"><p>Small groups gave students room to ask questions and try an idea before speaking to the class. Clear choices and frames reduced uncertainty, while reading, talking, and writing offered different ways to engage. I circulated, recognized effort, and used brief checks to provide help before frustration grew. The 65% at or above 75% on the content assessment, along with partial success elsewhere, suggests that the environment supported participation and progress.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-134\"><p>5. How did the students\u2019 information you provided in Part A (Written Narrative: Contextual Information) inform your content-specific lesson planning and teaching? a. Prior content-specific learning</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-135\"><p>Students already had experience finding central ideas, writing summaries, and discussing texts through gallery walks and Socratic Seminars. I built on those routines with both collaborative and individual work. Their earlier summaries and feedback helped me identify where the new lesson needed stronger support for explanation and academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-136\"><p>b. Students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-137\"><p>Students&#x27; cultural backgrounds and interests guided my attention to varied perspectives and texts about society and culture. Their preference for collaboration shaped the group activities, while independent options supported students who work more comfortably alone. I wanted students to draw on familiar experiences while encountering ideas beyond their own.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-138\"><p>c. Students\u2019 learning needs</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-139\"><p>Earlier work showed that students needed more consistent use of demonstrates, suggests, and indicates. I made those words part of the models, frames, and discussion prompts. Partner and group work also provided practice taking turns, asking relevant questions, and building on another person&#x27;s idea, connecting with ELD.PI.7.1Br.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-140\"><p>6. How did you respond intentionally to your students and adjust your instruction to provide \u201cjust-in-time\u201d support to advance their content-specific development?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-141\"><p>I watched for where students became stuck and responded with a model, a shorter explanation, a written frame, or encouragement to speak. When vocabulary use was unclear, I supplied an example in context. During discussion, I supported quieter students and redirected attention to the text. These immediate adjustments let students continue working on the central idea rather than remain blocked by the language or directions.</p></div>"}, {"id": "H", "title": "Part H Next Steps", "html": "<div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-ss-c1-142\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 4 pages). Refer to evidence from Step 1, Step 2, and/or Step 3 (e.g., Parts A\u2013G) to support your responses. Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see page 30 of the Instructional Cycle 1 Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-143\"><p>Single Subject Step 4: Apply\u2014Part H: Narrative: Application of What You Learned Template 1. Based on what you learned from the content-specific lesson you taught during this cycle, do you need to re-teach the students any part of the content-specific lesson in the next lesson you plan to teach? Explain why or why not. a.For the group of students:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-144\"><p>I would reteach the connection between a central idea and the evidence that supports it. Many students could choose an answer, especially with four options available, but some could not explain why it fit. Others still relied heavily on frames. The closing exchange in Clip 3 helped make that need visible. I would model an evidence-based explanation and provide practice with gradually less support while keeping scaffolds available where needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-145\"><p>b. For Focus Student 1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-146\"><p>FS1 needs focused practice using academic vocabulary to explain a central idea. His 72% summary result and 76% language result show progress and continuing need. I will keep the short directions, checklist, vocabulary preparation, and conferences, then gradually reduce the language I supply. A small group with supportive peers can give him repeated opportunities to rehearse an explanation before writing it independently.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-147\"><p>c. For Focus Student 2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-148\"><p>FS2 does not need the full lesson repeated. His 82% summary and 78% language results support more targeted work on connecting evidence to the central idea and explaining that connection in depth. I will retain attention supports while asking him to move beyond naming the topic or repeating a detail.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-149\"><p>d. For Focus Student 3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-150\"><p>FS3 needs practice organizing and focusing a summary, rather than a complete reteaching of the lesson. Her 83% language result is a strength to build on, while the 70% summary result identifies the next area of work. I will keep the predictable environment and purposeful discussion roles and add focused summary practice so academic growth continues alongside social-emotional support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-151\"><p>2. How will you continue to use UDL to engage students in content-specific learning?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-152\"><p>I will keep multiple ways to read, discuss, and express understanding, including collaborative and independent options. The reviewed choices and frames in Clip 2 at 44:41 and 1:16\u20132:00 helped students begin. The narrowed choices in Clip 3 at 28:84 helped one student participate, while showing the need to develop his own justification. I will use these scaffolds responsively and reduce them as students become more independent.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-153\"><p>3. What additional higher-order thinking skill(s) will you engage students in to advance their content-specific learning?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-154\"><p>I will have students compare interpretations, analyze how two pieces of evidence connect to an idea, and evaluate which support is stronger. They will need to explain a judgment rather than simply locate a matching detail. These tasks will deepen the reading work and give students a clearer purpose for an objective summary.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-155\"><p>4. How will you continue to advance these students\u2019 related content-specific ALD?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-156\"><p>Academic vocabulary will remain part of both discussion and writing. I will model words in context, provide frames, word banks, or organizers when needed, and give feedback on the precision of an explanation. Listening to conversations and reviewing writing will help me decide when to strengthen or reduce the language support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-ss-c1-157\"><p>5. How will you continue to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to their content-specific learning?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-ss-c1-158\"><p>I will select informational texts and examples that connect with students&#x27; cultures, languages, and interests. Students will have opportunities to contribute relevant experiences and learn from perspectives beyond their own. Those connections can make reading more meaningful and show how the knowledge they bring from home supports academic work.</p></div>"}], "images": ["assets/examples/ss-c1-image1.png", "assets/examples/ss-c1-image2.png", "assets/examples/ss-c1-image3.png", "assets/examples/ss-c1-image4.png", "assets/examples/ss-c1-image5.png"], "responseCount": 66, "words": 5441, "historical": false}, "mmsn-math": {"id": "mmsn-math", "path": "mmsn-math", "title": "Grade 2 addition and subtraction", "file": "07_MMSN_Grade_2_Math_Rewritten.docx", "source": "EXAMPLE_VI MMSN MPA (2nd Grade) Sample Submission.docx", "parts": [{"id": "A", "title": "Part A Contextual Information", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-1\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 1: Plan\u2014Part A: Written Narrative: Contextual Information Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-2\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 9 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-3\"><p>Contextual Information About the Group of Students</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-4\"><p>Directions: Provide contextual information for the group of students you plan to teach during this math lesson. For more information, see pages 9\u201311 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-5\"><p>\u2022 Age range(s) and grade level(s)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-6\"><p>I will teach three second graders, ages seven and eight. They are building number sense and beginning strategies for adding and subtracting within 20. Predictable routines, visual models, and guided work with materials help them participate.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-7\"><p>\u2022 Student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to the math content you plan to teach</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-8\"><p>All three students have experience with English and Spanish, and two are bilingual Latino students. One often counts in Spanish, which gives us another way to work through the mathematics. Their interests include K-Pop Demon Hunters, Spiderman, and cats. I will use those characters as moving pieces on a number line, combining familiar interests with the visual and hands-on approaches that help them learn.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-9\"><p>\u2022 Math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-10\"><p>The group is developing counting, number recognition, and an understanding of how quantities combine and separate. Students can work with single-digit problems and are beginning to use drawings, manipulatives, and number lines, though they still need prompts. I will model the process, guide their practice, and give immediate feedback as they extend these strategies to problems within 20.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-11\"><p>\u2022 Identified English learners</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-12\"><p>Two students are English learners. One primarily uses Spanish at home and school and is making progress in spoken English. The other prefers English at home but often does not speak at school. Visuals, modeling, and sentence openings will help both students engage with the mathematical language without relying only on an oral explanation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-13\"><p>\u2022 Identified learning needs (e.g., special education eligibility of the students)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-14\"><p>The students receive special education services under SLD, Autism, and Emotional Disability. They need repeated practice, explicit modeling, and concrete or visual tools. Movement and hands-on work help direct their energy into the task. Some are also learning to ask for assistance through gestures, writing, or responses to an adult. I will plan opportunities for those forms of communication alongside the math instruction.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-15\"><p>\u2022 Instructional delivery model (e.g., co-teaching, inclusion, self-contained classrooms, small group, state special schools)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-16\"><p>I will work with the three students in a small pull-out group. Their IEP math goals share a focus on addition and subtraction within 20. This setting lets me observe each student&#x27;s strategy closely, respond promptly, and adjust the amount of support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-17\"><p>\u2022 Instructional support personnel with whom you will collaborate and/or who will assist you in this lesson (e.g., paraprofessionals, instructional aides, cooperating teacher, interpreters, bilingual instructional assistants, speech and language pathologists)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-18\"><p>I will teach this session on my own. Outside the lesson, I regularly exchange observations with the aides who support these students. We coordinate tools and language across the pull-out and general education settings so students encounter familiar strategies. The lesson itself includes visuals, manipulatives, modeling, and guided practice to make that support available.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-19\"><p>\u2022 Language(s) of instruction to be used in the lesson (e.g., biliteracy, developmental bilingual [one-way immersion], dual language [two-way immersion], English only)</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-20\"><p>I will teach in English and clarify vocabulary or directions in Spanish when needed. Gestures, visual models, and manipulatives will give students additional ways to follow the addition and subtraction work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-21\"><p>Focus Students</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-22\"><p>Directions: Provide contextual information for the three focus students you selected. For more information, see pages 11\u201313 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. Focus Student 1</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-23\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-24\"><p>FS1 is an eight-year-old second grader developing both early math skills and English proficiency. A small group, visual tools, guided practice, and access to Spanish help them engage with problems within 20.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-25\"><p>\u2022 Assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-26\"><p>FS1 is curious, caring, and proud of speaking Spanish. Family stories about cousins, a parent, and grandparents are important to them. They also enjoy drawing and Spiderman. I will welcome Spanish when they need to clarify an idea and use a Spiderman counter to connect the number-line activity with an interest they bring to class.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-27\"><p>\u2022 If the FS is an English learner , current proficiency in English and proficiency in their primary language</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-28\"><p>FS1 expresses ideas more independently in Spanish. In English, they are developing everyday vocabulary, using individual words and short phrases, and learning to read and write CVC words with support. Their MAP reading scores were 154 in English and 163 in Spanish. The overall summative ELPAC score was 1398, described as beginning to develop in listening, reading, and writing. I will pair English instruction with Spanish clarification, a number line, and guided practice.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-29\"><p>\u2022 Math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-30\"><p>FS1 can count in Spanish and to 30 in English, and recognizes most numbers through 50. Their latest MAP math score is 161. Drawings and number lines help them solve addition and subtraction problems with assistance. Repeated, hands-on practice will support greater accuracy and confidence within 20.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-31\"><p>\u2022 Special education eligibility and the impact on FS1\u2019s academic language development (ALD) and math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-32\"><p>FS1&#x27;s eligibility is Specific Learning Disability. They need directions restated and concepts revisited before applying a new skill. I will combine questions, manipulatives, visuals, and Spanish clarification so they can work on number sense while developing English math vocabulary. These supports will help me see what they understand and where another explanation is needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-33\"><p>\u2022 IEP goal(s) related to the math content, math practices, and/or math academic language that will be addressed in the lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-34\"><p>FS1&#x27;s math goal involves solving addition and subtraction within 20 with strategies such as objects, drawings, and other visual supports. I will review the vocabulary, model my thinking, and give them time to try the number-line strategy with guidance. Questions and an exit ticket will help me monitor their progress toward using those tools more independently.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-35\"><p>\u2022 Additional supports reflected in FS1\u2019s IEP that may support their progress toward the math and ALD learning goals (e.g., behavior plan, current services, assistive technology, and/or augmentative and alternative communication [AAC])</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-36\"><p>Along with specialized academic instruction, FS1 receives speech therapy for articulation and narrative skills and occupational therapy for fine motor development. Those services support explaining a mathematical process and recording numbers during the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-37\"><p>Focus Student 2</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-38\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-39\"><p>FS2 is eight and attends second grade. Selective mutism affects spoken participation. They can take part in the small-group math work through visual tools, objects, and responses that do not require speech.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-40\"><p>\u2022 Student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-41\"><p>FS2 is a quiet, kind student who uses art, writing, and gestures to communicate. Drawing, cats, and anime are strong interests. I will include a preferred character and opportunities to represent a solution visually, giving FS2 a comfortable way to participate in the number-line work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-42\"><p>\u2022 Math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-43\"><p>FS2 recognizes and counts numbers through 100 and solves single-digit addition and subtraction with support. Their MAP math score is 159. A number line and repeated practice improve accuracy. Because counting aloud is not usually available to them at school, I will make the movement of a counter clear and observable, then guide practice as needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-44\"><p>\u2022 Special education eligibility and the impact on FS2\u2019s math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-45\"><p>FS2 receives services under Emotional Disability. Selective mutism limits verbal explanations and oral counting; they usually communicate by nodding, pointing, gesturing, or occasionally whispering to a trusted adult. I will use hands-on and visual tasks with nonverbal response options so I can assess the mathematics without making speech a condition of participation.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-46\"><p>\u2022 IEP goal(s) related to the math content, math practices, and/or math academic language that will be addressed in the lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-47\"><p>FS2&#x27;s goal is to solve addition and subtraction within 20. They will choose an object to move along the number line, making each jump visible. My questions and prompts will connect add, subtract, forward, and backward with those actions. This will let FS2 demonstrate a strategy and communicate mathematical understanding without needing to speak.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-48\"><p>\u2022 Additional supports reflected in FS2\u2019s IEP that may support their progress toward the math and ALD learning goals (e.g., behavior plan, current services, assistive technology, and/or augmentative and alternative communication [AAC])</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-49\"><p>FS2 receives counseling for anxiety and social-emotional needs and speech services addressing communication and volume. Their accommodations include extra time, reduced pressure to speak, and nonverbal ways to respond. I will use those supports during math so they can show understanding and continue working toward the IEP goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-50\"><p>Focus Student 3</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-51\"><p>\u2022 Age and grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-52\"><p>FS3 is a seven-year-old second grader. Guided small-group work, visual models, concrete materials, and movement breaks support their developing addition and subtraction skills.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-53\"><p>\u2022 Student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-54\"><p>FS3 brings humor, kindness, and energy to the group. They enjoy talking about family and K-Pop Demon Hunters. I will use those interests in the visual materials while helping them replace negative comments with positive ways of interacting. Character counters will connect their enthusiasm with the math task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-55\"><p>\u2022 Math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-56\"><p>FS3 is working below grade level in number sense. They identify numbers to 50 and need support with larger numbers. Their latest MAP math score was 146. Drawings and a number line help them begin adding and subtracting. I will guide them in choosing an operation, deciding which direction to move, and checking the result.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-57\"><p>\u2022 Special education eligibility and the impact on FS3\u2019s math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-58\"><p>FS3&#x27;s eligibility is Autism. They need support with social communication, attention, flexibility, and appropriate group responses. A strong interest can sometimes draw attention away from the current task, and a difficult problem can interrupt persistence. Clear routines, structured lessons, and visual models help them understand expectations and remain involved in the mathematics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-59\"><p>\u2022 Learning needs related to life experience(s) that may result in a need for additional adaptation(s) to support FS3\u2019s well-being and/or behavior during the math lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-60\"><p>Changes in routine and challenging work can leave FS3 frustrated or discouraged. At times, that leads to negative comments or difficulty staying regulated. I will provide predictable steps, clear expectations, reminders about positive language, and opportunities to pause or move so they can return to the task successfully.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-61\"><p>\u2022 IEP goal(s) related to well-being and/or behavior (e.g., attention, engaging with activities) that would impact their access to the math lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-62\"><p>FS3 is working on reducing inappropriate comments and gaining attention through conversation, compliments, or invitations to play. I will reinforce kindness in our daily affirmations, state expectations clearly, and use brief shake-out or movement breaks. These routines support appropriate interaction and participation in math.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-63\"><p>\u2022 Additional supports reflected in FS3\u2019s IEP that may support their progress toward the math and ALD learning goals (e.g., behavior plan, current services, assistive technology, and/or augmentative and alternative communication [AAC])</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-64\"><p>FS3 receives weekly speech and language services for expressive and receptive language and peer interaction, along with counseling for self-regulation. Visual supports, extra time, and preferential seating are additional accommodations. Together, these supports help with communication, frustration, attention, and task completion during the lesson.</p></div>"}, {"id": "B", "title": "Part B Lesson Planning", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-65\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 1: Plan\u2014Part B: Sample Math Lesson Plan Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-66\"><p>Directions: If you do not have access to a school or district lesson plan format, consider using this sample lesson plan template. Use the knowledge gathered about your students to establish age-level (PK/TK) or grade-level (K\u2013ATP) appropriate math-specific learning goals: one goal that supports math content and practice and one goal that supports math ALD. Based on those learning goals, plan one asset-based, UDL-focused math lesson. Provide answers to the following prompts by typing your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 10 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 14\u201319 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. Title of lesson</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-67\"><p>Using a Number Line to Add and Subtract Within 20</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-68\"><p>Subject</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-69\"><p>Elementary mathematics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-70\"><p>Grade level</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-71\"><p>Second grade.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-72\"><p>1. What are your goals for the math lesson? a. Select a Math Standard 1 and a Math Practice: Based on the students\u2019 prior learning, recent information you reviewed, student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests, learning needs, and IEP goal(s): i. For PK/TK: From the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations, select:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-73\"><p>\u25cf one age-level developmental strand:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-74\"><p>N/A</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-75\"><p>\u25cf one Mathematical Practice:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-76\"><p>N/A</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-77\"><p>ii. For K\u2013ATP: From the California Common Core State Standards Math, select:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-78\"><p>\u25cf one grade-level Mathematical Content Standard:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-79\"><p>Domain: Operations &amp; Algebraic Thinking 2.OA. Cluster: Add and subtract within 20. Standard 1: Fluently add and subtract within 20 using mental strategies. By the end of Grade 2, know from memory all sums of two one-digit numbers.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-80\"><p>The standard calls for mental calculation. My students will use number lines as an intermediate support while developing the foundational understanding needed for that work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-81\"><p>\u25cf one Math Practice (MP):</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-82\"><p>MP1: Make sense of problems and persevere in solving them.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-83\"><p>\u25cf Optional: If the student(s) are eligible for the California Alternate Assessments (CAA) for Math, or are eligible for an alternate curriculum as a part of their IEP, use the Core Content Connectors (CCCs) aligned with the grade level of the student(s) to plan your lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-84\"><p>N/A</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-85\"><p>b. Develop a Math Content and Practice Learning Goal: Based on the grade-level standard(s) you selected, write one developmentally appropriate math learning goal that includes both math content and practice (see examples on pages 17\u201318 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-86\"><p>Students will practice addition and subtraction within 20 (1.0A.6), using a number line to increase accuracy (MP5). They will explain or demonstrate the process through their movements on the line, making their reasoning visible (MP3).</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-87\"><p>2. Develop a Math ALD Learning Goal: Based on the relevant math academic vocabulary needed for the lesson, create a math ALD goal that supports the math content and practice learning goal (see example on page 19 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-88\"><p>Students will connect mathematical terms\u2014including add, subtract, plus, forward, and backward\u2014to a number-line solution. They may communicate the process through speech, writing, gestures, or movement, using the language and actions to show how they reached an answer.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-89\"><p>3. What will the students do in the math lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-90\"><p>Introduction, 6 minutes: We will begin with our familiar check-in, celebrations, affirmations, and visual schedule. This gives students a chance to express how they feel and see what comes next. We will review number flashcards and the meanings of addition and subtraction, then return to the number line using Rumi, Spiderman, and a cat.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-91\"><p>Modeling and guided practice, 15\u201320 minutes: I will demonstrate 7 + 4 on the smart board, thinking aloud about the starting number, direction, and four jumps that lead to 11. Each student will then choose a character and solve a problem at the board. They will decide how to move and explain or show their reasoning. For individual practice, they will use a pencil or character on the printed number lines. I will observe and clarify as needed, accepting pointing and gestures as well as speech.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-92\"><p>Assessment, 10 minutes: The exit ticket has two addition and two subtraction problems. I will check the selected operation, direction, number of jumps, and recorded answer, noting the support each student needs. Those observations will guide the next lesson and our work toward their IEP goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-93\"><p>Closure, 3 minutes: We will review what happens when we add or subtract and connect each operation to its direction on the line. Students may answer verbally or through gestures.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-94\"><p>a. What instructional materials and/or resources will you use in the math lesson? Why?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-95\"><p>I will use number lines on the screen, practice materials, and worksheet, along with number flashcards and laminated character counters. The different formats keep the same strategy visible across modeling and independent work. Moving a counter makes direction and quantity concrete and connects the lesson to students&#x27; interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-96\"><p>4. What UDL-focused instructional strategies will you use during the math lesson to support student learning? Describe how you will: a. Connect the content of this lesson to students\u2019 recent math learning.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-97\"><p>I will revisit the numbers, vocabulary, and number-line strategy students have already used. Presenting familiar ideas visually will help them connect earlier learning with the new problems. That continuity should make the work feel more manageable and reduce frustration.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-98\"><p>b. Create a safe, positive learning environment. 2 2 For example, take intellectual risks; foster positive attitudes toward mathematics; encourage students\u2019 curiosity and/or persistence; physical safety; providing a barrier-free space for independent mobility; sensory accommodations</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-99\"><p>Our predictable opening routine will include an emotional check-in and affirmations about kindness, persistence, and trying again after mistakes. I want students to enter the task knowing that they can share how they feel and take a risk without being embarrassed. These routines support the engagement they need for mathematical work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-100\"><p>c. Leverage your students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-101\"><p>Students may choose Rumi, Spiderman, or a cat as their counter. That choice brings their interests into the task and gives them some control over participation. I will present number lines on the smart board, a mat, and the printed worksheet so they can use the same idea at different sizes and in different forms.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-102\"><p>d. Support the math ALD of your students.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-103\"><p>I will explicitly connect the operation symbols with add, subtract, forward, and backward. During modeling, I will say what I am doing as I move the character. Guided practice will let students use the same vocabulary with support. During individual work, they may show its meaning through writing, speech, or movement, with English vocabulary clarified when needed.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-104\"><p>e. Engage students in age and/or developmentally appropriate higher-order thinking 3 to deepen their understanding of math content.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-105\"><p>I will ask students to predict how a number changes, choose a direction, and decide how many spaces to move. They will explain or demonstrate why their movement fits the operation. Linking a symbol, a change in quantity, and an action on the number line gives them a developmentally appropriate way to reason about a solution.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-106\"><p>f. Monitor the students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math content and practice and math ALD learning goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-107\"><p>I will watch starting points, directions, and counted jumps throughout the lesson and listen for the math vocabulary students use. Questions and specific feedback will help them notice and correct an error during practice. The exit ticket will add individual evidence of accuracy and strategy use, helping me plan the next level of support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-108\"><p>g. Collaborate with and/or facilitate instructional support personnel to support students\u2019 access.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-109\"><p>Before and after instruction, I will compare observations and student work with the aides and coordinate the language and tools used in other settings. I will also consult the speech-language pathologist about vocabulary and communication supports and the counselor about FS3&#x27;s regulation strategies. Shared approaches to prompting and modeling will help students use the strategy more consistently and independently.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-110\"><p>h. If applicable, integrate functional and/or life skills into the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-111\"><p>The check-in gives students practice recognizing feelings and managing frustration. Throughout the lesson, they will communicate a process, follow a routine, and use materials for a purpose. They can do this through speech, gestures, or movement. These habits support independence, while the addition and subtraction skills have practical uses beyond the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-112\"><p>Template notes</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-113\"><p>1 Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use age-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF).</p></div>"}, {"id": "C", "title": "Part C Planning Narrative", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-114\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 1: Plan\u2014Part C: Written Narrative: Math Lesson Adaptation(s) for Focus Students Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-115\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 7 pages). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see page 19\u201320 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-116\"><p>\u2022 Adaptation(s): Determine what adaptation(s) you will make to support Focus Student 1 (FS1), Focus Student 2 (FS2), and Focus Student 3 (FS3) to access and engage with the content of the math lesson. o FS1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-117\"><p>I will teach FS1 the essential vocabulary directly and provide repeated opportunities to use it. Visuals, number-line models, and gestures will connect add and subtract to their symbols and to quantities becoming larger or smaller. Guided repetition will let FS1 practice the English terms while engaging with the mathematics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-118\"><p>o FS2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-119\"><p>For FS2, I will pair explicit modeling with a visible number line. I will think aloud while moving a character, then let FS2 practice the same steps with a chosen counter. The board, mat, and worksheet will carry the strategy through the lesson. This makes the thinking observable without requiring speech and gives me a way to check understanding while reducing pressure related to selective mutism.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-120\"><p>o FS3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-121\"><p>FS3 will have a predictable sequence, a visual schedule, and posted expectations. A feelings chart and sentence frame will support the opening check-in. During math, a tangible counter and number line will make the task clear. These supports are intended to help FS3 regulate, anticipate what comes next, and stay involved.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-122\"><p>\u2022 Connections to Recent Math Learning: Explain how your adaptation(s) for FS1, FS2, and FS3 are informed by and connected to recent observations and/or math assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), IEP information, as well as information related to the students\u2019 math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities provided by your cooperating teacher, supervising faculty, service providers, and/or families/guardians. o FS1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-123\"><p>FS1&#x27;s MAP math score rose ten points to 161. Recent goal monitoring showed three of five problems correct in two of four trials. Their counting and number recognition have improved, but observations show a continuing need for repeated explanations and vocabulary support. I will use modeling, Spanish clarification, and guided number-line practice to build on that progress toward the IEP goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-124\"><p>o FS2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-125\"><p>FS2&#x27;s MAP score was 159, and recent IEP monitoring showed three of four correct in one of three trials. I have observed more accurate work when they can use a number line with support. A chosen counter will let FS2 rehearse that strategy and show the process without speaking, while giving me clear evidence of progress.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-126\"><p>o FS3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-127\"><p>FS3&#x27;s MAP math score was 146, although guessing during tablet testing may limit how well that score represents their knowledge. In goal monitoring, they answered four of five correctly in one of three trials. Classroom observations show success with guidance and visual support. I will continue structured number-line practice and consult the counselor about regulation so the opening check-in and math supports address current needs.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-128\"><p>\u2022 Leveraging Student Assets (Cultural and/or Linguistic) and/or Interests: Explain how the math lesson plan incorporates and leverages (builds on) FS1\u2019s, FS2\u2019s, and FS3\u2019s assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests to support math learning and ALD learning goals. o FS1:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-129\"><p>FS1 can draw on Spanish while practicing English math language. I will make that connection visible through demonstrations and allow practice in both languages. The Spiderman counter adds a familiar interest to the number-line work and gives FS1 a concrete way to show a solution.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-130\"><p>o FS2:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-131\"><p>FS2&#x27;s strength in visual and hands-on work will guide the response options. They may point, gesture, write, or move a cat counter to show the calculation. That familiar interest and the opportunity to work without speaking should support both engagement and clearer evidence of understanding.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-132\"><p>o FS3:</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-133\"><p>I will use FS3&#x27;s interest in K-Pop Demon Hunters by including Rumi as a counter. Moving the character forward or backward will connect that interest to addition and subtraction. The visual routine and guided practice will help FS3 sustain attention and connect mathematical words with actions.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-134\"><p>\u2022 Learning Needs: Explain how your planned adaptation(s) will address the individual learning needs of each focus student. o FS1: Describe how the adaptation(s) will support FS1\u2019s academic language development (ALD) within the math lesson through reading, writing, listening/viewing, and/or speaking/signing.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-135\"><p>FS1 will hear the math terms in my think-aloud, see them connected to symbols and movement, and practice explaining a step with support. Reading and recording numbers will accompany the visual work. Immediate feedback and opportunities to use Spanish will help them access the task while continuing to develop English academic language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-136\"><p>o FS2: Describe how the adaptation(s) will support FS2\u2019s specific math learning need(s).</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-137\"><p>For FS2, I will keep the mathematical task accessible through a small-group setting, repeated modeling, manipulatives, and a number line. They will be able to demonstrate each step without an oral explanation. This addresses the accuracy needs while reducing the anxiety associated with speaking.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-138\"><p>o FS3: Describe how you will adapt the environment to support FS3\u2019s well-being and/or behavior so that this student can engage in the math lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-139\"><p>FS3&#x27;s housing instability, home circumstances, and recent family changes can affect attention and emotional regulation. I will provide a consistent learning environment through predictable routines and visual expectations. Guided practice and clear representations will support confidence and sustained attention, allowing FS3 to engage with both the mathematics and its language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-140\"><p>\u2022 IEP Goal(s): Describe how you will support FS1\u2019s, FS2\u2019s, and FS3\u2019s progress toward meeting their related IEP goal(s). o FS1: Describe how you will support FS1\u2019s progress toward their IEP goal(s) related to the math content, math practices, and/or math academic language that will be addressed in the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-141\"><p>FS1 will use the number line to practice the addition and subtraction strategies in the IEP goal. I will model explicitly, review vocabulary, and provide guided support. The exit slip will show which steps are becoming secure and where future lessons should focus.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-142\"><p>o FS2: Describe how you will support FS2\u2019s progress toward their IEP goal(s) related to the math content, math practices, and/or math academic language that will be addressed in the lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-143\"><p>FS2 will solve problems within 20 with a number line and cat counter. I have seen better accuracy with this visual strategy. Making the jumps visible may help with errors that occur when oral counting is unavailable. Repeated practice and nonverbal responses will support the IEP math goal while accommodating selective mutism.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-144\"><p>o FS3: Describe how you will support FS3\u2019s progress toward their IEP goal(s) related to well-being and/or behavior (e.g., attention, engaging with activities) that would impact their access to the math lesson.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-145\"><p>I will support FS3&#x27;s participation through a feelings check-in, familiar routines, visual expectations, and opportunities for a classroom responsibility. Guided work with Rumi on the number line will connect those behavioral supports to the math task. The aim is to strengthen attention, positive interaction, and confidence in completing the work.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-146\"><p>Retained Source Materials</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-147\"><p>The following source pages retain the instructional materials, assessment tools, and student work as supplied.</p></div><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image1.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image1.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image2.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image2.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image3.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image3.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image4.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image4.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image5.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image5.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image6.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image6.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image7.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image7.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"example-artifact\"><button class=\"image-expand\" data-image=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image8.png\" data-caption=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: retained instructional artifact\" aria-label=\"Enlarge example artifact\"><img src=\"assets/examples/mmsn-math-image8.png\" alt=\"Grade 2 addition and subtraction: artifact retained in the supplied rewritten example\" loading=\"lazy\"><span>View full screen</span></button><figcaption>Artifact retained in the supplied example. Read it alongside the response.</figcaption></figure>"}, {"id": "F", "title": "Part F Teaching Commentary", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-148\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 2: Teach and Assess\u2014Part F: Commentary Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-149\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt for the video(s) you submitted (up to 8 pages). You must address each prompt at least once across all the video(s) submitted. Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 30\u201331 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. In your commentary for your video clip(s), address the following prompts:</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-150\"><p>\u25cf Describe what you did to create and sustain a safe, positive learning environment. Were these strategies effective? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-151\"><p>\u25cf Describe the UDL-focused strategy(ies) you used to actively engage the students in a math lesson that leverages students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests. o Why did the strategy(ies), including promoting developmentally appropriate higher-order thinking, support your focus students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math content and practice learning goal? o Why did the strategy(ies), including promoting developmentally appropriate higher-order thinking, support your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math ALD learning goal? o Why did the strategy(ies) support your students\u2019 progress toward meeting their IEP goals related to math content, math practices, math academic language, and/or well-being and/or behavior?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-152\"><p>\u25cf What strategies did you use to monitor the students\u2019 progress to support and/or advance their math content knowledge and/or math practice? Why?</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-153\"><p>\u25cf How did you adjust your instruction, and how did you respond intentionally to support student learning? Why were these intentional interactions supportive in helping the students make progress toward meeting the math content and practice and math ALD learning goals? Video 1: Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-154\"><p>From 0:00\u20131:30, I begin with our social-emotional check-in. Students can use the feelings visuals and sentence frame, but they may also gesture or use Spanish. At 0:22, FS1 begins explaining happiness in Spanish. At 1:12, I offer FS2 a thumbs-up or thumbs-down response. These options make participation possible without placing the same speaking demand on everyone.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-155\"><p>At 2:13, the familiar characters draw students into the math, especially FS3. Around 2:30, my questions connect the movement to words such as hop, forward, and backward. I watch each student&#x27;s actions and responses to decide when another prompt is needed. At 3:15, I guide FS3 back to an error through a question; they laugh and keep trying. At 3:55, FS3 thanks FS2, an example of the positive interaction we practice through our affirmations.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-156\"><p>The check-in, encouragement, and choices helped students enter the lesson and persist. The number line made their thinking visible, while the language options supported communication. Together, these approaches addressed the math goals and the participation and behavioral goals that allow students to access instruction.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-157\"><p>Video 2 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-158\"><p>At 0:15, I review symbols and vocabulary with gestures, making plus and minus with my arms and showing quantities becoming larger or smaller with my hands. At 1:22, I model the number-line process explicitly. The visual and physical representations connect the operation, direction, and number of jumps, giving students a concrete basis for deciding how to solve a problem.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-159\"><p>The character choices help sustain interest while students practice. FS3 connects movement and direction in an explanation. At 4:22, FS2 points to the starting place and direction and gestures to show the quantity increasing. At 5:00, FS1 uses prompting to explain that Spiderman moved six spaces backward. These responses let students communicate mathematical meaning through different means.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-160\"><p>I monitor both the calculation and how students represent the vocabulary. Asking about direction and the number of jumps helps me identify who can continue alone and who needs a scaffold. FS1 and FS3 still need support at points, but the demonstrations let them participate and practice the addition and subtraction strategies in their IEP goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-161\"><p>Video 3 (if submitted): Describe what is happening in the video and provide a rationale for what you are doing and why. Address all of the commentary prompts that relate to this video.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-162\"><p>From 0:00\u20130:32, students finish guided practice before moving to their own number lines. I respond calmly to mistakes, recognize effort, and let them retain a chosen character. That continuity supports the move from my earlier modeling to individual work.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-163\"><p>During 1:04\u20133:58, I circulate as students complete the exit slip. FS3 uses Rumi and describes the movement. FS2 uses the cat, pointing and gesturing to show a starting point, direction, and result. FS1 needs additional time and guidance, including encouragement to count in Spanish. These different responses help me see their mathematical thinking without making spoken English the only measure.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-164\"><p>FS2 and FS3 show accurate number-line work, with FS3 needing scaffolds earlier. FS1 requires more immediate support. Observation, questions, repeated modeling, and prompts let me respond to the particular step causing difficulty. The lesson supports progress toward the math and language goals while showing that accuracy and independence are still developing.</p></div>"}, {"id": "G", "title": "Part G Reflection", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-165\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 3: Reflect\u2014Part G: Written Narrative: Reflection on What You Learned Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-166\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 7 pages). In your reflection, refer to evidence from Step 1 and/or Step 2 (e.g., Parts A\u2013F). Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see pages 35\u201336 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. 1. How effective was your asset-based, UDL-focused instructional approach in advancing your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math content and practice learning goal? How did you monitor and support students\u2019 progress? Explain why or why not the approach was effective.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-167\"><p>The visual number line, gesture-supported symbols, and character choices helped students engage with addition and subtraction within 20. Their interest is visible in Videos 1\u20133. I monitored how they moved the counters and reviewed their exit tickets.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-168\"><p>FS1 initially recorded the number of jumps rather than the landing number. FS3 also needed help with the number of spaces. Questions helped both reconsider the process, while FS2 showed more independent use of the line. Two students solved three of four problems correctly on their own. I see progress from the earlier session and goal monitoring, but the need for prompts means the group has not yet reached complete independent mastery.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-169\"><p>2. How effective was your asset-based, UDL-focused instructional approach in advancing your students\u2019 progress toward meeting the math ALD learning goal? How did you monitor and support students\u2019 progress? Explain why or why not the approach was effective.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-170\"><p>Students could connect add, subtract, forward, and backward to a visible action even when an oral explanation was difficult. In Video 2 at 4:25, FS2 shows the starting number and movement by pointing. At 5:00, FS1 uses vocabulary after prompting. FS3 combines gestures and spoken explanation.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-171\"><p>I checked language understanding through questions and observation during guided and individual work. Every student communicated through at least one mode, although the vocabulary was not consistently accurate or independent. I would continue those access options while adding opportunities to explain in speech or writing and to name a direction more precisely than this way or that way.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-172\"><p>3. How effective was your collaboration with and/or facilitation of instructional support personnel to support student access? Explain why or why not the collaboration and/or facilitation was effective.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-173\"><p>The aides&#x27; input helped me plan useful supports even though they did not teach in the recorded lesson. One aide reported that FS3 confused the symbols, so I included gestures linking plus and minus with the corresponding movement. FS3 still needed help counting spaces but showed a clearer sense of direction during practice. Coordinating number lines, prompts, and vocabulary also made the tools familiar. Afterward, I shared the remaining needs shown in the guided work and exit tickets.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-174\"><p>4. How did the safe, positive learning environment 1 you created support the students\u2019 ability to engage in the math lesson?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-175\"><p>Our check-in, affirmations, and agenda gave the group a familiar beginning. FS3 shared happiness about a new baby sister, and I responded with a silent cheer. Gentle reminders about hand raising, encouragement after mistakes, and character choices helped students stay involved.</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-176\"><p>FS2 volunteered to work at the board, an activity they usually avoid, and whispered in front of the other focus students. That was notable because classmates and the general education teacher had not heard them whisper. These observations suggest that the supportive setting helped students participate and take risks while working through the mathematics.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-177\"><p>5. How did you respond intentionally to your students and adjust your instruction to provide \u201cjust-in-time\u201d support to advance their math development?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-178\"><p>I watched closely for the step behind an error. When FS1 wrote the number of hops, I asked about the number reached. When FS3 moved too many or too few spaces in Video 2, I asked them to compare the movement with the problem. These questions supported correction without supplying the answer. At the Step 2 moment labeled 5:12, FS2 demonstrated the movement successfully without speaking. The exit work showed that all three could apply the strategy with varying support. Next time, I would check vocabulary understanding more often to help prevent errors.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-179\"><p>6. How did the student information you provided in Part A (Written Narrative: Contextual Information) inform your math lesson planning and teaching? a. Prior math learning</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-180\"><p>Part A showed that students already recognized numbers and had some experience with addition, subtraction, and number lines. Their main need was greater accuracy in using the strategy. I therefore focused on the relationship between an operation and its movement rather than reteaching number recognition. Explicit models and guided practice targeted the starting point, direction, and jumps while supporting their IEP math goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-181\"><p>b. Student assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-182\"><p>Students&#x27; character interests, bilingual resources, and comfort with movement informed the lesson design. Spanish supported FS1&#x27;s thinking, while pointing and gestures gave FS2 a strong way to respond. Rumi and Spiderman drew attention to the number-line task; FS3 expressed excitement about Rumi and thanked the character after solving a problem. These choices helped students sustain participation and show understanding through movement, language, or both.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-183\"><p>c. Student learning needs</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-184\"><p>FS1 needed repeated directions and guided visual practice. FS2 needed ways to communicate mathematical understanding without speech. FS3 needed support for attention and regulation. I built the lesson around explicit models, number lines, counters, gestures, and predictable routines so these needs could be addressed within the shared task. The supports gave each student access while keeping the focus on the addition and subtraction goals.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-185\"><p>7. Analyze the effectiveness of the adaptation(s) used to support each focus student\u2019s learning needs. a. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 1\u2019s language learning needs effectively support their progress toward the learning goals and their IEP goal(s) related to the math content, math practices, and/or math academic language that were addressed in the lesson? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-186\"><p>Spanish clarification, gestures, and visual models helped FS1 stay with the mathematics as English language developed. In Video 1, I supplied an English word after inviting a Spanish explanation during the check-in. In Video 3, counting in Spanish supported the calculation. FS1 eventually reached the correct answer on every exit-ticket problem with support. That shows progress in using a strategy and persisting through a problem, rather than independent mastery of every step.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-187\"><p>b. Did the adaptation(s) for Focus Student 2\u2019s specific math learning need(s) effectively support their progress toward the learning goals and their IEP goal(s) related to the math content, math practices, and/or math academic language that were addressed in the lesson? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-188\"><p>FS2 showed understanding by pointing, moving a counter, and gesturing about quantities becoming larger or smaller. Those options supported the math goal without requiring speech. The exit ticket provides a useful measure of independence: FS2 answered three of four correctly without assistance. The visual strategy and guided practice therefore supported meaningful progress toward the IEP goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-189\"><p>c. Did the adaptation(s) to support Focus Student 3\u2019s well-being and/or behavior effectively support their progress toward the learning goals and their IEP goal(s) related to well-being and/or behavior 2 that could impact their access to the math lesson? Why or why not?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-190\"><p>The routine, visual tools, and positive responses to mistakes helped FS3 remain engaged. In Video 2 at 4:05, a guiding question led them to reconsider an error, laugh, and continue rather than become upset. They solved number-line problems with limited prompting and showed their thinking through movement and language. That persistence supports both mathematical practice and the IEP goal for positive engagement.</p></div>"}, {"id": "H", "title": "Part H Next Steps", "html": "<div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-191\"><p>Mild to Moderate Support Needs Step 4: Apply\u2014Part H: Narrative: Application of What You Learned Template</p></div><div class=\"example-directions\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-192\"><p>Directions: Type your responses within the brackets following each prompt (up to 4 pages). Refer to evidence from Step 1, Step 2, and/or Step 3 (e.g., Parts A\u2013G) to support your responses. Do not delete or alter the prompts. For more information, see page 39 of the Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide. 1. Based on what you learned from the math lesson you taught during this cycle, do you need to re-teach students any part of the lesson in the next math lesson you plan to teach? Explain why or why not. a. For the group of students</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-193\"><p>I would revisit the strategy with the group. This was only their second number-line lesson, and the observations and exit tickets show that support is still needed for consistent independent work. In Video 3 at 3:22, for example, FS1 needs help with the starting number and the spaces moved. More practice and explicit vocabulary instruction will strengthen the foundation for accurate addition and subtraction.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-194\"><p>b. For Focus Student 1</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-195\"><p>FS1 needs another supported lesson on the starting point, direction, and number of jumps. Video 3 at 3:22 shows why that practice matters. I would think aloud, connect each term with an action, and have FS1 repeat or demonstrate the step. The goal is to reduce reliance on prompts while strengthening both the math strategy and its language.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-196\"><p>c. For Focus Student 2</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-197\"><p>FS2 does not need the whole lesson retaught. At 4:45 in Video 2, gestures and pointing show a clear sense of the operation and direction. The exit ticket shows three of four problems completed independently, with support needed on one. I would retain nonverbal options and provide further practice to develop consistency before moving to harder problems. The next-step discussion also considers written ways to show thinking.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-198\"><p>d. For Focus Student 3</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-199\"><p>FS3 can use the number line when visual cues, routines, and hands-on supports are available. The self-correction at 4:05 in Video 2 shows persistence. I would give a brief review of the addition and subtraction symbols and their directions, rather than repeat all the content. Modeling each operation and asking FS3 to explain or show its meaning should build consistency. I would keep the interests, encouragement, and guided practice that supported engagement.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-200\"><p>2. How will you continue to use UDL to engage students in math learning (content and practice)?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-201\"><p>I will retain choices in how students access and show the mathematics, as illustrated at 4:32 in Video 2. Visual models, counters, gestures, and drawing will remain available. I would add movement through a floor number line, math scoots, or math hunts, along with anchor charts and practical examples. More peer discussion and guided collaboration would give students a chance to work through an explanation before sharing it. Brief checks and explicit vocabulary models will help me adjust support.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-202\"><p>3. What additional age and/or developmentally appropriate higher-order thinking skill(s) will you engage students in to advance their math content and practice?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-203\"><p>I will ask students to predict what changes when an operation or number changes\u2014for example, when one quantity becomes 15. Comparing two methods and explaining a choice will extend the reasoning beyond carrying out jumps. Sentence openings will support explanations, and FS3 may write or draw a response. I want students to connect their actions to a reason while becoming more independent.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-204\"><p>4. How will you continue to advance these students\u2019 math ALD?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-205\"><p>I will continue linking vocabulary to clear visual and physical examples, as in Video 2 at 5:45. Along with add and subtract, I will reinforce sum and difference through questions and sentence frames used during actual problems. Gestures and home-language connections will remain available. Repeated opportunities to use the terms meaningfully will support both mathematical understanding and communication.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-206\"><p>5. In the future, how will you collaborate with and/or facilitate instructional support personnel to support student access?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-207\"><p>I will keep exchanging progress information with aides and the general education teacher, as I did while planning this lesson. After sessions, I will share effective strategies and the steps that still need work. Coordinating tools, vocabulary, and prompts across settings\u2014and using colleagues&#x27; feedback to revise instruction\u2014will help students practice consistently.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-208\"><p>6. How will you continue to leverage the students\u2019 assets (cultural and/or linguistic) and/or interests related to their math learning?</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-209\"><p>The character choices helped students stay interested and persist, particularly FS3 with Rumi. I will continue using meaningful interests while allowing Spanish to support FS1&#x27;s thinking and nonverbal responses to support FS2&#x27;s communication. Those assets will remain part of how I plan access to the mathematical task.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-210\"><p>7. How will you continue progress toward the IEP goal(s) related to the math content, math practices, and/or math academic language that were addressed in the lesson and/or related to well-being and/or behavior (e.g., attention, engaging with activities) that impacts their access to math? a. For FS 1</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-211\"><p>FS1 will continue with a labeled number line, counters, and scaffolded practice within 20. I will connect symbols, words, and movements, including Spanish-English links such as total and sum. Sentence frames will support explaining a starting point and the number of jumps. Repeated modeling and gradual reduction of support will address accuracy and independence in the IEP goal.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-212\"><p>b. For FS 2</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-213\"><p>FS2 will use a large number line, a movable counter, and direction arrows. Pointing and gestures will show the operation and intended movement. Before independent practice, we can circle the starting number and trace the jumps together. These visual cues will support accuracy and persistence without requiring an oral response.</p></div><div class=\"example-prompt\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-214\"><p>c. For FS 3</p></div><div class=\"example-answer\" id=\"response-mmsn-math-215\"><p>For FS3, I will retain predictable routines, cue cards for the operations, matching gestures, and hands-on work such as a floor number line. Positive reinforcement, movement breaks, and guiding questions will support correction without frustration. These strategies address regulation and engagement while students continue adding and subtracting.</p></div>"}], "images": ["assets/examples/mmsn-math-image1.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-math-image2.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-math-image3.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-math-image4.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-math-image5.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-math-image6.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-math-image7.png", "assets/examples/mmsn-math-image8.png"], "responseCount": 89, "words": 7623, "historical": false}};
window.CALTPA.fullRubrics = {"ms-math": [{"number": 1, "pages": [25, 26], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-math-1-25.png", "assets/rubrics/ms-math-1-26.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nLearning About Students and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 21 \nStep 1 Rubrics \nRubric 1.1 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply findings from recent math learning* to plan one asset-based, UDL-focused math lesson \nthat engages students in higher-order thinking? How does the candidate plan to monitor student progress in a safe, positive environment? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not use \ninformation from recent math \nlearning* to plan instruction. \nCandidate does not select an \nage-/grade-level appropriate CA \nState Math Content \nStrand/Standard \nand/or a Math \nPractice Standard or they select a \nstrand/standard that is more \nthan one grade level above or \nbelow the students they plan to \nteach. \nCandidate does not develop a \nmath learning goal** that is \nbased on the selected math \nstrand/standard and/or \ndoes \nnot include math content and/or \npractice. \nCandidate does not develop a \nmath ALD learning goal or the \nALD learning goal does not align \nwith the math learning goal. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will create a safe, positive \nlearning environment or does \nnot describe how the \nenvironment will support \nstudents during the math lesson. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will leverage the students\u2019 \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will engage students in \nhigher-order thinking*** to \ndeepen their math \nunderstanding. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will monitor student \nlearning during the math lesson. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will use UDL-focused \ninstructional strategy(ies) to \nsupport student learning. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely uses \ninformation from recent math \nlearning* to plan instruction. \nCandidate selects an age-/grade-\nlevel appropriate CA State Math \nContent Strand/Standard and/or \na Math Practice Standard that is \neither one grade-level above or \nbelow the students they plan to \nteach. \nCandidate develops a math \nlearning goal** that is vaguely \nbased on the selected math \nstrand/standard and includes \nmath content and practice. \nCandidate develops a math ALD \nlearning goal that vaguely aligns \nwith the math learning goal. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will create a safe, positive \nlearning environment or vaguely \ndescribes how the learning \nenvironment will support \nstudents during the math lesson. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will leverage the students\u2019 \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will engage students in \nhigher-order thinking*** to \ndeepen their math \nunderstanding. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will monitor student \nlearning during the math lesson. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will use UDL-focused \ninstructional strategy(ies) to \nsupport student learning. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly uses \ninformation from recent math \nlearning* to plan instruction. \nCandidate selects an age-/grade-\nlevel appropriate CA State Math \nContent Strand/Standard and a \nMath Practice Standard for the \nstudents they plan to teach.  \nCandidate develops a math \nlearning goal** that is clearly \nbased on the selected math \nstrand/standard and includes \nboth math content and practice. \nCandidate develops a math ALD \nlearning goal that clearly aligns \nwith the math learning goal. \nCandidate describes how they \nwill create a safe, positive \nlearning environment that clearly \nsupports students during the \nmath lesson. \nCandidate clearly describes how \nthey will leverage the students\u2019 \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate clearly describes how \nthey will engage students in \nhigher-order thinking*** to \ndeepen their math \nunderstanding. \nCandidate clearly describes how \nthey will monitor student \nlearning during the math lesson. \nCandidate clearly describes how \nthey will use UDL-focused \ninstructional strategy(ies) to \nsupport student learning.\n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s planning clearly \nincludes opportunities for \nstudents to learn collaboratively \nto progress toward the math \ncontent and practice and math \nALD learning goals. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate designs a math lesson \nthat reflects the \ninterconnectedness of math plus \nan additional academic content \narea (e.g., literacy, social science, \nart, science, PE). \nCalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nLearning About Students and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 22 \nNotes: \n*For example, recent observations, math assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), and/or information provided by your \ncooperating teacher and/or supervising faculty \n**Math learning goal includes strand(s)/standard(s) and math practice. Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classrooms should use \nage-level developmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF).   \n***For example, analysis, synthesis, evaluation, interpretation, and transfer/generalization \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence:  \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Math Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Math Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4, 5; TPE 2, Element 2; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5; TPE 4, \nElements 1, 4; TPE 5, Elements 2, 8; TPE 6, Element 5 \n "}, {"number": 2, "pages": [27], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-math-2-27.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nLearning About Students and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 23 \nRubric 1.2 \u2014  Step 1: Plan  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply recent learning* to plan adaptation(s) to support the math ALD of FS1 based on \ntheir assets and/or interests and learning need(s)?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS1 are not \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) do not \nleverage FS1\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) do not \nsupport FS1\u2019s math ALD \nlearning need(s) through \nreading, writing, listening, \nand/or speaking. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS1 are \nvaguely based on recent \nlearning* or information used \nis not recent. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) vaguely \nleverage FS1\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) vaguely \nsupport FS1\u2019s math ALD \nlearning need(s) through \nreading, writing, listening, \nand/or speaking. \n \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS1 are clearly \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) clearly \nleverage FS1\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) clearly \nsupport FS1\u2019s math ALD \nlearning need(s) through \nreading, writing, listening, \nand/or speaking. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans adaptation(s) \nfor FS1 to participate in the \nmath lesson by using multiple \nmodes of communication** to \nsupport FS1\u2019s learning needs.   \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate\u2019s planning for FS1\u2019s \nadaptation(s) draws upon \nand/or refers to evidence-\nbased practice(s) and/or \nresearch appropriate to FS1\u2019s \nmath ALD. \n \nNotes:  \n*For example, recent observations, math assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), and/or information provided by your cooperating teacher \nand/or supervising faculty  \n**For example, collaborative, interpretive, and/or productive language \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Math Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Math Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 6; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 5; TPE 4, Elements 1, 4, 7; TPE 5, Element 2 \n "}, {"number": 3, "pages": [28], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-math-3-28.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nLearning About Students and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 24 \nRubric 1.3 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply recent learning* to plan adaptation(s) to support the math learning of FS2 based \non their assets and/or interests and learning need(s)?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS2 are not \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) do not \nleverage FS2\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) do not \nsupport FS2\u2019s math learning \nneed(s).** \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS2 are \nvaguely based on recent \nlearning* or information used \nis not recent. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) vaguely \nleverage FS2\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) vaguely \nsupport FS2\u2019s math learning \nneed(s).** \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS2 are clearly \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) clearly \nleverage FS2\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) clearly \nsupport FS2\u2019s math learning \nneed(s).** \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans adaptation(s) \nfor FS2 to participate in the \nmath lesson by using two or \nmore multisensory \nstrategies*** to support FS2\u2019s \nlearning needs.  \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate\u2019s planning for FS2\u2019s \nadaptation(s) draws upon \nand/or refers to evidence-\nbased math practice(s) and/or \nresearch appropriate to FS2\u2019s \nmath learning need(s). \nNotes: \n*For example, recent observations, math assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), and/or information provided by your cooperating teacher \nand/or supervising faculty \n**For example, gaps in math knowledge, misconceptions, IEP/504 plan/MTSS with math learning need \n***Multisensory strategies are teaching techniques that engage two or more senses simultaneously\u2014 visual, auditory, kinesthetic, and/or tactile \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Math Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Math Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 5; TPE 4, Elements 1, 2, 4, 5;  \nTPE 5, Elements 2, 8 \n "}, {"number": 4, "pages": [29], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-math-4-29.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nLearning About Students and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 25 \nRubric 1.4 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply recent learning* to adapt the environment to support FS3\u2019s well-being and/or \nbehavior based on their assets and/or interests, and learning need(s)? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS3 are not \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) do not \nleverage FS3\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) do not \nsupport FS3\u2019s well-being \nand/or behavior to meet their \nmath learning need(s). \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS3 are \nvaguely based on recent \nlearning* or information used \nis not recent. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) vaguely \nleverage FS3\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) vaguely \nsupport FS3\u2019s well-being \nand/or behavior to meet their \nmath learning need(s). \n \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS3 are clearly \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) clearly \nleverage FS3\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) clearly \nsupport FS3\u2019s well-being \nand/or behavior to meet their \nmath learning need(s). \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans adaptation(s) \nfor FS3 to participate in the \nmath lesson by creating a \ntargeted, proactive plan** to \nsupport FS3\u2019s learning needs.  \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate\u2019s planning for FS3\u2019s \nadaptation(s) draws upon \nand/or refers to evidence-\nbased practice(s) and/or \nresearch appropriate to FS3\u2019s \nwell-being and/or behavior. \n \nNotes: \n*For example, recent observations, math assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), and/or information provided by your cooperating teacher \nand/or supervising faculty \n**Proactive plan is intentionally setting up a learning environment that is conducive and targeted to the well-being and/or behavioral needs of the student (e.g., \nstructured routines, frequent breaks, safe space, positive reinforcement, minimizing triggering language and/or tasks) \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Math Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Math Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4; TPE 2, Elements 1, 2, 3, 4; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 5;  \nTPE 4, Elements 1, 2, 4; TPE 5, Elements 2, 8 \n "}, {"number": 5, "pages": [33], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-math-5-33.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nLearning About Students and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 29 \nStep 2 Rubrics \nRubric 1.5 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate maintain a safe, positive learning environment* and apply a UDL-focused strategy that \nsupports their students in making progress during the math lesson?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmaintain a safe, positive \nlearning environment* during \nthe math lesson. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use a \nUDL-focused strategy(ies) to \nsupport their students during \nthe math lesson. \nCandidate provides a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription with 4 or more \nminor or a major \ninaccuracy(ies) in their math \ncontent instruction. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the math lesson \ndescribed in Step 1. \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmaintain a safe, positive \nlearning environment* during \nthe math lesson. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use a \nUDL-focused strategy(ies) to \nsupport their students during \nthe math lesson. \nCandidate provides a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription with 3 or fewer \nminor inaccuracies in their \nmath content instruction. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the math lesson \ndescribed in Step 1. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a \ndemonstration and description \nof how they clearly maintain a \nsafe, positive learning \nenvironment* during the math \nlesson. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they use a UDL-focused \nstrategy(ies) to support their \nstudents during the math \nlesson. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof accurate math content \ninstruction. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof the math lesson described in \nStep 1.  \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they use \nmultiple UDL-focused \nstrategies to support their \nstudents during the math \nlesson.  \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate facilitates structured \nopportunities for the students \nto collaboratively explore the \nmath content and practice \nthrough discussion, writing, \nand/or reading. \nNote: \n*For example: establishing clear expectations; creating a safe and welcoming environment; greeting students; establishing cen tral question(s) and/or lesson hook; \nengaging students; establishing positive rapport \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 3 video clips, totaling up to 15 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 2, Elements 2, 3, 5, 6; TPE 4, Element 4 \n "}, {"number": 6, "pages": [34], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-math-6-34.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nLearning About Students and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 30 \nRubric 1.6 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate engage students in higher-order thinking during the math lesson and monitor and \nrespond intentionally to support student learning? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they engage \nthe students in higher-order \nthinking to support student \nlearning. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmonitor and/or respond \nintentionally to support \nstudent learning. \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they engage \nthe students in higher-order \nthinking to support student \nlearning. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmonitor and/or respond \nintentionally to support \nstudent learning. \n \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they engage the \nstudents in higher-order \nthinking to support student \nlearning. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they monitor and \nrespond intentionally to \nsupport student learning. \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they use \nmultiple assessment(s) to \nmonitor and adjust instruction \nto support student learning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they connect \nmath learning to real-life \ncontexts to engage, motivate, \nand/or extend student \nlearning. \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence:  \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 3 video clips, totaling up to 15 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 3, 5, 8; TPE 2, Element 5; TPE 4, Element 4 \n \n "}, {"number": 7, "pages": [37], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-math-7-37.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nLearning About Students and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 33 \nStep 3 Rubric \nRubric 1.7 \u2014  Step 3: Reflect  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate reflect on the impact of their asset-based, UDL-focused math lesson for the group and \nthe focus students in a safe, positive learning environment?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not reflect on \nwhat went well and/or what \ndid not go well or as expected \nin the lesson. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of getting to know \ntheir students before teaching \nthe lesson. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of the safe, positive \nenvironment they created to \ninclude all students. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of their asset-based \napproach. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of their UDL-\nfocused strategy(ies). \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of engaging \nstudents in higher-order \nthinking. \nCandidate does not analyze* \nstudent progress in meeting \nthe math and/or ALD learning \ngoals. \nCandidate does not analyze* \nthe impact of the adaptation(s) \nused to support FS1, FS2, \nand/or FS3 in making progress \ntoward meeting the learning \ngoals. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nwhat went well and/or what \ndid not go well or as expected \nin the lesson. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of getting to know \ntheir students before teaching \nthe lesson. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of the safe, positive \nenvironment they created to \ninclude all students. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of their asset-based \napproach. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of their UDL-\nfocused strategy(ies). \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of engaging \nstudents in higher-order \nthinking. \nCandidate vaguely analyzes* \nstudent progress in meeting \nthe math and/or ALD learning \ngoals. \nCandidate vaguely analyzes* \nthe impact of the adaptation(s) \nused to support FS1, FS2, \nand/or FS3 in making progress \ntoward meeting the learning \ngoals. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nwhat went well and what did \nnot go well or as expected in \nthe lesson. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of getting to know \ntheir students before teaching \nthe lesson. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of the safe, positive \nenvironment they created to \ninclude all students. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of their asset-based \napproach. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of their UDL-\nfocused strategy(ies). \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of engaging \nstudents in higher-order \nthinking. \nCandidate clearly analyzes* \nstudent progress in meeting \nthe math and ALD learning \ngoals. \nCandidate clearly analyzes* the \nimpact of the adaptation(s) \nused to support FS1, FS2, and \nFS3 in making progress toward \nmeeting the learning goals. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s reflection \ndemonstrates an \nunderstanding that affirming, \nvalidating, and/or leveraging \nthe students\u2019 assets and/or \ninterests influence student \nmath learning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nWhen reflecting on the impact \nof their instruction, the \ncandidate refers to specific \nexamples from the teaching of \ntheir math lesson to determine \nwhich strategy(ies) moved \nlearning forward. \n \nNote: \n*Analyze: Involves carefully and systematically looking for recurring themes, common misconceptions, and strengths and/or are as of growth across a collection of \nstudent work in order to gain insights into student understanding. Analysis goes beyond simply describing what happened; it delves into why, what was \nlearned/understood, and what the work reveals about student thinking. "}, {"number": 8, "pages": [40], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-math-8-40.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nLearning About Students and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 36 \nStep 4 Rubric \nRubric 1.8 \u2014  Step 4: Apply \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply what they have learned to determine next steps for math content and math \npractice and math ALD instruction? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe what \nthey learned throughout this \ncycle.  \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe how \nthey would advance the math \nand/or ALD learning for the \ngroup. \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe how \nthey would continue to support \nthe learning need(s) of FS1, \nFS2, and/or FS3. \nCandidate does not use a \nspecific example(s) from Steps \n1, 2, or 3 and/or does not \njustify their next instructional \nmove for math learning, or the \nprovided example does not \njustify the next instructional \nmove for math learning. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes what \nthey learned throughout this \ncycle.  \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes how \nthey would advance the math \nand/or ALD learning for the \ngroup. \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes how \nthey would continue to support \nthe learning need(s) of FS1, \nFS2, and/or FS3. \nCandidate uses an example(s) \nfrom Steps 1, 2, or 3 that \nvaguely justifies their next \ninstructional move for math \nlearning. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps clearly describes what \nthey learned throughout this \ncycle.  \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps clearly describes how \nthey would advance the math \nand ALD learning for the group. \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps clearly describes how \nthey would continue to support \nthe learning need(s) of FS1, \nFS2, and FS3. \nCandidate uses a specific \nexample(s) from Steps 1, 2, or 3 \nthat clearly justifies their next \ninstructional move for math \nlearning. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s responses \ndemonstrate how they will \ncontinue to leverage students\u2019 \nassets and/or interests in \nfuture lessons to support math \nlearning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate describes how they \nwill apply what they have \nlearned about the group and \nthe focus students to plan \nfuture instruction that clearly \nreinforces, strengthens, and/or \nextends students\u2019 higher-order \nthinking related to math and \nALD. \n \nStep 4 Source of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part H: Application of Learning (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL response) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 3, Element 2; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 6, Element 1 \n \n "}], "ms-literacy": [{"number": 1, "pages": [29, 30], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-literacy-1-29.png", "assets/rubrics/ms-literacy-1-30.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 25 \nStep 1 Rubrics \nRubric 2.1 \u2014 Step 1: Plan  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use their knowledge of CA State* ELA/Literacy strand(s)/standard(s) and ELD \nstrand(s)/standard(s), along with information gathered from recent learning/assessments, to develop appropriate ELA/Literacy and \nELD learning goals? How does the candidate develop formative and summative assessments that target the identified learning \ngoals? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not use \nlearning/assessment information \nto plan instruction. \nCandidate does not select a CA \nState* ELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nstrands/standards for the unit plan \nOR selects strands/standards that \nare more than one grade level \nabove or below the students they \nplan to teach in the unit plan. \nCandidate does not include \nfoundational reading skill(s)** \nand/or an additional theme*** in \ntheir ELA/Literacy goal(s) for the \nunit plan. \nCandidate does not include an ELD \ngoal, OR the ELD goal(s) are not \ngrade-level appropriate, OR the \nELD goal(s) do not support the \nELA/Literacy learning goal(s) for \nthe unit plan. \nCandidate does not plan formative \nassessments in each lesson, OR the \nformative assessments are not \nconnected to the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD learning goal(s). \nCandidate does not plan a \nsummative assessment OR plans a \nsummative assessment that does \nnot measure the students\u2019 \nprogress in meeting the \nELA/Literacy and ELD learning \ngoals for the unit plan. \nThe included rubric or \nperformance criteria for the \nsummative assessment do not \nmeasure the ELA/Literacy and \nELD learning goals for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate\u2019s planning has 4 or \nmore minor errors or a major error \nwhen addressing foundational \nreading skill(s),** additional \ntheme(s),*** and/or integrated \nELD. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely uses \ninformation from recent \nlearning/assessments or the \ninformation used to plan \ninstruction is not recent.  \nCandidate selects CA State* \nELA/Literacy and ELD \nstrands/standards that are either \none grade level above or one grade \nlevel below the grade of the \nstudents they plan to teach in the \nunit plan. \nCandidate develops ELA/Literacy \nlearning goal(s) that vaguely \ninclude foundational reading \nskill(s)** and/or an additional \ntheme*** for the unit plan. \nCandidate develops ELD goal(s) \nthat vaguely support the \nELA/Literacy learning goal(s) for \nthe unit plan. \nCandidate plans one or more \nformative assessment(s) in each \nlesson that vaguely connect to the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD learning \ngoal(s). \nCandidate plans one summative \nassessment that vaguely measures \nthe students\u2019 progress in meeting \nthe ELA/Literacy and ELD learning \ngoals for the unit plan. \nCandidate includes a rubric or \nperformance criteria for the \nsummative assessment that \nvaguely measure the ELA/Literacy \nand ELD learning goals for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate\u2019s planning includes 3 or \nfewer minor errors when \naddressing foundational reading \nskill(s),** additional theme(s),*** \nand/or integrated ELD. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly uses information \nfrom recent learning/assessments \nto plan instruction.  \nCandidate selects grade-level \nappropriate CA State* ELA/Literacy \nand ELD strands/standards for the \nunit plan. \nCandidate develops grade-level \nappropriate ELA/Literacy learning \ngoal(s) that include both \nfoundational reading skill(s)** and \nadditional theme(s)*** for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate develops grade-level \nappropriate ELD goal(s) that clearly \nsupport the ELA/Literacy learning \ngoal(s) for the unit plan. \nCandidate plans one or more \nformative assessment(s) in each \nlesson that are clearly connected \nto the ELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning goal(s). \nCandidate plans one summative \nassessment that clearly measures \nthe students\u2019 progress in meeting \nthe ELA/Literacy and ELD learning \ngoals for the unit plan. \nCandidate includes a rubric or \nperformance criteria for the \nsummative assessment that clearly \nmeasure the ELA/Literacy and\nELD learning goals for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate\u2019s planning accurately \naddresses foundational reading \nskill(s),** additional theme(s),*** \nand integrated ELD. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans multiple formative \nassessments within each lesson of \nthe unit to monitor the group\u2019s \nELA/Literacy and ELD \nunderstandings and learning \nneeds. \nL\nevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus \nWhen developing the summative \nassessment and corresponding \nrubric or performance criteria, the \ncandidate includes real-life \nscenarios that build upon the \ngroup\u2019s culture, language(s), \nand/or experiences. \nCalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 26 \nNotes: \n*You must use the California Common Core State Standards for English Language Arts & Literacy, California English Language Art s/English \nLanguage Development (ELA/ELD) Framework, and California English Language Development Standards (CA ELD Standards). Candidates in TK \nmust use the Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations: Language and Literacy Development . Candidates in bilingual \nclassrooms where the language of instruction is Spanish can use the California CCSS en Espa\u00f1ol (ELA/Literacy Standard[s]) and the California \nSpanish Language Development (SLD) Standards. Candidates in bilingual placements that use Spanish as the language of instruction using the \nCCSS en Espa\u00f1ol and the Spanish Language Development Standards should substitute SLA/Literacy and SLD goals for ELA/Literacy goals \nthroughout this guide. \n**Foundational reading skills: print concepts, including letters of the alphabet; phonological awareness, including phonemic awareness; \nphonics, spelling, and word recognition; decoding and encoding; morphology/morphological awareness; text reading fluency, including \naccuracy, prosody [expression], and rate [an indicator of automaticity]. For candidates in TK\u20133 settings, foundational reading skills also include \ncreating literacy environments that are print rich and that foster interest in print; and games, books, poetry, oral storytelling, and songs that \ndraw attention to print, the manipulation of sounds, and alphabet letters. \n***Additional themes: meaning making, language development, effective expression, and/or content knowledge \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages)\n\u2022 Part B: Unit Plan (up to 20 pages)\n\u2022 Part C: Summative Assessment Description (up to 5 pages)\n\u2022 Part D: Copies of Blank Summative Assessment and Rubric or Performance Criteria\n(up to 3 pages)\nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 3, 4; TPE 2, Element 2; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5, 6; TPE 4, \nElements 1, 2, 4; TPE 5, Element 1; TPE 7, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 11 "}, {"number": 2, "pages": [31], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-literacy-2-31.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 27 \nRubric 2.2 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate leverage student assets and/or interests and use UDL strategies to plan an aligned \nseries of lessons that build upon one another to create a progression of learning? How do the lessons of the unit plan (a) address the \nfoundational reading skill(s)* using a direct, systematic, and explicit approach; (b) actively engage the students in the additional \ntheme(s);** and (c) integrate ELD for the group, including the FS?\nLevel 1 \nIn their planning, the candidate \ndoes not leverage student \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate does not plan to use \nUDL strategy(ies) when \nteaching the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD content. \nThe unit plan does not include \nthree lessons or the lessons do \nnot align and build upon one \nanother to create a progression \nof learning. \nCandidate does not plan to \nteach foundational reading \nskill(s)* and/or does not plan \nto teach foundational reading \nskill(s) using a direct, \nsystematic, and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate does not include an \nadditional theme** and/or \ndoes not plan to actively \nengage the students while \nteaching the additional \ntheme(s). \nCandidate\u2019s plan does not \nintegrate ELD, and/or the ELD \ndoes not support the \nfoundational reading skill(s)* \nand/or the additional \ntheme(s).** \nCandidate does not include \nadaptation(s) that address the \nFS\u2019s assets and/or interests \nand/or learning needs. \nLevel 2 \nIn their planning, the candidate \nvaguely leverages student \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate vaguely plans to use \nUDL strategy(ies) when \nteaching the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD content. \nThe unit plan includes three \nlessons that vaguely align and \nbuild upon one another to \ncreate a progression of \nlearning. \nWhen planning to teach the \nfoundational reading skill(s),* \ncandidate vaguely includes a \ndirect, systematic, and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate vaguely plans to \nengage the students with the \nadditional theme(s).** \nCandidate\u2019s plan integrates ELD \nthat vaguely supports the \nfoundational reading skill(s)* or \nthe additional theme(s).** \nCandidate includes \nadaptation(s) that vaguely \naddress the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests and/or learning \nneeds. \n \nLevel 3 \nIn their planning, the candidate \nclearly leverages student assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate clearly plans to use \nappropriate UDL strategy(ies) \nwhen teaching the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD content. \nThe unit plan includes three \nlessons that clearly align and \nbuild upon one another to \ncreate a progression of \nlearning. \nWhen planning to teach the \nfoundational reading skill(s),* \ncandidate clearly includes a \ndirect, systematic, and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate clearly plans to \nactively engage the students \nwith the additional theme(s).** \nCandidate\u2019s plan integrates ELD \nthat clearly supports the \nfoundational reading skill(s)* \nand the additional theme(s).** \nCandidate includes a plan for \nadaptation(s) that clearly \naddress the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests and learning needs. \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate leverages student \nassets by intentionally \nincorporating students\u2019 cultural \nbackgrounds, lived experiences, \ninterests, and/or primary \nlanguages into the lesson \ndesign. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nEach of the candidate\u2019s lessons \ninclude multiple UDL strategies \nto support the group and the \nFS toward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and ELD learning \ngoal(s) based on their assets \nand/or interests and learning \nneeds. "}, {"number": 3, "pages": [36], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-literacy-3-36.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 32 \nStep 2 Rubrics \nRubric 2.3 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate actively engage students in learning the foundational reading skill(s)* using a direct and \nexplicit approach? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they teach \nthe foundational reading skill(s) \nusing a direct and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage the students \nduring the teaching of the \nfoundational reading skill(s). \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the literacy \nlesson(s) described in Step 1.  \nLevel 2 \nCandidate includes an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they teach \nthe foundational reading skill(s) \nusing a direct and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate includes an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage the students \nduring the teaching of the \nfoundational reading skill(s). \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the literacy \nlesson(s) described in Step 1.  \nLevel 3 \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they teach the \nfoundational reading skill(s) \nusing a direct and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they actively engage the \nstudents during the teaching of \nthe foundational reading \nskill(s). \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof the literacy lesson(s) \ndescribed in Step 1.  \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate models the targeted \nfoundational reading skill(s) \nusing precise academic \nlanguage and think-alouds to \nmake cognitive processes \nvisible. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nCandidate formatively assesses \nstudents\u2019 foundational reading \nskill(s) throughout the lesson(s) \nand structures frequent \nopportunities for students to \nrespond and receive feedback. \nNote: \n*Foundational reading skills: print concepts, including letters of the alphabet; phonological awareness, including phonemic aw areness; phonics, spelling, and word \nrecognition; decoding and encoding; morphology/morphological awareness; text reading fluency, including accuracy, prosody (expression), and rate (an indicator of \nautomaticity). For candidates in TK\u20133 settings, foundational reading skills also include creating literacy environments that are print rich and that foster inter est in \nprint; and games, books, poetry, oral storytelling, and songs that draw attention to print, the manipulation of sounds, and alphabet letters.  \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 4; TPE 3, Elements 1, 3; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 7, Elements 1, 2, 4, 5 "}, {"number": 4, "pages": [37], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-literacy-4-37.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 33 \nRubric 2.4 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate leverage student assets and/or interests to actively engage them in learning the \nadditional theme(s)* from the ELA/ELD Framework? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nleverage student assets and/or \ninterests while teaching the \nadditional theme(s)* from the \nELA/ELD Framework. \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage students in \nlearning the additional \ntheme(s) from the ELA/ELD \nFramework. \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides a vague \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nleverage student assets and/or \ninterests while teaching the \nadditional theme(s)* from the \nELA/ELD Framework. \nCandidate provides a vague \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage students in \nlearning the additional \ntheme(s) from the ELA/ELD \nFramework. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they leverage student \nassets and/or interests while \nteaching the additional \ntheme(s)* from the ELA/ELD \nFramework. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they actively engage \nstudents in learning the \nadditional theme(s) from the \nELA/ELD Framework.\n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate incorporates \nstudents\u2019 cultural backgrounds, \nlived experiences, interests, \nand/or primary languages into \ninstruction of the additional \ntheme(s) from the ELA/ELD \nframework.\n \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nCandidate provides structured \nopportunities for the students \nto collaboratively explore the \nadditional theme(s) through \ndiscussion, writing, and/or \nreading. \n \nNote: \n*Additional themes: meaning making, language development, effective expression, and/or content knowledge  \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 6; TPE 3, Elements 1, 3; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 7, Elements 1, 4, 11; Candidate Choice \nof Elements 6\u20139 "}, {"number": 5, "pages": [38], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-literacy-5-38.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 34 \nRubric 2.5 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use UDL strategy(ies) and integrate ELD when teaching foundational reading skill(s) \nand/or the additional theme(s)?  \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of UDL \nstrategy(ies). \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nintegrate ELD to support the \nteaching of the foundational \nreading skill(s) and/or the \nadditional theme(s). \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate includes a vague \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the UDL \nstrategy(ies) they use to teach \nthe ELA/Literacy and/or ELD \ncontent. \nCandidate provides a vague \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nintegrate ELD to support the \nteaching of the foundational \nreading skill(s) and/or the \nadditional theme(s). \n \nLevel 3 \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof the UDL strategy(ies) they \nuse to teach the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD content. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they integrate ELD to \nsupport the teaching of the \nfoundational reading skill(s) \nand/or the additional theme(s). \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus:  \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof multiple UDL strategies used \nto support the teaching of the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning goals. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate encourages \ntranslanguaging as a resource \nto deepen comprehension and  \nlanguage transfer. \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 2, 8; TPE 3, Element 3; TPE 4, Elements 4, 7; TPE 5, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5; TPE 7, \nElements 2, 4, 10 "}, {"number": 6, "pages": [39], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-literacy-6-39.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 35 \nRubric 2.6 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use formative assessment(s) to monitor student learning and respond intentionally to \nsupport student progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use \nformative assessment(s) to \nmonitor student progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals. \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nrespond intentionally to \nformative assessment results to \nsupport student progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides a vague \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use \nformative assessment(s) to \nmonitor student progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals. \nCandidate includes a vague \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nrespond intentionally to \nformative assessment results to \nsupport student progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they use formative \nassessment(s) to monitor \nstudent progress toward \nmeeting the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD goals. \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they respond \nintentionally to formative \nassessment results to support \nstudent progress toward \nmeeting the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD goals. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate uses multiple \nformative assessments, such as \npurposeful questioning, \nacademic discussion, analysis of \nstudent work, and/or real-time \nchecks for understanding, to \nmonitor student learning in \nrelation to both ELA/Literacy \nand ELD goals. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 and 4, plus: \nCandidate analyzes student \nresponses during instruction to \nidentify patterns and any \nmisconceptions. Based on this \nevidence, the candidate \nimmediately adapts instruction \nin meaningful ways, including \ndifferentiating tasks, providing \ntargeted scaffolds, and/or \nextending the learning for the \nstudents. \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 8; TPE 5, Elements 1, 2, 5; TPE 7, Element 10 "}, {"number": 7, "pages": [45], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-literacy-7-45.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 41 \nStep 3 Rubric \nRubric 2.7 \u2014 Step 3: Reflect \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use their analysis of the summative assessment results to identify students\u2019 \nunderstandings and/or misconceptions and to provide specific, actionable feedback to the FS? How does the candidate use their \nanalysis of the summative assessment results and the information they gathered during their teaching to determine instructional \nchanges they would make if they taught the unit again? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s reflection does not \ninclude an analysis of the \ngroup\u2019s and/or the FS\u2019s \nsummative assessment results. \nThe candidate does not identify \nunderstandings and/or \nmisconceptions related to the \ngroup\u2019s and/or the FS\u2019s \nprogress toward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and ELD goals, OR \nthe candidate does not base \ntheir analysis on the \ninformation they gathered \nwhile teaching and/or the \nsummative assessment results. \nCandidate does not provide \nspecific, actionable feedback \nfor the FS, OR the specific, \nactionable feedback is not \nbased on the summative \nassessment results. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndescription of the impact of \ntheir instructional approach(es) \nand/or their choice of student \nactivity(ies) during the unit for \nthe group, including the FS. \nCandidate does not provide an \nexplanation of what \ninstructional changes they \nwould make for the group, \nincluding the FS, if they taught \nthe unit again. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s reflection includes \na vague analysis of the group\u2019s \nand/or the FS\u2019s summative \nassessment results. \nBased on their analysis of the \nsummative assessment results \nand the information gathered \nduring their teaching, the \ncandidate vaguely identifies \nunderstandings and/or \nmisconceptions related to the \ngroup\u2019s and/or the FS\u2019s \nprogress toward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals. \nCandidate provides feedback to \nthe FS that is vague, not \nactionable, or not clearly based \non the student\u2019s performance \non the summative assessment, \nOR the candidate provides \ndevelopmentally inappropriate \nfeedback. \nCandidate provides a vague \ndescription of the impact of \ntheir instructional approach(es) \nand/or their choice of student \nactivity(ies) during the unit for \nthe group, including the FS. \nCandidate provides a vague \nexplanation of what \ninstructional changes they \nwould make for the group, \nincluding the FS, if they taught \nthe unit again. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s reflection includes \na clear analysis of the group\u2019s \nand the FS\u2019s summative \nassessment results. \nBased on their analysis of the \nsummative assessment results \nand the information gathered \nduring their teaching, the \ncandidate clearly identifies \nunderstandings and/or \nmisconceptions related to the \ngroup\u2019s and the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and ELD goals. \nCandidate provides specific, \nactionable feedback for the FS \nthat is clearly based on the FS\u2019s \nperformance on the summative \nassessment. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndescription of the impact of \ntheir instructional approach(es) \nand their choice of student \nactivity(ies) during the unit for \nthe group, including the FS. \nCandidate provides a clear \nexplanation of what \ninstructional changes they \nwould make for the group, \nincluding the FS, if they taught \nthe unit again. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s specific, actionable \nfeedback for the FS is aligned \nto the learning goals of the \nunit. It clearly names what the \nFS did well and/or what needs \nimprovement based on specific \nstrengths and/or needs of the \nFS using concrete examples \nfrom the summative \nassessment and/or \nobservations. \nL\nevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nWhen reflecting on the impact \nof their instruction, the \ncandidate uses data from the \nunit plan, specific examples \nfrom their teaching or \ncommentary, and data from \nformative and/or summative \nassessment(s) to determine \nwhich strategies moved \nlearning forward. "}, {"number": 8, "pages": [49], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ms-literacy-8-49.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Multiple Subject \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 45 \nStep 4 Rubric \nRubric 2.8 \u2014  Step 4: Apply \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply the analysis of the FS\u2019s performance during the unit and summative assessment \nresults to plan, provide an explanation for, and teach a follow-up activity (referring to specific examples from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3)? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not include a \nplan for follow-up instruction \nfor the FS, either re-teaching or \nextension, based on their \nanalysis of the FS\u2019s \nperformance during the unit, \nincluding summative \nassessment results.  \nThe video and commentary do \nnot include either: \n\u2022 Re-Teach: A demonstration \nand/or description of the \ntargeted intervention \nand/or individualized \nstrategy used to support the \nFS\u2019s progress toward \nmeeting the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD goal(s) \nOR \n\u2022 Extend: A demonstration \nand/or description of the \ninstruction that deepens \nand/or advances the FS\u2019s \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning  \nCandidate does not include \nspecific example(s) from  \nSteps 1, 2, and/or 3 to support \ntheir follow-up instructional \ndecision making, OR evidence \ncited does not support follow-\nup instructional decisions. \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate includes a vague \nplan for follow-up instruction \nfor the FS, either re-teaching or \nextension, based on their \nanalysis of the FS\u2019s \nperformance during the unit, \nincluding summative \nassessment results.  \nThe video and/or commentary \ninclude either: \n\u2022 Re-Teach: An unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the targeted \nintervention and/or \nindividualized strategy used \nto support the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \ngoal(s) \nOR \n\u2022 Extend: An unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the instruction \nthat deepens and/or \nadvances the FS\u2019s \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning  \nCandidate uses vague \nexample(s) from Steps 1, 2, \nand/or 3 to support their \nfollow-up instructional decision \nmaking. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate includes a clear plan \nfor follow-up instruction for the \nFS, either re-teaching or \nextension, based on their \nanalysis of the FS\u2019s \nperformance during the unit, \nincluding summative \nassessment results.  \nThe video and commentary \ninclude either: \n\u2022 Re-Teach: A clear \ndemonstration and \ndescription of the targeted \nintervention and/or \nindividualized strategy used \nto support the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \ngoal(s) \nOR \n\u2022 Extend: A clear \ndemonstration and \ndescription of the \ninstruction that deepens \nand/or advances the FS\u2019s \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning  \nCandidate clearly uses specific \nexample(s) from Steps 1, 2, \nand/or 3 to support their \nfollow-up instructional decision \nmaking. \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate leverages the FS\u2019s \nassets and/or interests by \nintentionally incorporating the \nstudent\u2019s cultural, linguistic, \nand/or lived experiences into \nthe follow-up instruction \ndesign. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate synthesizes multiple \nsources of evidence from Steps \n1, 2, and 3 to justify their \ninstructional decisions and \nclearly explains how the \nselected strategy accelerates \nthe FS\u2019s learning of the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \ncontent. "}], "ss-c1": [{"number": 1, "pages": [22, 23], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c1-1-22.png", "assets/rubrics/ss-c1-1-23.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 1 \nSingle Subject Learning About Students and Planning Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811 \nAll rights reserved. 18 \nStep 1 Rubrics \nRubric 1.1 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply findings from recent content-specific learning* to plan one asset-based, UDL-\nfocused content-specific lesson that engages students in higher-order thinking? How does the candidate plan to monitor student \nprogress in a safe, positive environment? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not use \ninformation from recent content-\nspecific learning* to plan \ninstruction. \nCandidate does not select a \ngrade-level appropriate CA State \ncontent-specific Strand/Standard \nand/or Curriculum Framework or \nthey select a strand/ \nstandard that is more than one \ngrade level above or below the \nstudents they plan to teach. \nCandidate does not develop a \ncontent-specific learning goal \nthat is based on the selected \ncontent strand/standard. \nCandidate does not develop an \nALD learning goal or the ALD \nlearning goal does not align with \nthe content-specific learning \ngoal. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will create a safe, positive \nlearning environment or does \nnot describe how the \nenvironment will support \nstudents during the content-\nspecific lesson. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will leverage the students\u2019 \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will engage students in \nhigher-order thinking** to \ndeepen their content-specific \nunderstanding. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will monitor student \nlearning during the content-\nspecific lesson. \nCandidate does not describe how \nthey will use UDL-focused \ninstructional strategy(ies) to \nsupport student learning. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely uses \ninformation from recent content-\nspecific learning* to plan \ninstruction. \nCandidate selects a grade-level \nappropriate CA State content-\nspecific Strand/Standard and/or \nCurriculum Framework that is \neither one grade level above or \nbelow the students they plan to \nteach. \nCandidate develops a content-\nspecific learning goal that is \nvaguely based on the selected \ncontent strand/standard. \nCandidate develops \nan ALD \nlearning goal that vaguely aligns \nwith the content-specific \nlearning goal. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will create a safe, positive \nlearning environment or vaguely \ndescribes how the learning \nenvironment will support \nstudents during the content-\nspecific lesson. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will leverage the students\u2019 \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will engage students in \nhigher-order thinking** to \ndeepen their content-specific \nunderstanding. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will monitor student \nlearning during the content-\nspecific lesson. \nCandidate vaguely describes how \nthey will use UDL-focused \ninstructional strategy(ies) to \nsupport student learning. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly uses \ninformation from recent content-\nspecific learning* to plan \ninstruction. \nCandidate selects a grade-level \nappropriate CA State content-\nspecific Strand/Standard and/or \nCurriculum Framework for the \nstudents they plan to teach.  \nCandidate develops a content-\nspecific learning goal that is \nclearly based on the selected \ncontent strand/standard. \nCandidate develops \nan ALD \nlearning goal that clearly aligns \nwith the content-specific \nlearning goal. \nCandidate describes how they \nwill create a safe, positive \nlearning environment that clearly \nsupports students during the \ncontent-specific lesson. \nCandidate clearly describes how \nthey will leverage the students\u2019 \nassets and/or interests.\n  \nCandidate clearly describes how \nthey will engage students in \nhigher-order thinking** to \ndeepen their content-specific \nunderstanding. \nCandidate clearly describes how \nthey will monitor student \nlearning during the content-\nspecific lesson. \nCandidate clearly describes how \nthey will use UDL-focused \ninstructional strategy(ies) to \nsupport student learning. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s planning clearly \nincludes opportunities for \nstudents to learn collaboratively \nto progress toward the content-\nspecific and related content-\nspecific ALD learning goals. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate designs a lesson that \nreflects the interconnectedness \nof the content-specific area plus \nan additional academic content \narea (e.g., literacy, social science, \nart, science, PE). \nCalTPA: Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 1 \nSingle Subject Learning About Students and Planning Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811 \nAll rights reserved. 19 \nNotes: \n*For example, recent observations, assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), and/or information provided by your \ncooperating teacher and/or supervising faculty \n**For example, analysis, synthesis, evaluation, interpretation, and transfer/generalization \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence:  \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4; TPE 2, Element 2; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5; TPE 4, \nElements 1, 4, 7; TPE 6, Element 5 \n "}, {"number": 2, "pages": [24], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c1-2-24.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 1 \nSingle Subject Learning About Students and Planning Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811 \nAll rights reserved. 20 \nRubric 1.2 \u2014  Step 1: Plan  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply recent learning* to plan adaptation(s) to support the ALD of FS1 based on their \nassets and/or interests and learning need(s)?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS1 are not \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) do \nnot leverage FS1\u2019s assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) do \nnot support FS1\u2019s ALD learning \nneed(s) through reading, \nwriting, listening, and/or \nspeaking. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS1 are \nvaguely based on recent \nlearning* or information used \nis not recent. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nvaguely leverage FS1\u2019s assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nvaguely support FS1\u2019s ALD \nlearning need(s) through \nreading, writing, listening, \nand/or speaking. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS1 are clearly \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nclearly leverage FS1\u2019s assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nclearly support FS1\u2019s ALD \nlearning need(s) through \nreading, writing, listening, \nand/or speaking. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans adaptation(s) \nfor FS1 to participate in the \ncontent-specific lesson by using \nmultiple modes of \ncommunication** to support \nFS1\u2019s learning needs. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate\u2019s planning for FS1\u2019s \nadaptation(s) draws upon \nand/or refers to evidence-\nbased practice(s) and/or \nresearch appropriate to FS1\u2019s \nALD. \nNotes:  \n*For example, recent observations, assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), and/or information provided by your cooperating teacher and/or \nsupervising faculty  \n**For example, collaborative, interpretive, and/or productive language \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 5; TPE 4, Elements 1, 2, 4, 5; TPE 5,  \nElements 2, 8 \n "}, {"number": 3, "pages": [25], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c1-3-25.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 1 \nSingle Subject Learning About Students and Planning Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811 \nAll rights reserved. 21 \nRubric 1.3 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply recent learning* to plan adaptation(s) to support the content-specific learning of \nFS2 based on their assets and/or interests and learning need(s)?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS2 are not \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) do \nnot leverage FS2\u2019s assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) do \nnot support FS2\u2019s content-\nspecific learning need(s).** \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS2 are \nvaguely based on recent \nlearning* or information used \nis not recent. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nvaguely leverage FS2\u2019s assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nvaguely support FS2\u2019s content-\nspecific learning need(s).** \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS2 are clearly \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nclearly leverage FS2\u2019s assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nclearly support FS2\u2019s content-\nspecific learning need(s).** \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans adaptation(s) \nfor FS2 to participate in the \ncontent-specific lesson by using \ntwo or more multisensory \nstrategies*** to support FS2\u2019s \nlearning needs. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate\u2019s planning for FS2\u2019s \nadaptation(s) draws upon \nand/or refers to evidence-\nbased content-specific \npractice(s) and/or research \nappropriate to FS2\u2019s content-\nspecific learning need(s). \nNotes: \n*For example, recent observations, assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), and/or information provided by your cooperating teacher and/or \nsupervising faculty \n**For example, gaps in content-specific knowledge, misconceptions, IEP/504 plan/MTSS with content-specific learning need \n***Multisensory strategies are teaching techniques that engage two or more senses simultaneously\u2014 visual, auditory, kinesthetic, and/or tactile \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 6; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 5; TPE 4, Elements 1, 4, 7; TPE 5, Element 2 \n "}, {"number": 4, "pages": [26], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c1-4-26.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 1 \nSingle Subject Learning About Students and Planning Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811 \nAll rights reserved. 22 \nRubric 1.4 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply recent learning* to adapt the environment to support FS3\u2019s well-being and/or \nbehavior based on their assets and/or interests and learning need(s)? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS3 are not \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) do \nnot leverage FS3\u2019s assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) do \nnot support FS3\u2019s well-being \nand/or behavior to meet their \ncontent-specific learning \nneed(s). \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS3 are \nvaguely based on recent \nlearning* or information used \nis not recent. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nvaguely leverage FS3\u2019s assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nvaguely support FS3\u2019s well-\nbeing and/or behavior to meet \ntheir content-specific learning \nneed(s). \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for FS3 are clearly \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nclearly leverage FS3\u2019s assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned content-\nspecific lesson adaptation(s) \nclearly support FS3\u2019s well-being \nand/or behavior to meet their \ncontent-specific learning \nneed(s). \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans adaptation(s) \nfor FS3 to participate in the \ncontent-specific lesson by \ncreating a targeted, proactive \nplan** to support FS3\u2019s \nlearning needs. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate\u2019s planning for FS3\u2019s \nadaptation(s) draws upon \nand/or refers to evidence-\nbased practice(s) and/or \nresearch appropriate to FS3\u2019s \nwell-being and/or behavior. \nNotes: \n*For example, recent observations, assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), and/or information provided by your cooperating teacher and/or \nsupervising faculty \n**Proactive plan is intentionally setting up a learning environment that is conducive and targeted to the well-being and/or behavioral needs of the student (e.g., \nstructured routines, frequent breaks, safe space, positive reinforcement, minimizing triggering language and/or tasks) \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 5; TPE 4, Elements 1, 2, 4, 5; TPE 5,  \nElements 2, 8 \n "}, {"number": 5, "pages": [30], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c1-5-30.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 1 \nSingle Subject Learning About Students and Planning Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 26 \nStep 2 Rubrics \nRubric 1.5 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate maintain a safe, positive learning environment* and apply a UDL-focused strategy that \nsupports their students in making progress during the content-specific lesson?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmaintain a safe, positive \nlearning environment* during \nthe content-specific lesson. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use a \nUDL-focused strategy(ies) to \nsupport their students during \nthe content-specific lesson. \nCandidate provides a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription with 4 or more \nminor or a major \ninaccuracy(ies) in their content-\nspecific instruction. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the content-\nspecific lesson described in \nStep 1. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmaintain a safe, positive \nlearning environment* during \nthe content-specific lesson. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use a \nUDL-focused strategy(ies) to \nsupport their students during \nthe content-specific lesson. \nCandidate provides a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription with 3 or fewer \nminor inaccuracies in their \ncontent-specific instruction. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the content-\nspecific lesson described in \nStep 1. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a \ndemonstration and description \nof how they clearly maintain a \nsafe, positive learning \nenvironment* during the \ncontent-specific lesson. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they use a UDL-focused \nstrategy(ies) to support their \nstudents during the content-\nspecific lesson. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof accurate content-specific \ninstruction. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof the content-specific lesson \ndescribed in Step 1.  \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they use \nmultiple UDL-focused \nstrategies to support their \nstudents during the content-\nspecific lesson. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate facilitates structured \nopportunities for the students \nto collaboratively explore the \ncontent-specific material \nthrough discussion, writing, \nand/or reading. \nNote: \n*For example: establishing clear expectations; creating a safe and welcoming environment; greeting students; establishing cen tral question(s) and/or lesson hook; \nengaging students; establishing positive rapport \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 3 video clips, totaling up to 15 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 2, Elements 2, 3, 5, 6; TPE 4, Element 4 \n "}, {"number": 6, "pages": [31], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c1-6-31.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 1 \nSingle Subject Learning About Students and Planning Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 27 \nRubric 1.6 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate engage students in higher-order thinking during the content-specific lesson and \nmonitor and respond intentionally to support student learning? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they engage \nthe students in higher-order \nthinking to support student \nlearning. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmonitor and/or respond \nintentionally to support \nstudent learning. \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they engage \nthe students in higher-order \nthinking to support student \nlearning. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmonitor and/or respond \nintentionally to support \nstudent learning. \n \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they engage the \nstudents in higher-order \nthinking to support student \nlearning. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they monitor and \nrespond intentionally to \nsupport student learning. \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they use \nmultiple assessment(s) to \nmonitor and adjust instruction \nto support student learning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they connect \ncontent-specific learning to \nreal-life contexts to engage, \nmotivate, and/or extend \nstudent learning. \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence:  \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 3 video clips, totaling up to 15 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 3, 5, 8; TPE 2, Element 5; TPE 4, Element 4 \n \n "}, {"number": 7, "pages": [34], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c1-7-34.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 1 \nSingle Subject Learning About Students and Planning Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 30 \nStep 3 Rubric \nRubric 1.7 \u2014  Step 3: Reflect  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate reflect on the impact of their asset-based, UDL-focused content-specific lesson for the \ngroup and the focus students in a safe, positive learning environment?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not reflect on \nwhat went well and/or what \ndid not go well or as expected \nin the lesson. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of getting to know \ntheir students before teaching \nthe lesson. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of the safe, positive \nenvironment they created to \ninclude all students. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of their asset-based \napproach. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of their UDL-\nfocused strategy(ies). \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of engaging \nstudents in higher-order \nthinking. \nCandidate does not analyze* \nstudent progress in meeting \nthe content-specific and/or \nALD learning goals. \nCandidate does not analyze* \nthe impact of the adaptation(s) \nused to support FS1, FS2, \nand/or FS3 in making progress \ntoward meeting the learning \ngoals. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nwhat went well and/or what \ndid not go well or as expected \nin the lesson. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of getting to know \ntheir students before teaching \nthe lesson. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of the safe, positive \nenvironment they created to \ninclude all students. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of their asset-based \napproach. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of their UDL-\nfocused strategy(ies). \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of engaging \nstudents in higher-order \nthinking. \nCandidate vaguely analyzes* \nstudent progress in meeting \nthe content-specific and/or \nALD learning goals. \nCandidate vaguely analyzes* \nthe impact of the adaptation(s) \nused to support FS1, FS2, \nand/or FS3 in making progress \ntoward meeting the learning \ngoals. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nwhat went well and what did \nnot go well or as expected in \nthe lesson. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of getting to know \ntheir students before teaching \nthe lesson. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of the safe, positive \nenvironment they created to \ninclude all students. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of their asset-based \napproach. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of their UDL-\nfocused strategy(ies). \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of engaging \nstudents in higher-order \nthinking. \nCandidate clearly analyzes* \nstudent progress in meeting \nthe content-specific and ALD \nlearning goals. \nCandidate clearly analyzes* the \nimpact of the adaptation(s) \nused to support FS1, FS2, and \nFS3 in making progress toward \nmeeting the learning goals. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s reflection \ndemonstrates an \nunderstanding that affirming, \nvalidating, and/or leveraging \nthe students\u2019 assets and/or \ninterests influence student \ncontent-specific learning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nWhen reflecting on the impact \nof their instruction, the \ncandidate refers to specific \nexamples from the teaching of \ntheir content-specific lesson to \ndetermine which strategy(ies) \nmoved learning forward. \n \nNote: \n*Analyze: Involves carefully and systematically looking for recurring themes, common misconceptions, and strengths and/or are as of growth across a collection of \nstudent work in order to gain insights into student understanding. Analysis goes beyond simply describing what happened; it delves into why, what was \nlearned/understood, and what the work reveals about student thinking."}, {"number": 8, "pages": [37], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c1-8-37.png"], "text": "CalTPA: Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 1 \nSingle Subject Learning About Students and Planning Instruction \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 33 \nStep 4 Rubric \nRubric 1.8 \u2014  Step 4: Apply \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply what they have learned to determine next steps for content-specific and ALD \ninstruction? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe what \nthey learned throughout this \ncycle.  \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe how \nthey would advance the \ncontent-specific and/or ALD \nlearning for the group. \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe how \nthey would continue to support \nthe learning need(s) of FS1, \nFS2, and/or FS3. \nCandidate does not use a \nspecific example(s) from Steps \n1, 2, or 3 and/or does not \njustify their next instructional \nmove for content-specific \nlearning, or the provided \nexample does not justify the \nnext instructional move for \ncontent-specific learning. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes what \nthey learned throughout this \ncycle.  \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes how \nthey would advance the \ncontent-specific and/or ALD \nlearning for the group. \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes how \nthey would continue to support \nthe learning need(s) of FS1, \nFS2, and/or FS3. \nCandidate uses an example(s) \nfrom Steps 1, 2, or 3 that \nvaguely justifies their next \ninstructional move for content-\nspecific learning. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate's application of next \nsteps clearly describes what \nthey learned throughout this \ncycle.  \nCandidate's application of next \nsteps clearly describes how \nthey would advance the \ncontent-specific and ALD \nlearning for the group. \nCandidate's application of next \nsteps clearly describes how \nthey would continue to support \nthe learning need(s) of FS1, \nFS2, and FS3. \nCandidate uses a specific \nexample(s) from Steps 1, 2, or 3 \nthat clearly justifies their next \ninstructional move for content-\nspecific learning. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s responses \ndemonstrate how they will \ncontinue to leverage students\u2019 \nassets and/or interests in \nfuture lessons to support \ncontent-specific learning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate describes how they \nwill apply what they have \nlearned about the group and \nthe focus students to plan \nfuture instruction that clearly \nreinforces, strengthens, and/or \nextends students\u2019 higher-order \nthinking related to content-\nspecific and ALD learning. \nStep 4 Source of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part H: Application of Learning (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL response) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 3, Element 2; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 6, Element 1 \n \n "}], "ss-c2": [{"number": 1, "pages": [26, 27], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c2-1-26.png", "assets/rubrics/ss-c2-1-27.png"], "text": "CalTPA Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 2 \nSingle Subject Assessment-Driven Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 22 \nStep 1 Rubrics \nRubric 2.1 \u2014 Step 1: Plan  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use their knowledge of CA State standard(s)/framework(s) and ELD standard(s), along \nwith information gathered from recent learning/assessments,* to develop appropriate content-specific and ELD learning goals? How \ndoes the candidate develop formative and summative assessments that target the learning goals? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not use \nlearning/assessment information \nto plan instruction. \nCandidate does not select a \ncontent-specific CA State \nstandard(s) and/or framework(s) \nand/or ELD standard(s) for the unit \nplan OR selects standards that are \nmore than one grade level above \nor below the students they plan to \nteach in the unit plan. \nCandidate does not include a \ncontent-specific goal for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate does not include an ELD \ngoal, OR the ELD goal(s) are not \ngrade-level appropriate, OR the \nELD goal(s) do not support the \ncontent-specific learning goal(s) for \nthe unit plan. \nCandidate does not plan formative \nassessments in each lesson, OR the \nformative assessments are not \nconnected to the content-specific \nand/or ELD learning goal(s) of the \nunit plan. \nCandidate does not plan a \nsummative assessment OR plans a \nsummative assessment that does \nnot measure the students\u2019 \nprogress in meeting the content-\nspecific and ELD learning goals for \nthe unit plan. \nCandidate does not include a \nrubric or performance criteria, OR \nthe included rubric or performance \ncriteria for the summative \nassessment do not measure the \ncontent-specific and ELD learning \ngoals for the unit plan. \nCandidate\u2019s planning has 4 or more \nminor errors or a major error. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely uses \ninformation from recent \nlearning/assessments* or the \ninformation used to plan \ninstruction is not recent. \nCandidate selects content-specific, \nCA State standard(s) and/or \nframework(s) and ELD standard(s) \nthat are either one grade level \nabove or one grade level below the \ngrade of the students they plan to \nteach in the unit plan. \nCandidate develops a content-\nspecific learning goal(s) that is not \ngrade-level appropriate for the \nunit plan. \nCandidate develops ELD goal(s) \nthat vaguely support the content-\nspecific learning goal(s) for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate plans one or more \nformative assessment(s) in each \nlesson that vaguely connect to the \ncontent-specific and/or ELD \nlearning goal(s) of the unit plan. \nCandidate plans one summative \nassessment that vaguely measures \nthe students\u2019 progress in meeting \nthe content-specific and ELD \nlearning goals for the unit plan.  \nCandidate includes a rubric or \nperformance criteria for the \nsummative assessment that \nvaguely measure the content-\nspecific and ELD learning goals for \nthe unit plan. \nCandidate\u2019s planning includes 3 or \nfewer minor errors. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly uses information \nfrom recent learning/assessments* \nto plan instruction. \nCandidate selects grade-level \nappropriate content-specific CA \nState standard(s) and/or \nframework(s) and ELD standard(s) \nfor the unit plan. \nCandidate develops grade-level \nappropriate content-specific \nlearning goal(s) for the unit plan. \nCandidate develops grade-level \nappropriate ELD goal(s) that clearly \nsupport the content-specific \nlearning goal(s) for the unit plan. \nCandidate plans one or more \nformative assessment(s) in each \nlesson that are clearly connected \nto the content-specific and/or ELD \nlearning goal(s) of the unit plan. \nCandidate plans one summative \nassessment that clearly measures \nthe students\u2019 progress in meeting \nthe content-specific and ELD \nlearning goals for the unit plan.  \nCandidate includes a rubric or \nperformance criteria for the \nsummative assessment that clearly \nmeasure the content-specific \nand ELD learning goals for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate\u2019s planning is accurate. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans multiple formative \nassessments within each lesson of \nthe unit to monitor the group\u2019s \ncontent-specific and ELD \nunderstandings and learning \nneeds. \nL\nevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus \nWhen developing the summative \nassessment and corresponding \nrubric or performance criteria, the \ncandidate includes real-life \nscenarios that build upon the \ngroup\u2019s culture, language(s), \nand/or experiences. \nCalTPA Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 2 \nSingle Subject Assessment-Driven Instruction \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 23 \nNote: \n*For example, recent observations, assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), and/or information provide d by your \ncooperating teacher and/or supervising faculty \nSources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Unit Plan (up to 20 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Summative Assessment Description (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Copies of Blank Summative Assessment and Rubric or Performance Criteria (up to 3 \npages) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 6; TPE 4, Elements 4, 7, 8; TPE 6,  \nElement 5 "}, {"number": 2, "pages": [28], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c2-2-28.png"], "text": "CalTPA Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 2 \nSingle Subject Assessment-Driven Instruction \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 24 \nRubric 2.2 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate plan an aligned series of lessons that build upon one another to create a progression of \nlearning? How does the candidate leverage student assets and/or interests, actively engage the students in the content-specific \nlearning and ELD learning, and use UDL strategies to support the group, including the FS? \nLevel 1 \nIn their planning, the candidate \ndoes not leverage student \nassets and/or interests.  \nCandidate does not plan to use \nUDL strategy(ies) when \nteaching the content-specific \nand/or ELD content. \nThe unit plan does not include \nthree lessons or the lessons do \nnot align and build upon one \nanother to create a progression \nof learning. \nCandidate does not plan active, \nengaging content-specific \ninstruction.  \nCandidate\u2019s plan does not \nintegrate ELD, and/or the ELD \ndoes not support content-\nspecific learning.  \nCandidate does not include \nadaptation(s) that address the \nFS\u2019s assets and/or interests \nand/or learning needs. \nLevel 2 \nIn their planning, the candidate \nvaguely leverages student \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate vaguely plans to use \nUDL strategy(ies) when \nteaching the content-specific \nand/or ELD content. \nThe unit plan includes three \nlessons that vaguely align and \nbuild upon one another to \ncreate a progression of \nlearning. \nCandidate vaguely plans active, \nengaging content-specific \ninstruction.  \nCandidate plans to integrate \nELD that vaguely supports \ncontent-specific learning.  \nCandidate includes \nadaptation(s) that vaguely \naddress the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests and/or learning \nneeds. \nLevel 3 \nIn their planning, the candidate \nclearly leverages student assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate clearly plans to use \nappropriate UDL strategy(ies) \nwhen teaching the content-\nspecific and/or ELD content. \nThe unit plan includes three \nlessons that clearly align and \nbuild upon one another to \ncreate a progression of \nlearning. \nCandidate clearly plans active, \nengaging content-specific \ninstruction.  \nCandidate plans to integrate \nELD that clearly supports \ncontent-specific learning.  \nCandidate includes a plan for \nadaptation(s) that clearly \naddress the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests and learning needs. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate leverages student \nassets by intentionally \nincorporating students\u2019 cultural \nbackgrounds, lived experiences, \ninterests, and/or primary \nlanguages into the lesson \ndesign. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nEach of the candidate\u2019s lessons \ninclude multiple UDL strategies \nto intentionally support the \ngroup and the FS based on \ntheir assets and/or interests \nand learning needs. \nSources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Unit Plan (up to 20 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Summative Assessment Description (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Copies of Blank Summative Assessment and Rubric or Performance Criteria (up to 3 pages) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 1; TPE 3, Elements 2, 5; TPE 5, Elements 1, 3 \n \n "}, {"number": 3, "pages": [32], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c2-3-32.png"], "text": "CalTPA Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 2 \nSingle Subject Assessment-Driven Instruction \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 28 \nStep 2 Rubrics \nRubric 2.3 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate engage students in deep learning of the content to enhance their content-specific \nknowledge, skills, and/or abilities? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not \ndemonstrate and/or describe \nhow they engage students in \ndeep learning of the content to \nenhance the students\u2019 content-\nspecific knowledge, skills, \nand/or abilities. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the content-\nspecific lesson(s) described in \nStep 1.  \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescribes how they engage \nstudents in deep learning of the \ncontent to enhance the \nstudents\u2019 content-specific \nknowledge, skills, and/or \nabilities. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the content-\nspecific lesson(s) described in \nStep 1.  \n \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly demonstrates \nand describes how they engage \nstudents in deep learning of the \ncontent to enhance the \nstudents\u2019 content-specific \nknowledge, skills, and/or \nabilities. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof the content-specific lesson(s) \ndescribed in Step 1.  \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate is responsive to \nstudents\u2019 age, language, \nand/or content-specific \ndevelopment to deepen and/or \nadvance students\u2019 knowledge \nrelated to the content. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they \nintentionally design and \nimplement learning \nexperiences that deeply engage \nstudents in the content. \nSources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nContent-Specific Pedagogy  \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 8; TPE 3, Element 5; TPE 5, Element 8 "}, {"number": 4, "pages": [33], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c2-4-33.png"], "text": "CalTPA Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 2 \nSingle Subject Assessment-Driven Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 29 \nRubric 2.4 \u2014 Step 2: Teach and Assess  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use UDL strategy(ies), leverage student assets and/or interests, and actively engage \nstudents in the content-specific and ELD learning? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nleverage student assets and/or \ninterests while teaching the \ncontent-specific and/or ELD \ncontent.  \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the UDL \nstrategy(ies) they use to teach \nthe content-specific and/or ELD \ncontent.  \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage the students \nduring the content-specific \nand/or ELD learning. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nleverage student assets and/or \ninterests while teaching the \ncontent-specific and/or ELD \ncontent.  \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the UDL \nstrategy(ies) they use to teach \nthe content-specific and/or ELD \ncontent.  \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage the students \nduring the content-specific \nand/or ELD learning. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they leverage student \nassets and/or interests while \nteaching the content-specific \nand/or ELD content.  \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof the UDL strategy(ies) they \nuse to teach the content-\nspecific and/or ELD content.  \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they actively engage the \nstudents during the content-\nspecific and/or ELD learning.\n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate incorporates the \nstudents\u2019 cultural backgrounds, \nlived experiences, interests, \nand/or primary languages into \nthe content-specific instruction. \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof multiple UDL strategies used \nto support the teaching of the \ncontent-specific and ELD \nlearning goals that address the \nFS\u2019s assets and/or interests and \nlearning needs. \nSources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes)\n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary)\nContent-Specific Pedagogy \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 8; TPE 3, Element 3; TPE 4, Elements 3, 4; TPE 5, Elements 1, 2"}, {"number": 5, "pages": [34], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c2-5-34.png"], "text": "CalTPA Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 2 \nSingle Subject Assessment-Driven Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 30 \nRubric 2.5 \u2014 Step 2: Teach and Assess  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate integrate ELD to support the group in making progress toward the content-specific \ngoal(s)? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nintegrate ELD to support the \ncontent-specific instruction. \nL\nevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nintegrate ELD to support the \ncontent-specific instruction. \nL\nevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they integrate ELD to \nsupport the content-specific \ninstruction. \nL\nevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate purposefully \nleverages students\u2019 assets \nand/or interests and explains \nwhy this instructional approach \nsupports students in \nprogressing toward meeting \nthe content-specific and ELD \ngoals. \nL\nevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nCandidate encourages \ntranslanguaging as a resource \nto deepen comprehension and \nlanguage transfer. \nSources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes)\n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary)\nContent-Specific Pedagogy \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 5, Element 3 "}, {"number": 6, "pages": [35], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c2-6-35.png"], "text": "CalTPA Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 2 \nSingle Subject Assessment-Driven Instruction \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 31 \nRubric 2.6 \u2014 Step 2: Teach and Assess  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use formative assessment(s) to monitor student learning and respond intentionally to \nsupport student progress toward meeting the content-specific and/or ELD goals? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use \nformative assessment(s) to \nmonitor student progress \ntoward meeting the content-\nspecific and/or ELD goals. \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nrespond intentionally to \nformative assessment results to \nsupport student progress \ntoward meeting the content-\nspecific and/or ELD goals. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use \nformative assessment(s) to \nmonitor student progress \ntoward meeting the content-\nspecific and/or ELD goals. \nCandidate includes an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nrespond intentionally to \nformative assessment results to \nsupport student progress \ntoward meeting the content-\nspecific and/or ELD goals. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they use formative \nassessment(s) to monitor \nstudent progress toward \nmeeting the content-specific \nand/or ELD goals. \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they respond \nintentionally to formative \nassessment results to support \nstudent progress toward \nmeeting the content-specific \nand/or ELD goals. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate uses multiple \nformative assessments, such as \npurposeful questioning, \nacademic discussion, analysis of \nstudent work, and/or real-time \nchecks for understanding, to \nmonitor student learning in \nrelation to both content-\nspecific and ELD goals. \nL\nevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 and 4, plus: \nCandidate analyzes student \nresponses during instruction to \nidentify patterns and any \nmisconceptions. Based on this \nevidence, the candidate \nimmediately adapts instruction \nin meaningful ways, including \ndifferentiating tasks, providing \ntargeted scaffolds, and/or \nextending the learning for the \nstudents. \nSources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes)\n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary)\nContent-Specific Pedagogy \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 5, Elements 1, 3, 5 "}, {"number": 7, "pages": [40], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c2-7-40.png"], "text": "CalTPA Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 2 \nSingle Subject Assessment-Driven Instruction \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 36 \nStep 3 Rubric \nRubric 2.7 \u2014  Step 3: Reflect \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use their analysis of the summative assessment results to identify students\u2019 \nunderstandings and/or misconceptions and to provide specific, actionable feedback to the FS? How does the candidate use their \nanalysis of the summative assessment results and the information they gathered during their teaching to determine instructional \nchanges they would make if they taught the unit again? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s reflection does not \ninclude an analysis of the \ngroup\u2019s and/or the FS\u2019s \nsummative assessment results. \nCandidate does not identify \nunderstandings and/or \nmisconceptions related to the \ngroup\u2019s and/or the FS\u2019s \nprogress toward meeting the \ncontent-specific and ELD goals, \nOR the candidate does not \nbase their analysis on the \ninformation they gathered \nwhile teaching and/or the \nsummative assessment results. \nCandidate does not provide \nspecific, actionable feedback \nfor the FS, OR the specific, \nactionable feedback is not \nbased on the summative \nassessment results. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndescription of the impact of \ntheir instructional approach(es) \nand/or their choice of student \nactivity(ies) during the unit for \nthe group, including the FS. \nCandidate does not provide an \nexplanation of what \ninstructional changes they \nwould make for the group, \nincluding the FS, if they taught \nthe unit again. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s reflection includes \na vague analysis of the group\u2019s \nand/or the FS\u2019s summative \nassessment results. \nBased on their analysis of the \nsummative assessment results \nand the information gathered \nduring their teaching, the \ncandidate vaguely identifies \nunderstandings and/or \nmisconceptions related to the \ngroup\u2019s and/or the FS\u2019s \nprogress toward meeting the \ncontent-specific and/or ELD \ngoals. \nCandidate provides feedback to \nthe FS that is vague, not \nactionable, or not clearly based \non the student\u2019s performance \non the summative assessment. \nCandidate provides a vague \ndescription of the impact of \ntheir instructional approach(es) \nand/or their choice of student \nactivity(ies) during the unit for \nthe group, including the FS. \nCandidate provides a vague \nexplanation of what \ninstructional changes they \nwould make for the group, \nincluding the FS, if they taught \nthe unit again. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s reflection includes \na clear analysis of the group\u2019s \nand the FS\u2019s summative \nassessment results. \nBased on their analysis of the \nsummative assessment results \nand the information gathered \nduring their teaching, the \ncandidate clearly identifies \nunderstandings and/or \nmisconceptions related to the \ngroup\u2019s and the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the content-\nspecific and ELD goals. \nCandidate provides specific, \nactionable feedback for the FS \nthat is clearly based on the FS\u2019s \nperformance on the summative \nassessment. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndescription of the impact of \ntheir instructional approach(es) \nand their choice of student \nactivity(ies) during the unit for \nthe group, including the FS. \nCandidate provides a clear \nexplanation of what \ninstructional changes they \nwould make for the group, \nincluding the FS, if they taught \nthe unit again. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s specific, actionable \nfeedback for the FS is aligned \nto the learning goals of the \nunit. It clearly names what the \nFS did well and/or what needs \nimprovement based on specific \nstrengths and/or needs of the \nFS using concrete examples \nfrom the summative \nassessment and/or \nobservations. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nWhen reflecting on the impact \nof their instruction, the \ncandidate uses data from the \nunit plan, specific examples \nfrom their teaching or \ncommentary, and data from \nformative and/or summative \nassessment(s) to determine \nwhich strategies moved \nlearning forward. "}, {"number": 8, "pages": [44], "images": ["assets/rubrics/ss-c2-8-44.png"], "text": "CalTPA Performance Assessment Guide Instructional Cycle 2 \nSingle Subject Assessment-Driven Instruction \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 40 \nStep 4 Rubric \nRubric 2.8 \u2014  Step 4: Apply \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply the analysis of the FS\u2019s performance during the unit and summative assessment \nresults to plan, provide an explanation for, and teach a follow-up activity (referring to specific examples from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3)? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not include a \nplan for follow-up instruction \nfor the FS, either re-teaching or \nextension, based on their \nanalysis of the FS\u2019s \nperformance during the unit, \nincluding summative \nassessment results.  \nThe video and commentary do \nnot include either: \n\u2022 Re-Teach: A demonstration \nand/or description of the \ntargeted intervention \nand/or individualized \nstrategy used to support the \nFS\u2019s progress toward \nmeeting the content-\nspecific and/or ELD learning \ngoal(s) \nOR \n\u2022 Extend: A demonstration \nand/or description of the \ninstruction that deepens \nand/or advances the FS\u2019s \ncontent-specific and/or ELD \nlearning  \nCandidate does not include \nspecific example(s) from Steps \n1, 2, and/or 3 to support their \nfollow-up instructional decision \nmaking, OR evidence cited does \nnot support follow-up \ninstructional decisions. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate includes a vague \nplan for follow-up instruction \nfor the FS, either re-teaching or \nextension, based on their \nanalysis of the FS\u2019s \nperformance during the unit, \nincluding summative \nassessment results.  \nThe video and/or commentary \ninclude either: \n\u2022 Re-Teach: An unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the targeted \nintervention and/or \nindividualized strategy used \nto support the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the content-\nspecific and/or ELD learning \ngoal(s) \nOR \n\u2022 Extend: An unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the instruction \nthat deepens and/or \nadvances the FS\u2019s content-\nspecific and/or ELD learning  \nCandidate uses vague \nexample(s) from Steps 1, 2, \nand/or 3 to support their \nfollow-up instructional decision \nmaking. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate includes a clear plan \nfor follow-up instruction for the \nFS, either re-teaching or \nextension, based on their \nanalysis of the FS\u2019s \nperformance during the unit, \nincluding summative \nassessment results.  \nThe video and commentary \ninclude either: \n\u2022 Re-Teach: A clear \ndemonstration and \ndescription of the targeted \nintervention and/or \nindividualized strategy used \nto support the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \ncontent-specific and/or ELD \nlearning goal(s) \nOR \n\u2022 Extend: A clear \ndemonstration and \ndescription of the \ninstruction that deepens \nand/or advances the FS\u2019s \ncontent-specific and/or ELD \nlearning  \nCandidate clearly uses specific \nexample(s) from Steps 1, 2, \nand/or 3 to support their \nfollow-up instructional decision \nmaking. \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate leverages the FS\u2019s \nassets by intentionally \nincorporating the student\u2019s \ncultural, linguistic, and/or lived \nexperiences into the follow-up \ninstruction design. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate synthesizes multiple \nsources of evidence from Steps \n1, 2, and 3 to justify their \ninstructional decisions and \nclearly explains how the \nselected strategy accelerates \nthe FS\u2019s learning of the \ncontent-specific and/or ELD \ncontent. "}], "mmsn-math": [{"number": 1, "pages": [28, 29], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-1-28.png", "assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-1-29.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 23 \nStep 1 Rubrics \nRubric 1.1 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply contextual information and support math-related IEP goal(s)* to plan one math \nlesson for one focus student (FS) with an IEP?  \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not select an \nage-/grade-level appropriate \nCA State Math Content \nStrand/Standard or \ndevelopmentally appropriate \nCore Content Connector \n(CCC)** and/or a Math Practice \nStandard or they select a \nstrand/standard that is more \nthan one grade level above or \nbelow the FS they plan to \nteach. \nCandidate does not develop a \nmath learning goal*** that is \nbased on the selected math \nstrand/standard or CCC and/or \ndoes not include math content \nand/or practice. \nCandidate does not develop a \nmath ALD learning goal or the \nALD learning goal does not \nalign with the math learning \ngoal.*** \nCandidate does not describe \nhow the lesson will support the \nFS\u2019s math-related IEP goal(s).* \nLevel 2 \nCandidate selects an age-/ \ngrade-level appropriate CA \nState Math Content \nStrand/Standard or \ndevelopmentally appropriate \nCore Content Connector \n(CCC)** and/or a Math Practice \nStandard that are either one \ngrade level above or below the \nFS they plan to teach. \nCandidate develops a math \nlearning goal*** that is vaguely \nbased on the selected math \nstrand/standard or CCC and \nincludes math content and \npractice. \nCandidate develops a math ALD \nlearning goal that vaguely \naligns with the math learning \ngoal.*** \nCandidate vaguely describes \nhow the lesson will support the \nFS\u2019s math-related IEP goal(s).* \nLevel 3 \nCandidate selects an age-/ \ngrade-level appropriate CA \nState Math Content \nStrand/Standard or \ndevelopmentally appropriate \nCore Content Connector \n(CCC)** and a Math Practice \nStandard for the FS they plan to \nteach.  \nCandidate develops a math \nlearning goal*** that is clearly \nbased on the selected math \nstrand/standard or CCC and \nincludes both math content \nand practice. \nCandidate develops a math ALD \nlearning goal that clearly aligns \nwith the math learning \ngoal.*** \nCandidate clearly describes \nhow the lesson will support the \nFS\u2019s math-related IEP goal(s).* \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s planning clearly \nincludes opportunities for the \nFS to learn collaboratively to \nprogress toward the math \ncontent and practice and math \nALD learning goals. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate designs a math \nlesson that reflects the \ninterconnectedness of math \nplus an additional academic \ncontent area (e.g., literacy, \nsocial science, art, science, PE).\nNotes: \n*Math-related IEP goal(s): Any IEP goal(s) that is related to the content or practice the candidate plans to teach in the math lesson (e.g., math IEP goal, IEP goal \nrelated to math content and/or practice, IEP goal related to academic language, IEP goal related to well-being or behavior that would impact access to the math \nlesson). \n**If the FS accesses an alternate curriculum that makes them eligible for the California Alternate Assessments (CAA), you may use gr ade-level/developmentally \nappropriate Core Content Connectors instead of the CA Common Core State Standards.  \n***Math learning goal includes strand(s)/standard(s) and math practice. Candidates placed in transitional kindergarten classroom s should use age-level \ndevelopmental strands from the California Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations (PTKLF).  \nEdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 24 \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence:  \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Math Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Math Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4, 5; TPE 2, Element 2; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5; TPE 4, \nElements 1, 4; TPE 5, Elements 2, 8; TPE 6, Element 5; TPE 7, Element 9 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 3, 4; TPE 2, Elements 2, 7, 8; TPE 3, Elements 1, \n2; TPE 4, Elements 2, 4, 6, 7; TPE 6, Element 1 \n "}, {"number": 2, "pages": [30], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-2-30.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 25 \nRubric 1.2 \u2014  Step 1: Plan  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply findings from recent learning* to plan one UDL-focused math lesson that engages \nthe FS in higher-order thinking in a safe, positive environment**?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not use \ninformation from recent math \nlearning* and/or math-related \nIEP goal(s) to plan instruction. \nCandidate does not describe \nhow they will engage the FS in \nUDL-focused instructional \nstrategy(ies). \nCandidate does not describe \nhow they will engage the FS in \nage and/or developmentally \nappropriate higher-order \nthinking to deepen their math \nunderstanding. \nCandidate does not describe \nhow they will create a safe, \npositive learning \nenvironment** or does not \ndescribe how the environment \nwill support the FS during the \nmath lesson. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely uses \ninformation from recent math \nlearning* and/or math-related \nIEP goal(s) to plan instruction. \nCandidate vaguely describes \nhow they will engage the FS in \nUDL-focused instructional \nstrategy(ies). \nCandidate vaguely describes \nhow they will engage the FS in \nage and/or developmentally \nappropriate higher-order \nthinking to deepen their math \nunderstanding. \nCandidate vaguely describes \nhow they will create a safe, \npositive learning \nenvironment** or vaguely \ndescribes how the learning \nenvironment will support the \nFS during the math lesson. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly uses \ninformation from recent math \nlearning* and math-related IEP \ngoal(s) to plan instruction. \nCandidate clearly describes \nhow they will engage the FS in \nUDL-focused instructional \nstrategy(ies). \nCandidate clearly describes \nhow they will engage the FS in \nage and/or developmentally \nappropriate higher-order \nthinking to deepen their math \nunderstanding. \nCandidate clearly describes \nhow they will create a safe, \npositive learning \nenvironment** that supports \nthe FS during the math lesson. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans for a safe, \npositive environment that \nconsiders the organization of \nspace, time, and/or materials \nto support the learning needs \nof the FS during the math \nlesson.      \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate intentionally \nincorporates functionally \nappropriate skills with math \nlearning to increase the FS's \nindependence in real-world \nenvironments.\n \nNotes:  \n*For example, recent observations or math assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), IEP information, as well as information related to th e \nstudents\u2019 math knowledge, skills, and/or abilities provided by your cooperating teacher, supervising faculty, service providers, and/or families/guardians  \n**For example, take intellectual risks, create safe and welcoming environment, foster positive attitudes toward mathematics, encourage students\u2019 curiosity and/or \npersistence, physical safety, providing a barrier-free space for independent mobility and/or sensory accommodations. \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Math Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Math Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4, 6; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 5, 6; TPE 4, Elements 1, 2, 4, 7; TPE 5, \nElements 2, 7, 8 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 2, 3; TPE 2, Element 1; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 3; TPE 4, Elements 2, \n4, 7; TPE 5, Elements 2, 4, 5 "}, {"number": 3, "pages": [31], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-3-31.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 26 \nRubric 1.3 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate monitor the FS\u2019s learning and collaborate and/or facilitate instructional \nsupport personnel during the math lesson?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not describe \nhow they will monitor the FS\u2019s \nlearning during the math \nlesson. \nCandidate does not describe \nhow they will collaborate with \nand/or facilitate instructional \nsupport personnel to either:  \n\u2022 provide individualized \ninstruction to the FS during \nthe math lesson OR  \n\u2022 support the daily routines, \nactivities, instruction, \nand/or intervention \nactivities of other students \nwhile the candidate \nfacilitates the math lesson. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely describes \nhow they will monitor the FS\u2019s \nlearning during the math \nlesson. \nCandidate vaguely describes \nhow they will collaborate with \nand/or facilitate instructional \nsupport personnel to either:  \n\u2022 provide individualized \ninstruction to the FS during \nthe math lesson OR  \n\u2022 support the daily routines, \nactivities, instruction, and/or \nintervention activities of \nother students while the \ncandidate facilitates the \nmath lesson. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly describes \nhow they will monitor the FS\u2019s \nlearning during the math \nlesson. \nCandidate clearly describes \nhow they will collaborate with \nand/or facilitate instructional \nsupport personnel to either: \n\u2022 provide individualized \ninstruction to the FS during \nthe math lesson OR  \n\u2022 support the daily routines, \nactivities, instruction, and/or \nintervention activities of \nother students while the \ncandidate facilitates the \nmath lesson. \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s lesson plan \ndemonstrates a purposeful \nintegration of the instructional \nsupport personnel into the \nclassroom environment, \nactivities, and routines to \nmaximize the FS\u2019s learning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate collaborates with \ntheir cooperating \nteacher/mentor, instructional \nsupport personnel, and/or \nparents/guardians to identify \nthe FS\u2019s assets and/or interests \nto plan a math lesson that \nengages the FS across routines, \nactivities, and/or \nenvironments. \n \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Math Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Math Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4, 5; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 5, 6; TPE 4, Elements 1, 2, 4; TPE 5, Elements \n2, 8 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 4; TPE 2, Element 1; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 3; TPE 4, Elements 2, 4, \n7; TPE 5, Element 2 "}, {"number": 4, "pages": [32], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-4-32.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 27 \nRubric 1.4 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply recent learning* to plan adaptation(s)** for the FS based on their assets and/or \ninterests, learning needs, and IEP goal(s)? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for the FS are not \nbased on recent learning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s)** do not \nleverage the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) do not \nsupport the FS\u2019s math and/or \nALD learning need(s). \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) do not \nsupport the FS\u2019s math-related \nIEP goal(s).\u2020 \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for the FS are \nvaguely based on recent \nlearning* or information used \nis not recent. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s)** vaguely \nleverage the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) vaguely \nsupport the FS\u2019s math and/or \nALD learning need(s). \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) vaguely \nsupport the FS\u2019s math-related \nIEP goal(s).\u2020 \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s planned \nadaptation(s) for the FS are \nclearly based on recent \nlearning.* \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s)** clearly \nleverage the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests. \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) clearly \nsupport the FS\u2019s math and ALD \nlearning need(s). \nCandidate\u2019s planned math \nlesson adaptation(s) clearly \nsupport the FS\u2019s math-related \nIEP goal(s).\u2020 \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans adaptation(s) \nfor the FS to participate in the \nmath lesson by using two or \nmore multisensory strategies\u2021 \nto support the FS\u2019s learning \nneeds. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate\u2019s planning for the \nFS's adaptation(s) draw upon \nand/or refer to evidence-based \nmath practice(s) and/or \nresearch appropriate to the \nFS\u2019s math learning need(s). \nNotes: \n*For example, recent observations or math assessment results (e.g., formative, summative, state testing), IEP information, as  well as information related to the FS\u2019s \nmath knowledge, skills, and/or abilities provided by your cooperating teacher, supervising faculty, service providers, and/or families/guardians \n**Adaptations may include accommodations, modifications, and/or augmentative and alternative communication (AAC) (low -, mid-, or high-tech, or a combination). \n\u2020Math-related IEP goal(s): Any IEP goal(s) that are related to the content or practice the candidate plans to teach in the math les son (e.g., math IEP goal, IEP goal \nrelated to math content and/or practice, IEP goal related to academic language, IEP goal related to well-being or behavior that would impact access to the math \nlesson). \n\u2021Multisensory strategies are teaching techniques that engage two or more senses simultaneously\u2014 visual, auditory, kinesthetic, and/or tactile. \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 4 pages) \n\u2022 Part B: Math Lesson Plan (up to 5 pages) \n\u2022 Part C: Focus Student Adaptation(s) (up to 10 pages) \n\u2022 Part D: Math Lesson Resources and/or Materials (up to 7 pages) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4; TPE 2, Elements 1, 2, 3, 4; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 5, 6; TPE 4, Elements \n1, 2, 4; TPE 5, Elements 2, 8 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 7; TPE 2, Elements 1, 7; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 3; TPE 4, Elements 2, \n3, 7; TPE 5, Element 2 \n \n "}, {"number": 5, "pages": [36], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-5-36.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 31 \nStep 2 Rubrics \nRubric 1.5 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate maintain a safe, positive learning environment* and apply a UDL-focused strategy that \nsupports the FS in making progress during the math lesson?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmaintain a safe, positive \nlearning environment* during \nthe math lesson. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use a \nUDL-focused strategy(ies) to \nsupport the FS during the math \nlesson. \nCandidate provides a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription with 4 or more \nminor or a major \ninaccuracy(ies) in their math \ncontent instruction. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the math lesson \ndescribed in Step 1. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmaintain a safe, positive \nlearning environment* during \nthe math lesson. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use a \nUDL-focused strategy(ies) to \nsupport the FS during the math \nlesson. \nCandidate provides a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription with 3 or fewer \nminor inaccuracies in their \nmath content instruction. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the math lesson \ndescribed in Step 1. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they maintain a safe, \npositive learning environment* \nduring the math lesson. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they use a UDL-focused \nstrategy(ies) to support the FS \nduring the math lesson. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof accurate math content \ninstruction. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof the math lesson described in \nStep 1.  \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they use \nmultiple UDL-focused \nstrategies to support the FS \nduring the math lesson. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate intentionally \ndemonstrates and describes \nhow they leverage the FS\u2019s \nassets and/or interests during \nthe math lesson. \nNote: \n*For example, take intellectual risks, create safe and welcoming environment, foster positive attitudes toward mathematics, encourage FS\u2019s curiosity and/or \npersistence, physical safety, providing a barrier-free space for independent mobility and/or sensory accommodations \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 3 video clips, totaling up to 15 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \n \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 4; TPE 2, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5, 6; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 7, Element 9 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 4, 7; TPE 2, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10; TPE 3, Element 2; TPE 4, \nElement 3 \n \n "}, {"number": 6, "pages": [37], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-6-37.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 32 \nRubric 1.6 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate engage the FS in developmentally appropriate higher-order thinking during the math \nlesson and monitor and respond intentionally to support the FS\u2019s learning during the math lesson? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they engage \nthe FS in developmentally \nappropriate higher-order \nthinking during the math \nlesson. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmonitor and/or respond \nintentionally to support the \nFS\u2019s learning during the math \nlesson. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they engage \nthe FS in developmentally \nappropriate higher-order \nthinking during the math \nlesson. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nmonitor and/or respond \nintentionally to support the \nFS\u2019s learning during the math \nlesson. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they engage the FS in \ndevelopmentally appropriate \nhigher-order thinking during \nthe math lesson. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they monitor and \nrespond intentionally to \nsupport the FS\u2019s learning during \nthe math lesson. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they use \nmultiple assessment(s) to \nmonitor and adjust instruction \nto support the FS\u2019s learning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate demonstrates and \ndescribes how they connect \nmath learning to real-life \ncontexts to engage, motivate, \nand/or extend the FS\u2019s \nlearning. \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence:  \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 3 video clips, totaling up to 15 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 3, 5, 8; TPE 2, Element 5; TPE 3, Element 3; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 5, \nElement 5 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 4; TPE 2, Elements 1, 10; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 5, Element 1  \n \n "}, {"number": 7, "pages": [40, 41], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-7-40.png", "assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-7-41.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 35 \nStep 3 Rubric \nRubric 1.7 \u2014  Step 3: Reflect  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate reflect on the impact of their asset-based, UDL-focused math lesson for the FS in a safe, \npositive learning environment?\nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not reflect on \nwhat went well and/or what \ndid not go well or as expected \nin the lesson. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of getting to know \nthe FS before teaching the \nlesson. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of the safe, positive \nenvironment they created. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of their asset-based \napproach. \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of their UDL-\nfocused strategy(ies). \nCandidate does not reflect on \nthe impact of engaging the FS \nin higher-order thinking. \nCandidate does not analyze* \nthe FS\u2019s progress in meeting \nthe math and/or ALD learning \ngoals. \nCandidate does not analyze* \nthe impact of the adaptation(s) \nused to support the FS in \nmaking progress toward \nmeeting the learning goals. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nwhat went well and/or what \ndid not go well or as expected \nin the lesson. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of getting to know \nthe FS before teaching the \nlesson. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of the safe, positive \nenvironment they created. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of their asset-based \napproach. \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of their UDL-\nfocused strategy(ies). \nCandidate vaguely reflects on \nthe impact of engaging the FS \nin higher-order thinking. \nCandidate vaguely analyzes* \nthe FS\u2019s progress in meeting \nthe math and/or ALD learning \ngoals. \nCandidate vaguely analyzes* \nthe impact of the adaptation(s) \nused to support the FS in \nmaking progress toward \nmeeting the learning goals. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nwhat went well and what did \nnot go well or as expected in \nthe lesson. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of getting to know \nthe FS before teaching the \nlesson. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of the safe, positive \nenvironment they created. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of their asset-based \napproach. \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of their UDL-\nfocused strategy(ies). \nCandidate clearly reflects on \nthe impact of engaging the FS \nin higher-order thinking. \nCandidate clearly analyzes* the \nFS\u2019s progress in meeting the \nmath and ALD learning goals. \nCandidate clearly analyzes* the \nimpact of the adaptation(s) \nused to support the FS in \nmaking progress toward \nmeeting the learning goals. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s reflection \ndemonstrates an \nunderstanding that affirming, \nvalidating, and/or leveraging \nthe FS\u2019s assets and/or interests \ninfluence math learning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nWhen reflecting on the impact \nof their instruction, the \ncandidate refers to specific \nexamples from the teaching of \ntheir math lesson to determine \nwhich strategy(ies) moved \nlearning forward. \n \n \nNote: \n*Analyze: Involves carefully and systematically looking for recurring themes, common misconceptions, strengths and/or areas of growth across a collection of student \nwork in order to gain insights into student understanding. Analysis goes beyond simply describing what happened; it delves into why, what was learned/understood, \nand what the work reveals about student thinking. \nStep 3 Source of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part G: Lesson Reflection and Analysis (up to 5 pages) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 5, Element 2; TPE 6, Elements 1, 3 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 5, Element 2  \nEdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 36 \nStep 4: Apply   \nI. Application of What You Learned  \n\uf076 Template/Upload: Part H: Application of Learning (written, verbal, or ASL response)  \nDirections: Respond to the following prompts and explain what your next steps would be to \nadvance the FS\u2019s math knowledge. \nNote: If you choose to submit a video commentary response, it may be up to 5 minutes.   \nRespond naturally and authentically to explain your future math teaching plans. \nApplication Prompts: \n1. Describe what you learned throughout this cycle (e.g., what went well and what did not \ngo well or as expected). Use a specific example(s) from Steps 1, 2, or 3 to justify your \nnext instructional move for math learning. \n2. Describe the next math activity that you would teach to the FS to advance their math \nand ALD learning. Use a specific example(s) from Steps 1, 2, or 3 to justify your next \ninstructional move for math learning.  \n3. How would you continue to support your FS\u2019s learning need(s) during this next math \nactivity? Think about how you might leverage student assets and/or interests. Use a \nspecific example(s) from Steps 1, 2, or 3 to justify your next instructional move for math \nlearning. \n4. Describe how you plan to collaborate with and/or facilitate instructional support \npersonnel to support the FS in future instruction. \nStep 4 Evidence to Be Submitted  \n\uf076 Part H: Application of Learning (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or \nASL response) "}, {"number": 8, "pages": [42], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-math-8-42.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Math Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nLearning About Students with IEPs and Planning a Math Lesson Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 37 \nStep 4 Rubric \nRubric 1.8 \u2014  Step 4: Apply \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply what they have learned to determine next steps for math content and math \npractice and math ALD instruction? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe what \nthey learned throughout this \ncycle.  \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe how \nthey would advance the FS\u2019s \nmath and/or ALD learning. \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe how \nthey would continue to support \nthe FS\u2019s learning need(s). \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps does not describe how \nthey would continue to \ncollaborate with and/or \nfacilitate instructional support \npersonnel to support the FS in \nfuture instruction. \nCandidate does not use a \nspecific example(s) from Steps \n1, 2, or 3 and/or does not \njustify their next instructional \nmove for math learning, or the \nprovided example does not \njustify the next instructional \nmove for math learning. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes what \nthey learned throughout this \ncycle.  \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes how \nthey would advance the FS\u2019s \nmath and/or ALD learning. \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes how \nthey would continue to support \nthe FS\u2019s learning need(s). \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps vaguely describes how \nthey would continue to \ncollaborate with and/or \nfacilitate instructional support \npersonnel to support the FS in \nfuture instruction. \nCandidate uses an example(s) \nfrom Steps 1, 2, or 3 that \nvaguely justifies their next \ninstructional move for math \nlearning. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps clearly describes what \nthey learned throughout this \ncycle.  \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps clearly describes how \nthey would advance the FS\u2019s \nmath and ALD learning. \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps clearly describes how \nthey would continue to support \nthe FS\u2019s learning need(s). \nCandidate\u2019s application of next \nsteps clearly describes how \nthey would continue to \ncollaborate with and/or \nfacilitate instructional support \npersonnel to support the FS in \nfuture instruction. \nCandidate uses a specific \nexample(s) from Steps 1, 2, or 3 \nthat clearly justifies their next \ninstructional move for math \nlearning. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s responses \ndemonstrate how they will \ncontinue to leverage the FS\u2019s \nassets and/or interests in \nfuture lessons to support math \nlearning. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate describes how they \nwill apply what they have \nlearned about the FS to plan \nfuture instruction that clearly \nreinforces, strengthens, and/or \nextends their higher-order \nthinking related to math and \nALD. \nStep 4 Source of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part H: Application of Learning (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 6 minutes of verbal or ASL response) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 1; TPE 3, Element 2; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 5, Element 8; TPE 6, Element 1 \n \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 1; TPE 3, Element 2; TPE 4, Elements 4, 6, 7; TPE 6, Element 1 \n \n "}], "mmsn-literacy": [{"number": 1, "pages": [32, 33], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-literacy-1-32.png", "assets/rubrics/mmsn-literacy-1-33.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction for Students with IEPs Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 27 \nStep 1 Rubrics \nRubric 2.1 \u2014 Step 1: Plan  \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use their knowledge of CA State* ELA/Literacy strand(s)/standard(s) or developmentally \nappropriate Core Content Connectors (CCCs)** and ELD strand(s)/standard(s), along with information gathered from recent \nlearning/assessments and the FS\u2019s IEP, to create developmentally appropriate ELA/Literacy and ELD learning goals? How does the \ncandidate develop formative and summative assessments that target the identified learning goals? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not use recent \nlearning/assessment and/or the FS\u2019s \nIEP information to plan instruction. \nCandidate does not select a CA \nState* ELA/Literacy strand/standard \nor developmentally appropriate \nCCCs** and/or ELD \nstrands/standards for the unit plan \nOR selects CA State ELA/Literacy \nstrands/standards or \ndevelopmentally appropriate CCCs \nand/or ELD strands/standards that \nare more than one grade level above \nor below the FS they plan to teach in \nthe unit plan. \nCandidate does not include \nfoundational reading skill(s)\u2020 and/or \nan additional theme\u2021 in their \nELA/Literacy goal(s), OR the \nELA/Literacy learning goal(s) are not \ndevelopmentally appropriate for the \nunit plan. \nCandidate does not include an ELD \ngoal, OR the ELD goal(s) are not \ndevelopmentally appropriate, OR the \nELD goal(s) do not support the \nELA/Literacy learning goal(s) for the \nunit plan. \nCandidate does not plan formative \nassessments in each lesson, OR the \nformative assessments are not \nconnected to the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD learning goal(s). \nCandidate does not plan a \nsummative assessment OR plans a \nsummative assessment that does not \nmeasure the FS\u2019s progress in \nmeeting the ELA/Literacy and ELD \nlearning goals for the unit plan. \nThe included rubric or performance \ncriteria for the summative \nassessment do not measure the \nELA/Literacy and ELD learning goals \nfor the unit plan. \nCandidate\u2019s planning has 4 or more \nminor errors or a major error when \naddressing foundational reading \nskill(s),\u2020 additional theme(s),\u2021 \nand/or integrated ELD. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate vaguely uses \ninformation from recent \nlearning/assessments and/or the \nFS\u2019s IEP, OR the information used \nto plan instruction is not recent.  \nCandidate selects CA State* \nELA/Literacy strands/standards or \ndevelopmentally appropriate \nCCCs** and ELD strands/standards \nthat are either one grade level \nabove or one grade level below the \ngrade of the FS they plan to teach \nin the unit plan. \nCandidate creates ELA/Literacy \nlearning goal(s) that vaguely \ninclude foundational reading \nskill(s)\u2020 and/or an additional \ntheme\u2021 for the unit plan. \nCandidate creates ELD goal(s) that \nvaguely support the ELA/Literacy \nlearning goal(s) for the unit plan. \nCandidate plans one or more \nformative assessment(s) in each \nlesson that vaguely connect to the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD learning \ngoal(s). \nCandidate plans one summative \nassessment that vaguely measures \nthe FS\u2019s progress in meeting the \nELA/Literacy and ELD learning \ngoals for the unit plan. \nCandidate includes a rubric or \nperformance criteria for the \nsummative assessment that \nvaguely measure the ELA/Literacy \nand ELD learning goals for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate\u2019s planning includes 3 or \nfewer minor errors when \naddressing foundational reading \nskill(s),\u2020 additional theme(s),\u2021 \nand/or integrated ELD. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate clearly uses information \nfrom recent learning/assessments \nand the FS\u2019s IEP to plan instruction. \nCandidate selects grade-level \nappropriate CA State* ELA/Literacy \nstrands/standards or \ndevelopmentally appropriate \nCCCs** and ELD strands/standards \nfor the unit plan. \nCandidate creates developmentally \nappropriate ELA/Literacy learning \ngoal(s) that include both \nfoundational reading skill(s)\u2020 and \nadditional theme(s)\u2021 for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate creates developmentally \nappropriate ELD goal(s) that clearly \nsupport the ELA/Literacy learning \ngoal(s) for the unit plan. \nCandidate plans one or more \nformative assessment(s) in each \nlesson that clearly connect to the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD learning \ngoal(s). \nCandidate plans one summative \nassessment that clearly measures \nthe FS\u2019s progress in meeting the \nELA/Literacy and ELD learning \ngoals for the unit plan. \nCandidate includes a rubric or \nperformance criteria for the \nsummative assessment that clearly \nmeasure the ELA/Literacy and \nELD learning goals for the unit \nplan. \nCandidate\u2019s planning accurately \naddresses foundational reading \nskill(s),\u2020 additional theme(s),\u2021 and \nintegrated ELD. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate plans multiple formative \nassessments within each lesson of \nthe unit to monitor the FS\u2019s \nELA/Literacy and ELD \nunderstandings and learning \nneeds. \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus \nWhen developing the summative \nassessment and corresponding \nrubric or performance criteria, the \ncandidate includes real-life \nscenarios that build upon the FS\u2019s \nculture, language(s), and/or \nexperiences. \nEdSp CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction for Students with IEPs Directions and Rubrics \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 28 \nNotes: \n*You must use the California Common Core State Standards for English Language Arts & Literacy, California English Language Art s/English \nLanguage Development (ELA/ELD) Framework, and California English Language Development Standards (CA ELD Standards). Candidates in TK \nmust use the Preschool/Transitional Kindergarten Learning Foundations: Language and Literacy Development . Candidates in bilingual \nclassrooms where the language of instruction is Spanish can use the California CCSS en Espa\u00f1ol (ELA/Literacy Standard[s]) and the California \nSpanish Language Development (SLD) Standards. Candidates in bilingual placements that use Spanish as the language of instruction using the \nCCSS en Espa\u00f1ol and the Spanish Language Development Standards should substitute SLA/Literacy and SLD goals for ELA/Literacy goals \nthroughout this guide. \n**If the FS accesses an alternate curriculum that makes them eligible for the California Alternate Assessments (CAA), you may  use grade-\nlevel/developmentally appropriate Core Content Connectors instead of the CA Common Core State Standards. \n\u2020Foundational reading skills: print concepts, including letters of the alphabet; phonological awareness, including phonemic aw areness; phonics, \nspelling, and word recognition; decoding and encoding; morphology/morphological awareness; text reading fluency, including accuracy, \nprosody [expression], and rate [an indicator of automaticity]. For candidates in TK\u2013 3 settings, foundational reading skills also include creating \nliteracy environments that are print rich and that foster interest in print; and games, books, poetry, oral storytelling, and songs that draw \nattention to print, the manipulation of sounds, and alphabet letters. \n\u2021Additional themes: meaning making, language development, effective expression, and/or content knowledge  \nStep 1 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part A: Contextual Information (up to 5 pages)\n\u2022 Part B: Unit Plan (up to 24 pages)\n\u2022 Part C: Summative Assessment Description (up to 5 pages)\n\u2022 Part D: Copies of Blank Summative Assessment and Rubric or Performance Criteria\n(up to 3 pages)\nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 3, 4; TPE 2, Element 2; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5, 6; TPE 4, \nElements 1, 2, 4; TPE 5, Element 1; TPE 7, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 11 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 1, 3; TPE 2, Element 9; TPE 3, Elements 1, 2; TPE 4, \nElements 2, 4, 6, 7; TPE 5, Elements 1, 2; TPE 6, Element 1; TPE 7, Elements 1, 3, 4 "}, {"number": 2, "pages": [34], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-literacy-2-34.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction for Students with IEPs Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 29 \nRubric 2.2 \u2014  Step 1: Plan \nEssential Question: How does the candidate leverage student assets and/or interests and use UDL strategies to plan an aligned \nseries of lessons that build upon one another to create a progression of learning? How do the lessons of the unit plan (a) address the \nfoundational reading skill(s)* using a direct, systematic, and explicit approach; (b) actively engage the FS in the additional \ntheme(s);** and (c) integrate ELD for the FS? How does the candidate address collaboration with and/or facilitation of instructional \nsupport personnel to support the unit plan?   \nLevel 1 \nIn their planning, the candidate \ndoes not leverage the FS\u2019s \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate does not plan to use \nUDL strategy(ies) when \nteaching the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD content. \nThe unit plan does not include \nthree lessons or the lessons do \nnot align and/or build upon \none another to create a \nprogression of learning. \nCandidate does not plan to \nteach foundational reading \nskill(s)* and/or does not plan \nto teach foundational reading \nskill(s) using a direct, \nsystematic, and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate does not include an \nadditional theme** and/or \ndoes not plan to actively \nengage the FS while teaching \nthe additional theme(s). \nCandidate\u2019s plan does not \nintegrate ELD, and/or the ELD \ndoes not support the \nfoundational reading skill(s)* \nand/or the additional \ntheme(s).** \nCandidate does not include \nadaptation(s) that address the \nFS\u2019s assets and/or interests \nand/or learning needs. \nCandidate does not include a \nplan for collaboration with \nand/or facilitation of \ninstructional support \npersonnel. \nLevel 2 \nIn their planning, the candidate \nvaguely leverages the FS\u2019s \nassets and/or interests. \nCandidate vaguely plans to use \nUDL strategy(ies) when \nteaching the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD content. \nThe unit plan includes three \nlessons that vaguely align \nand/or build upon one another \nto create a progression of \nlearning. \nWhen planning to teach the \nfoundational reading skill(s),* \ncandidate vaguely includes a \ndirect, systematic, and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate vaguely plans to \nengage the FS with the \nadditional theme(s).** \nCandidate\u2019s plan integrates ELD \nthat vaguely supports the \nfoundational reading skill(s)* \nand/or the additional \ntheme(s).** \nCandidate includes \nadaptation(s) that vaguely \naddress the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests and/or learning \nneeds. \nCandidate includes a vague \nplan for collaboration with \nand/or facilitation of \ninstructional support \npersonnel. \n \n \n \nLevel 3 \nIn their planning, the candidate \nclearly leverages student assets \nand/or interests. \nCandidate clearly plans to use \nappropriate UDL strategy(ies) \nwhen teaching the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD content. \nThe unit plan includes three \nlessons that clearly align and \nbuild upon one another to \ncreate a progression of \nlearning. \nWhen planning to teach the \nfoundational reading skill(s),* \ncandidate clearly includes a \ndirect, systematic, and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate clearly plans to \nactively engage the FS with the \nadditional theme(s).** \nCandidate\u2019s plan integrates ELD \nthat clearly supports the \nfoundational reading skill(s)* \nand the additional theme(s).** \nCandidate includes \nadaptation(s) that clearly \naddress the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests and learning needs. \nCandidate includes a clear plan \nfor collaboration with and/or \nfacilitation of instructional \nsupport personnel. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate leverages the FS\u2019s \nassets by intentionally \nincorporating their cultural \nbackground, lived experiences, \ninterests, and/or primary \nlanguage into the lesson \ndesign. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nEach of the candidate\u2019s lessons \ninclude multiple UDL strategies \nto support the FS toward \nmeeting the ELA/Literacy and \nELD learning goal(s) based on \ntheir assets and/or interests \nand learning needs. "}, {"number": 3, "pages": [39], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-literacy-3-39.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction for Students with IEPs Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 34 \nStep 2 Rubrics \nRubric 2.3 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate actively engage the FS in learning the foundational reading skill(s)* using a direct and \nexplicit approach? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they teach \nthe foundational reading \nskill(s)* using a direct and \nexplicit approach. \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage the FS during \nthe teaching of the \nfoundational reading skill(s). \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the literacy \nlesson(s) described in Step 1. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate includes an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they teach \nthe foundational reading \nskill(s)* using a direct and \nexplicit approach. \nCandidate includes an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage the FS during \nthe teaching of the \nfoundational reading skill(s). \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the literacy \nlesson(s) described in Step 1. \n \nLevel 3 \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they teach the \nfoundational reading skill(s)* \nusing a direct and explicit \napproach. \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they actively engage the \nFS during the teaching of the \nfoundational reading skill(s). \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof the literacy lesson(s) \ndescribed in Step 1.  \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate models the targeted \nfoundational reading skill(s) \nusing precise academic \nlanguage and think-alouds to \nmake cognitive processes \nvisible. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nCandidate formatively assesses \nthe FS\u2019s foundational reading \nskill(s) throughout the lesson(s) \nand structures frequent \nopportunities for the FS to \nrespond and receive feedback.  \n \nNote: \n*Foundational reading skills: print concepts, including letters of the alphabet; phonological awareness, including phonemic aw areness; phonics, spelling, and word \nrecognition; decoding and encoding; morphology/morphological awareness; text reading fluency, including accuracy, prosody (expression), and rate (an indicator of \nautomaticity). For candidates in TK\u20133 settings, foundational reading skills also include creating literacy environments that are print rich and that foster inter est in \nprint; and games, books, poetry, oral storytelling, and songs that draw attention to print, the manipulation of sounds, and alphabet letters.  \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 4; TPE 3, Elements 1, 3; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 7, Elements 1, 2, 4, 5 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 2, Element 10; TPE 3, Element 1; TPE 4, Elements 2, 4; TPE 7, Elements 1, 4 "}, {"number": 4, "pages": [40], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-literacy-4-40.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction for Students with IEPs Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 35 \nRubric 2.4 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate leverage the FS\u2019s assets and/or interests to actively engage them in learning the \nadditional theme(s)* from the ELA/ELD Framework? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nleverage the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests while teaching the \nadditional theme(s)* from the \nELA/ELD framework. \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage the FS in \nlearning the additional \ntheme(s) from the ELA/ELD \nFramework. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nleverage the FS\u2019s assets and/or \ninterests while teaching the \nadditional theme(s)* from the \nELA/ELD framework.\n \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nactively engage the FS in \nlearning the additional \ntheme(s) from the ELA/ELD \nFramework. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they leverage the FS\u2019s \nassets and/or interests while \nteaching the additional \ntheme(s)* from the ELA/ELD \nframework. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they actively engage the \nFS in learning the additional \ntheme(s) from the ELA/ELD \nFramework.\n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate incorporates the FS\u2019s \ncultural background, lived \nexperiences, interests, and/or \nprimary language into \ninstruction of the additional \ntheme(s) from the ELA/ELD \nframework. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nCandidate provides structured \nopportunities for the FS to \ncollaboratively explore the \nadditional theme(s) through \ndiscussion, writing, and/or \nreading. \n \nNote: \n*Additional themes: meaning making, language development, effective expression, and/or content knowledge  \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 6; TPE 3, Elements 1, 3; TPE 4, Element 4; TPE 7, Elements 1, 4, 11; Candidate Choice \nof Elements 6\u20139 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 2, 3; TPE 2, Element 10; TPE 3, Element 1; TPE 4, Elements 2, 4; TPE 7, \nElement 4 "}, {"number": 5, "pages": [41], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-literacy-5-41.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction for Students with IEPs Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 36 \nRubric 2.5 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question How does the candidate use UDL strategy(ies) and integrate ELD when teaching foundational reading skill(s) \nand/or the additional theme(s)? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of UDL \nstrategy(ies). \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nintegrate ELD to support the \nteaching of the foundational \nreading skill(s) and/or the \nadditional theme(s). \n \nLevel 2 \nCandidate includes an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the UDL \nstrategy(ies) they use to teach \nthe ELA/Literacy and/or ELD \ncontent. \nCandidate provides an unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nintegrate ELD to support the \nteaching of the foundational \nreading skill(s) and/or the \nadditional theme(s). \n \nLevel 3 \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof the UDL strategy(ies) they \nuse to teach the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD content. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they integrate ELD to \nsupport the teaching of the \nfoundational reading skill(s) \nand/or the additional theme(s). \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus:  \nCandidate allows the FS to \ndemonstrate mastery of \nfoundational reading skills \nand/or the additional theme(s) \norally, in writing, visually, or \nthrough performance.  \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof multiple UDL strategies used \nto support the teaching of the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning goals. \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Elements 2, 8; TPE 3, Element 3; TPE 4, Elements 4, 7; TPE 5, Elements 1, 2, 3, 5; TPE 7, \nElements 2, 4, 10 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 4; TPE 2, Element 4; TPE 3, Element 1; TPE 4, Elements 2, 4; TPE 5,  \nElements 1, 2; TPE 7, Element 1 "}, {"number": 6, "pages": [42], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-literacy-6-42.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction for Students with IEPs Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 37 \nRubric 2.6 \u2014  Step 2: Teach and Assess \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use formative assessment(s) to monitor the FS\u2019s learning and respond intentionally to \nsupport the FS\u2019s progress toward meeting the ELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not provide a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use \nformative assessment(s) to \nmonitor the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals. \nCandidate does not include a \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nrespond intentionally to \nformative assessment results to \nsupport the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate provides a vague \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they use \nformative assessment(s) to \nmonitor the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals. \nCandidate includes a vague \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of how they \nrespond intentionally to \nformative assessment results to \nsupport the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD goals. \nLevel 3 \nCandidate provides a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they use formative \nassessment(s) to monitor the \nFS\u2019s progress toward meeting \nthe ELA/Literacy and/or ELD \ngoals. \nCandidate includes a clear \ndemonstration and description \nof how they respond \nintentionally to formative \nassessment results to support \nthe FS\u2019s progress toward \nmeeting the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD goals. \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate uses multiple \nformative assessments, such as \npurposeful questioning, \nacademic discussion, analysis of \nstudent work, and/or real-time \nchecks for understanding, to \nmonitor the FS\u2019s learning in \nrelation to both ELA/Literacy \nand ELD goals. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 and 4, plus: \nCandidate analyzes student \nresponses during instruction to \nidentify patterns and any \nmisconceptions. Based on this \nevidence, the candidate \nimmediately adapts instruction \nin meaningful ways, including \ndifferentiating tasks, providing \ntargeted scaffolds, and/or \nextending learning for the FS. \nStep 2 Sources of Evidence: \n\u2022 Part E: Video Clip(s) (1 to 4 video clips, totaling up to 20 minutes) \n\u2022 Part F: Commentary (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) \nTPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 8; TPE 5, Elements 1, 2, 5; TPE 7, Element 10 \nMMSN TPEs and Elements: TPE 1, Element 4; TPE 5, Elements 1, 2; TPE 7, Element 1 "}, {"number": 7, "pages": [47], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-literacy-7-47.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction for Students with IEPs Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 42 \nStep 3 Rubric \nRubric 2.7 \u2014  Step 3: Reflect \nEssential Question: How does the candidate use their analysis of the summative assessment results to (a) identify the FS\u2019s \nunderstandings and/or misconceptions; (b) provide specific, actionable feedback to the FS; and (c) provide feedback to the \nfamily/guardian(s) of the FS to support caregivers in understanding the assessment results and how they can support the FS\u2019s \nlearning beyond the classroom? How does the candidate use their analysis of the summative assessment results and the information \nthey gathered during their teaching to determine instructional changes they would make if they taught the unit again? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate\u2019s reflection does not \ninclude an analysis of the FS\u2019s \nsummative assessment results. \nCandidate does not identify \nunderstandings and/or \nmisconceptions related to the \nFS\u2019s progress toward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and ELD goals, OR \nthe candidate does not base \ntheir analysis on the information \nthey gathered while teaching \nand/or the summative \nassessment results. \nCandidate does not provide \nspecific, actionable feedback for \nthe FS, OR the specific, \nactionable feedback is not based \non the summative assessment \nresults. \nCandidate does not provide a \ndescription of the impact of their \ninstructional approach(es) or \ntheir choice of student \nactivity(ies) during the unit for\n \nthe FS. \nCandidate does not provide an \nexplanation of what instructional \nchanges they would make for the \nFS if they taught the unit again. \nCandidate does not provide \nfeedback to the \nfamily/guardian(s) of the FS to \nsupport caregivers in \nunderstanding the assessment \nresults and\n/or how they can \nsupport the FS\u2019s learning beyond \nthe classroom.  \nLevel 2 \nCandidate\u2019s reflection includes a \nvague analysis of the FS\u2019s \nsummative assessment results. \nBased on their analysis of the \nsummative assessment results \nand the information gathered \nduring their teaching, the \ncandidate vaguely identifies \nunderstandings and/or \nmisconceptions related to the \nFS\u2019s progress toward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and ELD goals. \nCandidate provides feedback to \nthe FS that is vague\n, not \nactionable, or not clearly based \non the FS\u2019s performance on the \nsummative assessment, OR the \ncandidate provides \ndevelopmentally inappropriate \nfeedback. \nCandidate provides a vague \ndescription of the impact of their \ninstructional approach(es) \nand/or their choice of student \nactivity(ies) during the unit for \nthe FS. \nCandidate provides \na vague \nexplanation of what instructional \nchanges they would make for the \nFS if they taught the unit again. \nCandidate provides vague \nfeedback to the \nfamily/guardian(s) of the FS to \nsupport caregivers in \nunderstanding the assessment \nresults and\n/or how they can \nsupport the FS\u2019s learning beyond \nthe classroom.  \nLevel 3 \nCandidate\u2019s reflection includes a \nclear analysis of the FS\u2019s \nsummative assessment results. \nBased on their analysis of the \nsummative assessment results \nand the information gathered \nduring their teaching, the \ncandidate clearly identifies \nunderstandings and/or \nmisconceptions related to the  \nFS\u2019s progress toward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and ELD goals. \nCandidate provides specific, \nactionable feedback for the FS \nthat is clearly based on the FS\u2019s \nperformance on the summative \nassessment. \nCandidate provides a clear \ndescription of the impact of their \ninstructional approach(es) and \ntheir choice of student \nactivity(ies) during the unit for \nthe FS. \nCandidate provides a clear \nexplanation of what instructional \nchanges they would make for the \nFS if they taught the unit again. \nCandidate provides clear \nfeedback to the \nfamily/guardian(s) of the FS to \nsupport caregivers in \nunderstanding the assessment \nresults and how they can support \nthe FS\u2019s learning beyond the \nclassroom.  \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate\u2019s specific, actionable \nfeedback for the FS is aligned to \nthe learning goals of the unit. It \nclearly names what the FS did \nwell and/or what needs \nimprovement based on specific \nstrengths and/or needs of the FS \nusing concrete examples from \nthe summative assessment \nand/or observations. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus: \nWhen reflecting on the impact of \ntheir instruction, the candidate \nuses data from the unit plan, \nspecific examples from their \nteaching or commentary, and \ndata from formative and/or \nsummative assessment(s) to \ndetermine which strategies \nmoved learning forward. "}, {"number": 8, "pages": [51], "images": ["assets/rubrics/mmsn-literacy-8-51.png"], "text": "EdSp CalTPA: Literacy Cycle Performance Assessment Guide Mild to Moderate Support Needs \nAssessment-Driven Literacy Instruction for Students with IEPs Directions and Rubrics \n \nCopyright \u00a9 2026 by the California Commission on Teacher Credentialing \nMay Lee State Office Complex, 651 Bannon Street, Suite 600, Sacramento, CA 95811. All rights reserved. 46 \nStep 4 Rubric \nRubric 2.8 \u2014  Step 4: Apply \nEssential Question: How does the candidate apply the analysis of the FS\u2019s performance during the unit and summative assessment \nresults to plan, provide an explanation for, and teach a follow-up activity (referring to specific examples from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3)? \nLevel 1 \nCandidate does not include a \nplan for follow-up instruction \nfor the FS, either re-teaching or \nextension, based on their \nanalysis of the FS\u2019s \nperformance during the unit, \nincluding summative \nassessment results.  \nThe video and commentary do \nnot include either: \n\u2022 Re-Teach: A demonstration \nand/or description of the \ntargeted intervention \nand/or individualized \nstrategy used to support the \nFS\u2019s progress toward \nmeeting the ELA/Literacy \nand/or ELD learning goal(s) \nOR \n\u2022 Extend: A demonstration \nand/or description of the \ninstruction that deepens \nand/or advances the FS\u2019s \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning  \nCandidate does not include \nspecific example(s) from Steps \n1, 2, and/or 3 to support their \nfollow-up instructional decision \nmaking, OR evidence cited does \nnot support follow-up \ninstructional decisions. \nLevel 2 \nCandidate includes a vague \nplan for follow-up instruction \nfor the FS, either re-teaching or \nextension, based on their \nanalysis of the FS\u2019s \nperformance during the unit, \nincluding summative \nassessment results.  \nThe video and/or commentary \ninclude either: \n\u2022 Re-Teach: An unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the targeted \nintervention and/or \nindividualized strategy used \nto support the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning goal(s) \nOR \n\u2022 Extend: An unclear \ndemonstration and/or \ndescription of the instruction \nthat deepens and/or \nadvances the FS\u2019s \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning  \nCandidate uses vague \nexample(s) from Steps 1, 2, \nand/or 3 to support their \nfollow-up instructional decision \nmaking.  \nLevel 3 \nCandidate includes a clear plan \nfor follow-up instruction for the \nFS, either re-teaching or \nextension, based on their \nanalysis of the FS\u2019s \nperformance during the unit, \nincluding summative \nassessment results.  \nThe video and commentary \ninclude either: \n\u2022 Re-Teach: A clear \ndemonstration and \ndescription of the targeted \nintervention and/or \nindividualized strategy used \nto support the FS\u2019s progress \ntoward meeting the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning goal(s) \nOR \n\u2022 Extend: A clear \ndemonstration and \ndescription of the \ninstruction that deepens \nand/or advances the FS\u2019s \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \nlearning  \nCandidate clearly uses specific \nexample(s) from Steps 1, 2, \nand/or 3 to support their \nfollow-up instructional decision \nmaking. \n \nLevel 4 \nAll of Level 3, plus: \nCandidate leverages the FS\u2019s \nassets by intentionally \nincorporating the FS\u2019s cultural, \nlinguistic, and/or lived \nexperiences into the follow-up \ninstruction design. \n \nLevel 5 \nAll of Levels 3 & 4, plus:  \nCandidate synthesizes multiple \nsources of evidence from Steps \n1, 2, and 3 to justify their \ninstructional decisions and \nclearly explains how the \nselected strategy accelerates \nthe FS\u2019s learning of the \nELA/Literacy and/or ELD \ncontent. "}]};
